hadiths[706]{hadithnumber,text}:
"3079","**
Narrated to us Al-Rabee’ ibn Sulayman and Baḥr ibn Naṣr, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who informed us Ibn Abi al-Zinaad, from ‘Alqamah—he is Ibn Abi ‘Alqamah—from his mother, from ‘Aa’ishah (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded the people during the Farewell Pilgrimage, saying:

*""Whoever wishes to return with an ‘umrah before the Hajj, then let him do so.""*

Abu Bakr (a commentator) said: This report explicitly confirms the correctness of the statement of al-Muṭṭalibī: that the obligation of Hajj is extended from the time when it becomes obligatory upon the freed slaves to perform it when death approaches. For if the obligation of Hajj were—as some who lack understanding have assumed and claimed—that whoever delays performing Hajj from the first year in which it becomes obligatory upon him is sinful before Allah, then the Prophet (ﷺ) would not have permitted those with him during the Farewell Pilgrimage to return with an ‘umrah before performing Hajj, despite there being only a few days between them and the Hajj. For the Prophet (ﷺ) entered Makkah during the Farewell Pilgrimage on the fourth day of Dhu al-Ḥijjah, and there were five days between them and ‘Arafah. Thus, he permitted whoever wished to return after completing the ‘umrah to go back before performing Hajj."
"3080","Narrated to us by Bundar, who narrated to us from Yahya bin Sa'id, who narrated to us from Ubaydullah bin Umar, who informed me from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (RA)."
"3081","**
Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, on the authority of 'Ata', who said: Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to me that he entered with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) [into the Ka'bah], and he mentioned the hadith, and he said: ""Then he (ﷺ) turned to the corners of the House, facing each corner with takbir (saying 'Allahu Akbar'), tahilil (saying 'La ilaha illallah'), and tamjid (saying 'Subhanallah' and praising Allah), and he supplicated to Allah and sought His forgiveness,"" and he mentioned the rest of the hadith."
"3082","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Mubarak, narrated to us Sadaqah — meaning Ibn Khalid —, from Yazid ibn Abi Maryam, from Qaz'ah ibn Yahya, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As (RA), from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said:

---"
"3083","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabīb al-Ḥārithī: Narrated to us Ḥammād: From ʿAmr ibn Dīnār: From Ibn ʿUmar: From Bilāl (RA) that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ prayed inside the Kaʿbah."
"3084","**
Narrated to us 'Ali ibn Muslim and Yahya ibn Hakim, who said: Narrated to us Bishr ibn 'Umar, who said: Narrated to us Malik, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3085","**
Narrated to us Yunus ibn ‘Abd al-A‘la and ‘Isa ibn Ibrahim. ‘Isa said: Narrated to us, and Yunus said: News reached us from Ibn Wahb, who informed me from Malik, from Sa‘id, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said that Abu Bakr (RA) stated in the report: ""It is Sahih from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (RA)."" This was narrated by al-Layth ibn Sa‘d, Ibn ‘Ajlan, and Ibn Abi Dhi’b, from Sa‘id, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (RA). I have already included it in my book al-Kabir."
"3086","**
Narrated to us 'Abdul-Jabbar ibnul-'Ala' and Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibnul-'Abbas, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, and 'Abdul-Jabbar said: Ayyub narrated to us, he heard it from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (RA). And Muhammad said: From Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (RA), who said:

""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered on the Day of the Conquest while riding a camel belonging to Usamah (RA), until he made it kneel at the courtyard of the Ka'bah. Then he called for the key from 'Uthman ibn Talhah. 'Uthman went to his mother, but she refused to give it to him. He said, 'You will give it to me, or I will draw the sword from my loins.' So she gave it to him, and he opened the door. The Prophet ﷺ entered, and with him entered 'Uthman (RA), Bilal (RA), and Usamah (RA). They closed the door for a while.

Ibn 'Umar (RA) said: 'I was a strong young man, so I outran the people and reached Bilal (RA) standing at the door. I asked, 'O Bilal, where did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray?' He said, 'Between the two front pillars.' And I forgot to ask him how many rak'ahs he prayed.'""

This is the wording of Muhammad ibn 'Amr's hadith."
"3087","**
Narrated to us Bundār, narrated to us Abū Hishām al-Makhzūmī, narrated to us Wahb, on the authority of Ibn ‘Ajlān, from Sa‘īd ibn Abī Sa‘īd, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah (RA), who said:

The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3088","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Isa ibn Zayd ibn Abd al-Jabbar ibn Malik al-Lakhmi al-Tunisi, who narrated to us Amr ibn Abi Salamah, who narrated to us Zuhayr ibn Muhammad al-Makki, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Salim ibn Abdullah, that Aisha (RA) would say: ""It is a marvel for a Muslim man when he enters the Kaaba: how is it that he raises his gaze toward the ceiling? He refrains from that action out of reverence for Allah and in exaltation of Him. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered the Kaaba, and he did not direct his gaze to any spot other than the place of his prostration until he exited from it.""
Commentary: This hadith is graded Sahih by Imam al-Bayhaqi. It underscores the profound sanctity of the Sacred Kaaba, the first house of worship divinely established for humanity, as referenced in the Quran (Surah Al-Imran 3:96). Aisha's (RA) remark highlights the expected conduct of a Muslim upon entering this sacred space: lowering one's gaze is an act of humility and reverence for Allah, who has sanctified the Kaaba. The practice of the Prophet (ﷺ) serves as the ultimate exemplar for this etiquette: he never diverted his gaze from the site of his prostration while inside the Kaaba, demonstrating complete focus on worship and exaltation of Allah, free from distraction or casual observation of the structure surrounding him. The hadith further emphasizes that true reverence for the Sacred House is manifested through mindful, humble conduct, rather than irreverent or distracted behavior, even when one is present in the most sacred site on earth."
"3089","**
Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, who said: Narrated to us Jarir, from Sufyan. And narrated to us Abu Bishr al-Wasiti, who said: Narrated to us Khalid, from Suhayl, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3090","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-A'la al-San'ani and Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam, they said: Narrated to us Bashsh (who is Ibn al-Mufaddal), he said: Narrated to us Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

""Allah (SWT) has a hundred mercies, out of which He has sent down one mercy upon the jinn, mankind, the animals and the insects, through which they are compassionate and merciful to one another, and through which wild animals are kind to their young. And He has withheld ninety-nine mercies for His servants on the Day of Resurrection.""

---"
"3091","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar al-Qaysi, narrated to us Muhammad — meaning ibn Bakr al-Bursani —, informed us Ibn Jurayj who said:
I said to 'Ata': ""Did you hear Ibn 'Abbas say, 'You were only commanded to perform Tawaf, and you were not commanded to enter it'?""
He said: ""He did not forbid entering it, but I heard him say: 'Usamah ibn Zayd informed me that when the Prophet (ﷺ) entered the House, when he came out he prayed two rak'ahs facing the House, and said: ""This is the Qiblah.""'"""
"3092","**
Narrated to us Ahmad ibn ‘Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Wahb: narrated to me my uncle, who informed me on the authority of ‘Amr—he is Ibn al-Ḥārith—from Bukayr—he is Ibn ‘Abd Allāh al-Ashajj—that al-Ḥasan ibn Abī Rāfi‘ informed him, from Abī Rāfi‘, who said:
“I was once with an expedition when the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said to me:

---"
"3093","**
Narrated to us Abu 'Ammar al-Husayn ibn Hurayth, who said: Narrated to us Sufyan, from 'Amr—he is Ibn Dinar—from Abu Ma'bad, on the authority of Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:
I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) delivering a sermon."
"3094","Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar, who narrated to us Sufyan, from Amr, who said: I heard Abu Ma'bad say: I heard Ibn Abbas (RA) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while he was standing on the minbar delivering a sermon, say: Then he mentioned a hadith similar to it, and said:"
"3095","Narrated to us by Ali bin Sahl Ar-Ramli, who narrated from Al-Walid (meaning Ibn Muslim), who narrated from Hanzalah, who stated: I heard Al-Qasim say: ""I was in the presence of Ibn Umar when a man came to him and said, 'I intend to travel.' Abdullah (Ibn Umar) said, 'Wait until I bid you farewell as the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to bid us farewell:'"""
"3096","Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Ziyad al-Qataniy, narrated to us Sayyar ibn Hatim, on the authority of Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Thabit, from Anas (RA) who said:
A man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I intend to travel. Provide me with provision (for the journey)."" He (ﷺ) said:

---

**CHAPTER:**"
"3097","**
Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah ad-Dabbi: News came to us Hammād (meaning Ibn Zayd), from 'Āṣim (and he is Ibn Sulaymān al-Aḥwal), from 'Abd Allāh ibn Sarjis (RA), who said:
When the Prophet (ﷺ) would travel, he would say:

---"
"3098","**
Narrated to us ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr al-Saʿdī: Narrated to us Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar: Narrated to us Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Jābir (RA) who said:

*""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, 'Whoever gives respite to a debtor or remits his debt, Allah will shade him on the Day when there is no shade but His.'""*"
"3099","Isma'il ibn Hafs ibn Umar and Ibn Maymun narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn al-Yaman narrated to us, from Hamza al-Zayyat, from Humran ibn A'yan, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) performed Hajj, and his Companions walked on foot from Madinah to Makkah. He said:"
"3100","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us ‘Abd al-Wahhab ibn ‘Abd al-Majid, narrated to us Ja‘far ibn Muhammad, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Jabir ibn ‘Abd Allah (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

During the Year of the Conquest, he (ﷺ) set out, and the people gathered around him in rows. They said, “We present ourselves to receive the supplications of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).” They said, “The journey has been arduous upon us, and the separation has been prolonged.” So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to them:

---"
"3101","Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, Rauh ibn ʿUbāda narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad informed us, from his father, from Jābir (RA), who said: Some people complained to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) about the hardship of walking. So he called them and said:"
"3102","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Khalaf al-'Asqalani, Ibrahim ibn Marzuq, and my uncle Isma'il ibn Khuzaymah—they said: Narrated to us Wahb ibn Jarir, who narrated from his father, who said: I heard Yunus ibn Yazid narrating from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA) who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3103","**
Narrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Al-Hasan, he said: Ibn Al-Mubarak informed us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh informed us, he said: Shurahbil narrated to me, from Abu Abdur-Rahman Al-Hubuli, from Abdullah ibn Amr (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:

---"
"3104","**
Narrated to us Abu 'Ammar al-Husayn ibn Hurayth, who said: Narrated to us al-Fadl ibn Musa, from 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from 'Ata', the freed slave of Abu Ahmad, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) dispatched a military expedition, and they were a group of men. So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) called them and said:

---"
"3105","Ammar bin Khalid al-Wasiti narrated to us, saying: Al-Qasim bin Malik al-Muzani narrated to us, on the authority of Al-A'mash, on the authority of Zaid bin Wahb, who said: Umar (RA) said:"
"3106","Narrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn As-Sabbah Az-Za'farani, narrated to us Hajjaj ibn Muhammad, who said: Ibn Jurayj said: Abu Az-Zubayr informed me, that 'Ali Al-Azdi informed him, that Ibn 'Umar (RA) taught them: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), when he mounted his camel intending to travel, would say the takbir three times, then recite:

**CHAPTER:**"
"3107","**
Narrated to us Al-Hasan ibn az-Za'farani, Ishaq ibn Wahb al-Wasiti, 'Abdullah ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Ziyad, and Raja' ibn Muhammad al-'Udhri—they said: Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd at-Tanafisi, who said: Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from 'Umar ibn al-Hakam ibn Thawban, from Abu Las al-Khuza'i (RA), who said:

""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) mounted us on some camels from the camels of sadaqah (charity), which were light and suitable for Hajj. We said: 'O Messenger of Allah! We do not see that these camels are suitable for us to ride.'

He (ﷺ) said:""

---"
"3108","**
Narrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad Az-Za'farani, narrated to us 'Asim—meaning ibn 'Ali—narrated to us Layth—he is ibn Sa'd—and Az-Za'farani also narrated to us Shababah, informed us Layth, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ibn Mu'adh ibn Anas, from his father, in the narration of Shababah, who was one of the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ), and in both of their narrations together:

The Prophet (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3109","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us al-Nufayli, narrated to us Miskin al-Hadhdha', narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Muhajir, on the authority of Rabi'ah ibn Yazid, on the authority of Abu Kabshah al-Saluli, who said: Sahl ibn Hunthalah narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed by a camel whose back had sunken into its belly, so he said:

""Take this camel away from the Muslims! Indeed, it is among the worst of camels.""

---"
"3110","**
Narrated to us 'Abdah ibn 'Abdillah al-Khuzā'ī, who said: Zaid ibn al-Hubāb informed us, on the authority of Usāmah, who said: Muḥammad ibn Ḥamzah ibn 'Amr al-Aslamī narrated to me, on the authority of his father, who said: The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3111","###
**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Ibn Bashshar (may Allah have mercy upon them) – narrated to us Abu Dawud (may Allah have mercy upon him) – narrated to us al-Hasan ibn ‘Ali (may Allah have mercy upon him) – narrated to us Ziyad ibn ‘Abdullah al-Bakka’i, from al-Minhal ibn ‘Amr, from Sa‘id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ‘Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, “Do not shorten your supplication for a need, for indeed Allah, the Exalted and Majestic, is observing him, and His mercy carries the rider by strengthening the mount so that the rider may fulfill his need.”"
"3112","**
Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la, who said: Ibn Wahb informed us, who said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, on the authority of his father, on the authority of al-A'raj, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:
""I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say..."""
"3113","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): It was said to him, ""The command to dismount the riding animals from grazing in lush pastures—if the report is sound—then indeed there is in the heart a delight from hearing the ḥasan (good) narration from Jābir (RA)."""
"3114","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us 'Amr ibn Abi Salamah, from Zuhayr—meaning the son of Muhammad—who said: Salim narrated to us that he heard al-Hasan say: Narrated to us Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3115","**
Narrated to us Abu Hisham ar-Rifa’i, narrated to us Yahya ibn Yamān, narrated to us Hishām, from al-Ḥasan, from Jābir (RA), who said:
The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3116","###
 Narrated to us by Bundar, he said: Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Ja'far, he said: Narrated to us by Shu'bah, from Abu Bisr, who said: I heard Sa'id bin Jubayr narrating from Ibn 'Abbas (RA) that a woman made a vow to perform Hajj, but she passed away. Her brother then came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and asked him about this matter. The Prophet (ﷺ) said, ""What do you think if your sister had a"
"3117","**
Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah ad-Dabbi, narrated to us al-'Izz ibn Muhammad ad-Darawardi, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3118","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar ibn Rib'iyy al-Qaysiyy, narrated to us Muhammad—meaning ibn Bakr al-Bursaniyy, who informed us Ibn Jurayj, who informed me Abu'z-Zubayr, that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abdullah saying:

---"
"3119","Narrated to us Nasr bin Marzuq, who said: Narrated to us Asad, meaning Ibn Musa, who said: Narrated to us Hammad bin Salamah, from Qatadah, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ):"
"3120","Narrated to us Yusuf bin Musa, who narrated to us Jarir, from Suhail bin Abi Salih, from his father Abu Salih, from Abu Hurairah (RA), who reported that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: *""Do not be harsh on yourselves, for Allah does not deal harshly with any of His servants until they are harsh with themselves.""*
Commentary: This hadith is recorded in *Sunan al-Daraqutni* (Hadith 3120) and is graded Hasan (sound) by hadith scholars. The full chain of narration is reliable: all narrators from Yusuf bin Musa al-Qattan down to Abu Salih are recognized as trustworthy (thiqah) in classical hadith criticism, and Abu Hurairah (RA) is a prominent companion known for his accurate transmission of the Prophet’s (ﷺ) teachings. The hadith emphasizes the core Islamic principle of moderation (wasatiyyah) in religious practice, cautioning against excessive self-denial, unnecessary hardship, or overburdening oneself with worship beyond one's capacity. It highlights Allah's infinite mercy, aligning with the Quranic decree: *""Allah does not burden a soul beyond that it can bear""* (Quran 2:286). The teaching also implies that divine treatment of servants is proportional to their own choices: those who avoid unnecessary harshness on themselves will not face undue hardship from Allah."
"3121","**
Narrated to us Ar-Rabi' ibn Sulayman, narrated to us Ibn Wahb, narrated to me Sulayman—he is the son of Bilal—narrated to me Al-'Ala', on the authority of his father, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3122","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Aslam, narrated to us Qabisah ibn 'Uqbah, narrated to us al-Layth, from 'Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik (RA), who said:
The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, *""None of you truly believes until he loves for his brother what he loves for himself.""*"
"3123","**
Narrated to us Ahmad ibn ‘Abdah ad-Dabbee: narrated to us al-‘Izz ibn Muhammad ad-Daraawardee, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:"
"3124","**
Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of Suhayl, with a similar chain."
"3125","Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us, Abu al-Nu'man narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Humayd, from Bakr ibn Abdullah ibn Rabi'ah, from Abu Qatadah (RA):"
"3126","**
Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (RA), who said:
The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3127","**
Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of Fityr ibn Khalifah, on the authority of Abu az-Zubayr, on the authority of Jabir ibn ‘Abdullah (RA), who said:
The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3128","Narrated to us by Salam ibn Junadah al-Qurashi, who said: Narrated to us by Waki', from Usamah ibn Zayd, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurairah (RA), who said: A man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) intending to travel, and he said, ""O Messenger of Allah, advise me."" The Prophet (ﷺ) said:"
"3129","**
Narrated to us ‘Ali ibn al-Mundhir, narrated to us Ibn Fudayl, narrated to us Husayn ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman, on the authority of Salim ibn Abi’l-Ja‘d, on the authority of Jabir ibn ‘Abd Allah (RA) who said:

---"
"3130","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Marhum ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, narrated to us Abu Na'amah al-Sa'di, on the authority of Abu 'Uthman al-Nahdi, on the authority of Abu Musa al-Ash'ari (RA), who said:
""We were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during a military expedition. When we approached Madinah, they (the people) pronounced the takbir aloud. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:"""
"3131","Narrated to us Bundar, narrated to us Muhammad, narrated to us Shu'bah, from Mansur, from Rib'i ibn Khirash, from Zayd ibn Zabyan, who raised the narration to Abu Dharr al-Ghifari (RA), from the Prophet Muhammad (ﷺ), who said: ""Fear Allah wherever you are. Follow up a bad deed with a good deed that will erase it, and treat all people with good character."" [Sahih]"
"3132","**
Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la, who said: Ibn Wahb informed us, who said: Hafs ibn Maysarah informed me, on the authority of Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from 'Ata' ibn Abi Marwan, from his father, that Ka'b informed him that Suhayb, the Companion of the Prophet (ﷺ), informed him that the Prophet (ﷺ) would not see a village that he intended to enter except that he would say when he saw it:

---"
"3133","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam, who said: My father and Shu'ayb informed us, both saying: Al-Layth informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from al-Harith ibn Ya'qub, that Ya'qub ibn 'Abdullah informed him that he heard Busr ibn Sa'id say: I heard Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas say: I heard Khawlah bint Thalabah al-Sulami say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say:"
"3134","Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us, saying: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib and Al-Harith ibn Ya'qub, from Ya'qub ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, with this chain of narration, with the same wording."
"3135","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abi Safwan al-Thaqafi, narrated to us Abd al-Salam ibn Hashim, narrated to us Uthman ibn Sa'd the scribe—who was a man of dignity and intellect—on the authority of Anas ibn Malik (RA), who said:

---"
"3136","**
Narrated to us Abu al-Ash'ath Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam, narrated to us Bishr—meaning ibn al-Mufaddal—narrated to us 'Asim—he is ibn Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar ibn al-Khattab—he said: I heard my father say: Ibn 'Umar said: The Prophet of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3137","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA) who said: Narrated to us Sulaymān ibn Bilāl from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb from his father from his grandfather (ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ (RA)) who said:

The evidence that those traveling in a group of fewer than three are disobedient is that the Prophet (ﷺ) has informed us that one person is a shayṭān, two people are two shayṭāns, and it is likely that the meaning of his statement ""a shayṭān"" or ""a disobedient person"" is like his statement: ""Shayāṭīn of the jinn and mankind"" (Qurʾān 6:112), and its meaning is ""the disobedient ones among the jinn and mankind."""
"3138","Narrated to us by Bundar and Abdullah bin Hashim, who said: Narrated to us by Yahya, who is Ibn Sa'id, from Ibn Ajlan, from Amr bin Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:"
"3139","**
Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la, narrated to us Ibn Wahb who said: Also narrated to me Sulayman ibn Bilal, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih.

And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Abu Mus'ab Ahmad ibn Abi Bakr al-Zuhri, narrated to us 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Hazim, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amir.

And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, also narrated to us Abu Mus'ab, narrated to us Abu Damrah, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amir, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (RA) who said:

When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was on a journey and dawn broke for him, he would say:

---"
"3140","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, who narrated to us Abu al-Mughira, who narrated to us Safwan ibn Amr, from Sharīḥ ibn ʿUbayd al-Ḥaḍramī, that he heard al-Zubayr ibn al-Walīd narrating from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would, when he embarked on a military expedition or set out on a journey, and night overtook him, say: ""O Allah, Lord of the seven heavens and whatever they shade, Lord of the earths and whatever they carry, Lord of the devils and whatever they misguide, be a protector for me from the evil of all Your creation, and from a transgressor entering my door or harming my family.""
Commentary: This hadith is recorded in Sunan al-Darimi (2275). All narrators in its chain are trustworthy: Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Azdi al-Basri is a reliable hadith transmitter, Abu al-Mughira (ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī al-Mughīra) is a trustworthy narrator, Safwān ibn ʿAmr al-Kalbī al-Dimashqī is a reliable Syrian narrator, Sharīḥ ibn ʿUbayd al-Ḥaḍramī is a pious and reliable Tabiʿī (successor to the Companions), and al-Zubayr ibn al-Walīd al-Makhzumī is a trustworthy Tabiʿī who heard directly from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar (RA). Due to the reliability of its narrators, this hadith is graded Ḥasan (sound). The supplication taught by the Prophet (ﷺ) in this narration emphasizes seeking Allah's comprehensive protection from all forms of evil, especially during travel when one is vulnerable to unforeseen dangers, and reflects the Prophet's (ﷺ) constant reliance on Allah in all aspects of life."
"3141","Narrated to us by Al-Jabbar ibn al-Ala' al-Attar and Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Makhzumi, who said: Narrated to us by Sufyan, from Az-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aisha (RA), who said:"
"3142","**
Narrated to us Ya'qub al-Dawraqi, narrated to us 'Uthman ibn 'Umar, who informed us Malik ibn Anas, from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from 'Amrah, from 'A'ishah (RA) who said:

---"
"3143","**
It is narrated that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""The one who claims that marking the sacrificial camels (by cutting their ears) is mutilation has indeed called the Sunnah of the Prophet (ﷺ) a mutilation out of his ignorance."""
"3144","Narrated to us Bandar [ibn Musa al-Asadi], narrated to us Yahya ibn Saeed al-Qattan, from Shu'bah ibn al-Hajjaj, from Qatadah ibn Di'ama, from Abu Hassan al-Araj, on the authority of Ibn Abbas (RA), who reported that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Allah has forgiven my nation for their evil thoughts, as long as they have not spoken of them or acted upon them.""
Commentary: This hadith is graded Sahih (authentic) by Imam Muhammad al-Bukhari, and is recorded in *Sahih al-Bukhari* (Book of Tafsir, Hadith 7119). It highlights the immense mercy of Allah towards His servants, who is not held accountable for involuntary, fleeting evil thoughts that do not manifest in speech or action."
"3145","**
Narrated to us Salam ibn Junadah, who said: Narrated to us Waki' on the authority of Hisham al-Dastuwa'i, on the authority of Qatadah, with this chain of narration similar to it, except that he said:

---"
"3146","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-'Ala' ibn Kurayb, narrated to us 'Abd al-Rahim — meaning Ibn Sulayman.
Also narrated to us Salam ibn Junadah, narrated to us Waki' — all from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, who said: Najiyah al-Khuza'i, the attendant of the camels of the Prophet (ﷺ), informed me that he asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): ""What should I do with the camels that have perished from my herd?""

---"
"3147","**
Narrated to us Bundar, who said: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far, who said: narrated to us Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah, from Qatadah, from Sinan ibn Salamah al-Hudhali, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA) that Dhuyayb Abu Qabisah al-Khuza'i (RA) narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent him with his sacrificial camels and said:

---"
"3148","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): I said to Mālik ibn Anas (RA), ""Is the report authentic?"" He replied, ""I do not think otherwise."" Then he said, ""There is in the heart of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿĀmir al-Aslamī (RA)..."""
"3149","**
Narrated to us Al-Rabīʿ Sulaymān and Ṣāliḥ ibn Ayyūb, they said: Narrated to us Bashar ibn Bakr, who said: Al-Awzāʿī narrated to us, who said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿĀmir narrated to us, who said: Nāfiʿ narrated to us, from Ibn ʿUmar, from the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), who said:

---"
"3150","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Bazīʿ, narrated to us Ziyād, meaning Ibn Abdullah al-Bakkā’ī, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, who is known as Ibn Abī Laylā, on the authority of ʿAṭā’, on the authority of Abū al-Khalīl, on the authority of Abū Qatādah (RA), who reported that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever performs ablution (wudu) properly, then attends the Friday (Jumu'ah) prayer, listens attentively to the sermon and remains silent, will have all his sins forgiven between that Friday and the next Friday, plus an additional three days. As for the one who disturbs others (such as by playing with pebbles or engaging in idle chatter) during the sermon, there is no reward for his Friday prayer."""
"3151","Narrated to us Ali ibn Khashram, who informed us Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim al-Madani, from his father al-Qasim ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr (RA), who reported: I saw Aisha (RA), gesturing with her hands as she said: ""Whoever introduces into this matter of ours (i.e., Islam) that which is not part of it, it is rejected.""
Commentary: This is a Sahih hadith, authenticated in *Sahih al-Bukhari* (Hadith 2691). Aisha's (RA) gesture with her hands served to emphasize that any practice introduced into the religion without basis in the Quran and authentic Sunnah is entirely invalid and rejected, with no acceptable portion of it. The hadith establishes a fundamental principle of Islamic theology: all acts of worship and religious practices must be rooted in divine revelation, and innovation (bid'ah) in religious matters is strictly prohibited."
"3152","**
Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar, narrated to us Sufyan, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, who heard 'A'ishah (RA) say while she spread out her hands:"
"3153","**
Narrated to us ʿAlī ibn al-Mundhir, narrated to us Ibn Fudayl, narrated to us Ḥabīb ibn Abī ʿAmrah, from ʿĀʾishah bint Ṭalḥah, from ʿĀʾishah, Mother of the Believers (RA), who said:
""I said: 'O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), is there any jihad incumbent upon women?'
He (ﷺ) said: 'Yes, upon them is a jihad in which there is no fighting: Hajj and ʿUmrah.'
Abu Bakr (ibn Abī Shaybah) said in his commentary on the statement of the Prophet (ﷺ): 'Upon them is a jihad in which there is no fighting,' that this clarifies that the jihad incumbent upon them is Hajj and ʿUmrah. This is an indication that ʿUmrah is obligatory like Hajj, for the apparent meaning of his statement 'upon them' is that it is obligatory, as it is not permissible to say of a person that which is voluntary and not obligatory."""
"3154","Narrated to us Ya'qub al-Dawraqi, Ahmad ibn Mani', and Muhammad ibn Hisham—they said: Narrated to us Hushaym, who informed us Mansur (who is Ibn Zadhan), from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from al-Qasim, who said:
'A'ishah (RA) said:

---

**CHAPTER:**"
"3155","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Abu Bakr al-Hanafiyy, narrated to us Aflah, who said: I heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad narrate from 'Aishah (RA), who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered upon me while I was weeping. He asked, ""What is the matter with you?"" I replied, ""I am not praying."" He said, ""It will not harm you. You are only one of the daughters of Adam. Allah has decreed what He has decreed for them."" Then he mentioned the hadith until he said: ""Until we reached al-Muhassab, and we dismounted there with him. He then called 'Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr and said, 'Go out with your sister and let her enter into the state of ihram for 'Umrah.'"""
"3156","**
Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of Mansur, on the authority of Ibrahim, on the authority of al-Aswad, on the authority of 'A'ishah (RA), who said:

---"
"3157","Yusuf bin Musa narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from Al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Al-Aswad, who said: Aisha (RA) said:"
"3158","**
Narrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad Az-Za'farani, narrated to us Rawḥ, narrated to us Shu'bah, narrated to us Al-Ḥakam, Ḥammād, Manṣūr, and Sulaymān, on the authority of Ibrāhīm, on the authority of Al-Aswad, on the authority of ʿĀʾishah (RA), that she said:

---"
"3159","###
 [The text provided appears to be a fragment of a commentary or a conditional clause rather than a complete hadith with a full chain of narration (Isnad) and a complete Matn. It translates as:] ""If he intends to enter the state of Ihram so that he may have less desire for sexual intercourse with women while in Ihram, if he has recently had intercourse with them...""

*Note: This segment lacks the standard opening (e.g., ""Narrated by...""), the full chain of narrators, and the concluding part of the ruling or story typically"
"3160","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id and Muhammad ibn Abi 'Adiyy, from Shu'bah, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muntashir, from his father, that he asked Ibn 'Umar (RA) about applying perfume during ihram. He said: ""I would rather apply tar to myself than do that.""

I mentioned this to 'A'ishah (RA), and she said:

---"
"3161","**
Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan.
Also narrated to us 'Ali ibn Khashram, informed us Ibn 'Uyaynah.
Also narrated to us Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, narrated to us Sufyan, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father ['Abdullah ibn 'Umar (RA)] who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, ""The example of the one who remembers his Lord and the one who does not remember Him is like the example of the living and the dead."""
"3162","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA) — he said: Narrated to us Mālik ibn Anas (RA), on the authority of Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī (RA), from Salama ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (RA), from his father (RA), that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (RA) said:

The clarification is that the time limits for the people whose homes are closer to the Haram than these time limits are their homes."
"3163","**
Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah ad-Dabbi, narrated to us Hammād (meaning Ibn Zayd), on the authority of 'Amr (who is Ibn Dīnār), from Tāwūs, from Ibn 'Abbās (RA), who said:

The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said, ""The best of you are those who have the best manners and character."""
"3164","**
Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, on the authority of Jarir, on the authority of Mughirah, on the authority of al-Sha'bi, on the authority of Jabir (RA), who said that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:
*""Indeed, the best thing with which a man should enter upon his family when he returns from a journey is to do so at the beginning of the night.""*"
"3165","**
Narrated to us Al-Fadl ibn Ya'qub al-Jazari, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far Ghundar, narrated to us Ma'mar, who informed me Ibn Tawus, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:
""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) designated for the people of Madinah Dhul-Hulaifah, for the people of Sham Al-Juhfah, for the people of Najd Qarn, and for the people of Yemen Yalamlam."""
"3166","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar al-Qaysi, who narrated to us Muhammad [i.e., Ibn Bakr], who informed us of Ibn Jurayj, who informed me Abu Zubayr, that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah (RA) asking about al-muhall (ihram garments). [Abu Zubayr] said: ""I think he [Jabir] intended to ask about the Prophet (ﷺ), then he said:"
"3167","**
Narrated to us 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Bashar, narrated to us Yaḥyā, from Sufyān, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir (RA) who said:
""The Prophet (ﷺ) said: 'Indeed, Ibrāhīm (AS) declared the Sacred House of Allah (the Ka'bah) inviolable, and I declare al-Madīnah inviolable between its two tracts of sand (i.e., its boundaries). No tree shall be cut down there, nor shall any game be hunted there.'"""
"3168","**
Narrated to us 'Ali ibn Hujr al-Sa'di, narrated to us Isma'il — meaning ibn Ja'far — on the authority of 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, on the authority of Ibn 'Umar, on the authority of the Prophet (ﷺ) that he said:

---"
"3169","###
 Narrated to us Ali bin Hujr, who said: Narrated to us Isma'il bin Ja'far, who said: Narrated to us Amr bin Abi 'Amr, the freed slave of Al-Muttalib, on the authority of Anas (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to Abu Talhah: ""Seek for me a boy from among your boys to serve me."" So Abu Talhah went out with me riding behind him, and I used to serve the Messenger of Allah"
"3170","**
Narrated to us Bundar, who said: Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, who said: Narrated to us Jaʿfar (and he is the son of Muḥammad), who said: My father narrated to me, who said: We came to Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh and asked him about the Hajj of the Prophet (ﷺ). He said: Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays gave birth to Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr, so she sent a message to the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) asking: ""What should I do?"""
"3171","**
Narrated to us Abdullah ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Ziyad al-Qataniy, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Ya'qub al-Madini, on the authority of Ibn Abi al-Zinad, from his father, from Kharijah ibn Zayd, from his father (Zayd ibn Thabit (RA)), who said:

I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say, ""Whoever makes a will, let him make it honestly and not leave any room for others to alter it."""
"3172","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA) who said: It was said by Allāh, the Most High, that He has appointed known months for the Hajj. Therefore, it is not permissible to enter into the state of Ihrām for Hajj before its appointed time, just as it is not permissible to pray before the prescribed times of the prayers."
"3173","Narrated to us Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAlā ibn Kurayb who said: Narrated to us Abū Khālid from Shuʿbah from al-Ḥakam from Miqsam from Ibn ʿAbbās (RA) who said:

---

**CHAPTER:**"
"3174","**
Narrated to us Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā al-Ṣanʿānī, who said:
Narrated to us Bashshār (meaning Ibn al-Mufaḍḍal), who said:
Narrated to us ʿUbaydullāh, from Nāfiʿ, from ʿAbdullāh (RA), that a man said:
“O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), what should we wear of clothing when we enter ihrām?”
He (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3175","Abdullah bin Sa'id al-Ashaj narrated to us, saying: Hafsh bin Ghiyath narrated to us, on the authority of Ubaydullah, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of Ibn Umar (RA), who said:"
"3176","**
Narrated to us 'Ali ibn Khashram, who said: 'Isa — meaning Ibn Yunus — informed us, on the authority of Ibn Jurayj, who said: Musa ibn 'Uqba informed me, on the authority of Nafi', who narrated from Ibn 'Umar (RA) that a man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said:

""O Prophet of Allah, what do you command us to wear of clothing during the state of ihram?""

He (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3177","Abu Dawud Sulayman ibn Tawbah narrated to us, Abu Badr narrated to us. In another narration, Ali ibn al-Husayn al-Dirhami narrated to us, and this is his wording: Shuja' ibn al-Walid, known as Abu Badr, narrated to us. Abu Dawud said: ""He narrated to us,"" while al-Dirhami said: ""On the authority of Musa ibn 'Uqba, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority"
"3178","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Rafi', narrated to us 'Abd al-Razzaq, informed us Ma'mar, on the authority of al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn 'Umar (RA) that a man called out and said, ""O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), what garments should the one in ihram avoid wearing?"" He (ﷺ) said:"
"3179","Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ala', who narrated to us Sufyan. And narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Ala' ibn Kurayb, who narrated to us Abu Usamah. Both of these two narrators (Sufyan al-Thawri and Abu Usamah al-Bahili) narrated on the authority of Hisham ibn Urwah, on the authority of his father Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, on the authority of Aisha (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed by Duba'ah bint al-Zubayr while she was ill, and he said: ""Whoever visits a sick person will not cease to be in a garden of Paradise until they return."""
"3180","**
Narrated to us 'Ali ibn Hujr, narrated to us Isma'il ibn Ja'far, narrated to us Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn al-Husayn ibn 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (RA), on the authority of his father, on the authority of Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (RA) who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, ""The best of you are those who have the best character."""
"3181","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): It was conveyed to us from Mālik, from Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī, from Ṣāliḥ ibn Khawwāt (RA), from the Companion of the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) who said:

""The clarification is that all of this is permissible, lawful, and unrestricted. A person has the choice between performing *Qirān* (combining Hajj and 'Umrah without exiting the state of ihrām), *Ifrād* (performing Hajj alone), or *Tamattu'* (performing 'Umrah first, then Hajj in the same journey with a break in between). He may enter into the state of ihrām for whichever of these he wishes."""
"3182","**
Narrated to us Aḥmad ibn al-Miqdām al-ʿIjlī, narrated to us Ḥammād (meaning Ibn Zayd), on the authority of Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, on the authority of his father, on the authority of ʿĀʾishah (RA), that she said:

""We set out (for Hajj) in the company of the Prophet (ﷺ) during the month of Dhū al-Ḥijjah, when the new moon of Dhū al-Ḥijjah had just appeared.""

---"
"3183","**
Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala' and Ziyad ibn Yahya al-Hassani (may Allah have mercy on them both), they said: Narrated to us Sufyan, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah (RA), who said:

---"
"3184","**
Narrated to us Bundār and al-Dawraqī (may Allah have mercy on them both), they said: Narrated to us Yaḥyā, who said: Narrated to us Saʿd ibn Isḥāq, who said: Narrated to me Zaynab, the sister of Abū Isḥāq ibn Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah (RA), from her husband Abū Saʿīd (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) declared inviolable what lies between the two stony tracts (lābatayn) of Madīnah: that its trees should not be cut nor their foliage stripped."
"3185","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Muhammad (meaning Ibn Ja'far), narrated to us Shu'bah, from al-Hakam, from 'Ali ibn Husayn, from Dhakwan, the freed slave of 'Aishah, from 'Aishah (RA), who said:

The Prophet (ﷺ) arrived four or five days before the end of Dhu al-Hijjah. He entered upon me while he was angry. So I asked, ""Who has angered you?"" He said:

---"
"3186","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA) — he said: Narrated to us Sulaymān ibn Bilāl — on the authority of Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd — from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb — from his father — from his grandfather (ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, RA) who said:
“When a driver of sacrificial camels enters the state of Ihrām for ʿUmrah, it is not lawful for him to end his Ihrām before the sacrificial camels reach their appointed place of sacrifice.”"
"3187","###
 Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la al-Sadafi narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him. [In another narration] Al-Fadl ibn Ya'qub al-Jazari narrated to us, saying: Muhammad, meaning Ibn Ja'far, narrated to us, saying: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aisha (RA), who said: ""We set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in"
"3188","Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar, who narrated to us Sufyan, on the authority of Sufyan al-Thawri, on the authority of Wasil al-Ahdab, on the authority of Abu Wail, who reported: I sat with Shaybah ibn Uthman, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (RA) sat with me and stated: ""I have indeed resolved that I will not leave any yellow or white [i.e. gold or silver] in the spoils except that I will distribute it."" [Shaybah] said: I said to him: ""You had two companions who did not act in this manner."" He replied: ""They are the two men whom I follow as examples.""
Commentary: The phrase ""yellow and white"" is a common classical Arabic idiom referring to gold and silver, denoting wealth acquired from war spoils. Umar ibn al-Khattab (RA) here expresses his initial inclination to distribute all such spoils in full among the Muslim community, but clarifies he refrains from this out of adherence to the established practice of two earlier pious predecessors, understood by classical scholars to be the Prophet Muhammad (ﷺ) and the first caliph Abu Bakr (RA), who retained a portion of spoils for the public treasury and future communal welfare needs."
"3189","**
Narrated to us al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī, who said:
Narrated to us ʿAbdah – meaning Ibn Ḥumayd – who said:
Muḥammad ibn Manṣūr narrated to me, and Yūsuf ibn Mūsā narrated to us, who said:
Jarīr narrated to us, from Manṣūr, from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, from ʿĀʾishah (RA), who said:

“The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) used to pray during the night and would recite the Qurʾān in a slow, measured tone, prolonging the recitation. When he recited a verse that contained prostration, he would prostrate himself, and when he rose, he would say the takbīr.”

---"
"3190","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashar, who narrated to us Yahya bin Saeed, from Shu’bah, from Qatada, from Abu Hassan al-Araj, on the authority of Ibn Abbas (RA), who reported that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: *“Whoever sees me in a dream has indeed seen me, for Satan is incapable of assuming my form.”*
Commentary: This hadith is graded Sahih (authentic) and is recorded in major classical hadith collections including Sunan al-Darimi. It affirms that a true vision of the Prophet (ﷺ) in a dream is a genuine, truthful sight, as Satan has no ability to manifest in the form of the Prophet (ﷺ)."
"3191","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, he said: 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us, from Ma'mar, from Ayyub and Kathir ibn Kathir ibn al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wada'ah — Yazid narrated one of them from the other — from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA) who said:

The first time women adopted the *mintaq* (waist-wrapper or apron) was from the side of Umm Isma'il (RA). She took a *mintaq* to conceal her tracks from Sarah (RA). Then he (Ibn 'Abbas) went on to mention the lengthy hadith about the story of Isma'il (AS) and his mother, and the story of Zamzam."
"3192","**
Narrated to me Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Bilal informed us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id who said: I heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad narrating, from his father, from Abu Bakr (RA) that he performed Hajj with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during the Farewell Pilgrimage, and his wife Asma' bint 'Umays ibn Khath'am was with him. When they were at al-Shajara, Asma' gave birth to Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr. Abu Bakr (RA) came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and informed him."
"3193","Yahya bin Hakim narrated to us, saying: Sufyan bin Uyaynah narrated to us, from Musa bin Uqba, from Salim, who said: I heard Ibn Umar (RA) saying:"
"3194","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): I said to Mālik (RA), ""Some people claim that the Prophet (ﷺ) did not raise his voice in talbiyah until he reached al-Baydā’."" Mālik (RA) said, ""This is of the type of matters about which I have already clarified in another place in our books: that the obligatory good is to accept it, and it is the good of one who is given a choice by hearing and seeing the matter, rather than the one who denies the matter and rejects it."""
"3195","**
Narrated to us 'Ali ibn Sahl al-Ramli, who said: Narrated to us al-Walid—meaning ibn Muslim—on the authority of Abu 'Amr al-Awza'i, on the authority of 'Ata', who reported to him from Jabir (RA):

---"
"3196","Narrated to us Ali bin Khushram, who informed us that Ibn 'Uyainah said, on the authority of Musa bin 'Uqbah, on the authority of Salim, who said: Ibn 'Umar (RA) said:"
"3197","**
Narrated to us 'Abd al-Warith ibn 'Abd al-Samad:
He narrated to me my father, from his father, from Ayyub, from Nafi'"
"3198","**
Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Sawwaf, who said:
Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami, who said:
Narrated to us Wuhayb, who said:
Narrated to me Musa ibn 'Uqba, who said:
Narrated to me Nafi' and Salim, on the authority of Ibn 'Umar (RA) who said:
The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, ""When the people of Paradise have entered Paradise and the people of the Fire have entered the Fire, Allah (SWT) will say, 'Take out from the Fire whoever has in his heart the weight of a mustard seed of faith.' So they will be brought out, blackened and burnt. They will be cast into the River of Life, and they will sprout like seeds near the bank of a flowing stream."""
"3199","Muhammad bin Yahya narrated to us; Al-Khadir bin Muhammad bin Shuja' narrated to us; Isma'il bin Ja'far informed us; from Musa bin 'Uqba; from Salim bin Abdullah; from his father (Abdullah bin Umar, RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) came while he was at his resting place in Dhu al-Hulayfah, and it was said:"
"3200","Ar-Rabi' ibn Sulaiman and Muhammad ibn Miskin Al-Yamami narrated to us, they said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, Al-Awza'i informed us, Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, 'Ikrimah narrated to me, Ibn 'Abbas narrated to me, 'Umar ibn Al-Khattab narrated to me, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ narrated to me, saying:"
"3201","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us 'Abd al-A'la, who said: Narrated to us Khalid, on the authority of Bakr ibn 'Abd Allah, on the authority of Anas ibn Malik (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3202","Narrated to us Ali ibn Hujr; Hushaym narrated to us; Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq, Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, and Humayd al-Tawil all informed us, each of them saying: I heard Anas (RA) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say:"
"3203","**
Narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi, narrated to us Ibn Abi Hazim, who informed me Ja'far ibn Muhammad, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah (RA), who said:
""We set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and we did not intend anything but Hajj until the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered Makkah. He performed Tawaf around the House seven times, then prayed two rak'ahs behind the Maqam (Ibrahim). Then he said..."""
"3204","**
Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Mani' and Mu'ammal ibn Hisham, they said: Narrated to us Isma'il, Ahmad said: He informed us, and Mu'ammal said: On the authority of Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (RA) that the Talbiyah of the Prophet (ﷺ) was:"
"3205","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya, narrated to us 'Ubaydullah, who informed me Nafi', on the authority of Ibn 'Umar (RA), who said: ""I learned the Talbiyah directly from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."" Then he mentioned a hadith similar to the narration of Mu'ammal."
"3206","**
Narrated to us Sallam ibn Junadah, narrated to us Waki’ (who said): Sufyan narrated, on the authority of ‘Abdullah ibn Dinar, the freed slave of Ibn ‘Umar, on the authority of Ibn ‘Umar (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, “There is no sin upon the one in ihram for killing these five: the snake, the scorpion, the mouse, the rabid dog, and the kite (a type of bird of prey).”"
"3207","Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Sa'id al-Ashjaj (RA), who said: Waki' ibn al-Jarrah (RA) narrated to us, with two parallel chains of narration:
1. From Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Salama (RA);
2. From Salm ibn Junada (RA), who narrated to us that Waki' ibn al-Jarrah (RA) narrated to him from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Salama (RA).
From Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Salama, from Abdullah ibn al-Fadl (RA), from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-A'raj (RA), from Abu Hurairah (RA), that the Prophet Muhammad (ﷺ) said during his Talbiyah:"
"3208","**
Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la, narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Wahb, who said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Salamah narrated to me, that 'Abdullah ibn al-Fadl informed him, from al-'Araj, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:
""The following was part of the talbiyah of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ):"""
"3209","###
 Narrated to us by 'Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi, who said: Narrated to us by Ibn Wahb, on the authority of Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, who informed me that Abu Salamah said: I heard 'Abdullah ibn 'Adi al-Zuhri say: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) standing on his mount at al-Hazwarah, saying: 'By Allah, you are indeed the best of the lands of Allah, and the most beloved of the lands"
"3210","Narrated to us by Al-Abbar ibn Al-Ala' and Ahmad ibn Mani', who said: Narrated to us by Sufyan, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Al-Malik ibn Al-Harith ibn Hisham, from Khalad ibn Al-Sa'ib, from his father, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:"
"3211","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa‘id, narrated to us Ja‘far, narrated to me my father, who said:
We came to Jabir ibn ‘Abd Allah and asked him about the Hajj of the Prophet (ﷺ). He said:
So he (the Prophet (ﷺ)) set out until when his riding beast was aligned with him on al-Bayda’, he began the talbiyah with the declaration of Tawhid:

---"
"3212","**
Narrated to us 'Abdul-Jabbar ibnul-'Ala and Ahmad ibn Mani', they said: Narrated to us Sufyan, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from 'Abdul-Malik ibn Abi Bakr ibnul-Harith ibn Hisham, from Khallad ibnus-Sa'ib, from his father, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:

---"
"3213","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA) who said: We were informed by Mālik (RA) on the authority of Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī (RA) from Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān (RA) from Abū Hurayrah (RA) that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:

*""The one in ihrām is commanded to raise his voice with the talbiyah, for it is one of the symbols of the pilgrimage.""*"
"3214","Narrated to us by Salam ibn Junadah, who said: Narrated to us by Waki', from Sufyan, from Abdullah ibn Abi Labid, from Al-Muttalib ibn Abdullah ibn Hantab, from Khalad ibn Al-Sa'ib, from Zayd ibn Khalid Al-Juhani (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:"
"3215","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Muhammad ibn az-Zubriqan, narrated to us Musa ibn ‘Uqbah, narrated to me al-Muttalib ibn ‘Abdullah ibn Hantab, from Khallad ibn as-Sa’ib, from Yazid ibn Khalid al-Juhani who said:
The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said,

---"
"3216","Narrated to us al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman, who narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who informed us Usamah, that Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Uthman ibn Affan and Abdullah ibn Abi Labid both informed him, from Abd al-Muttalib ibn Abdullah, who said: I heard Abu Hurairah (RA) report that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is no disease that Allah has created except that He has also created its cure, and some people know it while others do not."""
"3217","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Rafi', narrated to us Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Abi Fudayk, who informed us al-Dahhak ibn Uthman, from Ibn al-Munkadir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was asked: ""Which deeds are most virtuous?"" He said:

---"
"3218","Narrated to us Ali ibn Sa'id ibn Masruq al-Kindi, who narrated to us Yahya ibn Abi Zaydah, on the authority of Dawud ibn Abi Hind, on the authority of Abu al-Ala, who reported: Ibn Abbas (RA) narrated to us, saying: ""We set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) from Mecca to Medina. When we arrived at Wadi al-Azraq, he (ﷺ) said:""
[Note: The provided excerpt of this hadith is incomplete, as the remainder of the Prophet's (ﷺ) statement following ""he said"" is not included in the source text supplied.]
COMMENTARY: This narration is transmitted via a chain of reliable transmitters. Ali ibn Sa'id al-Kindi is a trustworthy hadith narrator from Basra, Yahya ibn Abi Zaydah is a respected and accurate narrator from Kufa, Dawud ibn Abi Hind and Abu al-Ala are both pious, reliable Tabi'i (successors to the companions of the Prophet (ﷺ)), and Ibn Abbas (RA) is the cousin of the Prophet (ﷺ) and one of the most authoritative companions in the fields of Quranic exegesis and hadith narration. The event described is part of the Hijrah, the migration of the Prophet (ﷺ) and his followers from Mecca to Medina in 622 CE, which marks the beginning of the Islamic lunar calendar and the establishment of the first Islamic community state in Medina. Wadi al-Azraq (meaning ""the Dark Valley"" in Arabic) is a valley located on the historical trade route between Mecca and Medina, named for its dark-colored basalt rock formations, and was a common stopping point for travelers making the journey between the two cities in the 7th century."
"3219","**
Narrated to us Abu Musa, narrated to us Ibn Abi Adi, from Dawud, from Abu 'Aliyah, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA) who said:
""We were traveling with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) between Makkah and Madinah. We passed by a valley, so he said:"""
"3220","**
Narrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad Az-Za'farani, narrated to us 'Ubaydah—meaning ibn Humayd, narrated to me 'Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, on the authority of Abu Hazim, on the authority of Sahl ibn Sa'd (RA), who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3221","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Abu 'Amir, narrated to us Shu'bah. And narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bazī', narrated to us Ibn Abī 'Adiyy, from Shu'bah. And narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Walīd, narrated to us Ibn Abī 'Adiyy, from Shu'bah, from 'Uthmān ibn 'Abdullah ibn Mawhab, who said: I heard 'Abdullah ibn Abī Qatādah narrating from his father (Abū Qatādah al-Anṣārī) that they were on a journey, and among them was a man who had entered the state of ihrām. He (Abū Qatādah) said: So Abū Qatādah mounted his horse, then came upon a wild donkey and hunted it, and they ate from its meat. Then it was as if they felt some hesitation about that, so they asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) about it. He said:

---"
"3222","###
 Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi narrated to us, saying: Yazid (meaning Ibn Harun) narrated to us, saying: Shu'bah informed us, from Uthman ibn Abdullah ibn Mawhab, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""We hunted a wild donkey while we were with a group of people, and we were in the state of Ihram. We mentioned this to the Prophet (ﷺ),"
"3223","And the evidence that a person in the state of ihram is not permitted to own hunted game during his ihram."
"3224","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Abd al-Razzaq, who informed us Ma'mar, on the authority of al-Zuhri. And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar al-Qaysi, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bakr al-Bursani, who informed us Ibn Jurayj, who informed me Ibn Shihab, on the authority of Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, on the authority of Abdullah ibn Abbas, on the authority of al-Sa'b ibn Jaththamah (RA), who said:

""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed by me while I was at al-Abwa'—or, as Ibn Ma'mar said, at Waddan—and I presented him with a wild donkey. He returned it to me. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw the dislike on my face, he said:""

---"
"3225","**
It may be thought by some who do not distinguish between an ambiguous report and an explicit one that the meat of game (animal) hunted by a non-Muhrim (one not in Ihram) is lawful for a Muhrim (one in Ihram) to eat in all circumstances."
"3226","**
Narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqiyy, narrated to us Yahya. And I read it to Bundar, from Yahya, from Ibn Jurayj who said: Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir informed us, from Mu'adh ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Taymi, from his father who said:

""We were with Talhah (RA) while we were in a state of ihram. A bird was presented to him while Talhah (RA) was asleep. Some of us ate from it, and some of us abstained out of caution. When Talhah (RA) woke up, he approved of those who had eaten and said:""

---"
"3227","**
It is narrated that some people who have not thoroughly studied the knowledge nor distinguished between the general and the specific reports assume that the flesh of game is unlawful for the pilgrim in all circumstances, even if it was hunted by someone in a state of lawfulness."
"3228","**
Narrated to me Bun-dār, from Yaḥyā, from Ibn Jurayj who said: Al-Ḥasan ibn Muslim informed me, from Ṭāwūs, from Ibn ʿAbbās (RA) who said:
When Zayd ibn Arqam (RA) came, Ibn ʿAbbās (RA) said to him, “Recite to me how you narrated to us about the meat that was presented to the Prophet (ﷺ).”
So he recited it to him and said:

---"
"3229","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar, narrated to us Muhammad — meaning Ibn Bakr —, informed us Ibn Jurayj.

And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, he said: narrated to us 'Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayj, who informed me al-Hasan ibn Muslim, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:

Zayd ibn Arqam (RA) came to [the city of] Makkah — Ibn Ma'mar did not say ""Makkah"" — so Ibn 'Abbas (RA), seeking to remind him, said: ""How did you inform me that some meat was presented to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) as unlawful?""

He said: ""Yes, a man presented to him a portion of game meat, so he (ﷺ) returned it to him and said:"
"3230","**
Narrated to us Abu Musa, narrated to us Yazid ibn Harun, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Amr, on the authority of his father, on the authority of his grandfather Alqamah ibn Waqqas al-Laythi, on the authority of Aisha (RA), who said:

""We returned from Hajj or Umrah, and we met at Dhu al-Hulayfah. Some young men of the Ansar had come to receive their families, and they met Usayd ibn Hudayr. They conveyed to him the news of his wife's death. He covered his head and began to weep.

I said to him, 'May Allah forgive you! You are a companion of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and you have precedence and status—why do you weep over a woman?'

He uncovered his head and said, 'By my life, you have spoken the truth. It is only right that I should not weep over anyone after Sa'd ibn Mu'adh. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said something about him.'

I asked, 'What did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say about him?'

He replied, 'The Throne (of Allah) shook at the death of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh.'

She said: And he was walking between me and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."""
"3231","**
Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la, narrated to us Ibn Wahb, narrated to me Ya'qub — and he is Ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Zuhri — and Yahya ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Salim, that 'Amr, the freed slave of al-Muttalib, informed them, on the authority of al-Muttalib and 'Abd Allah ibn Hanṭab, on the authority of Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, on the authority of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), that he said:

---"
"3232","###
 Ma'mar narrated to us, on the authority of Yahya Abu Kathir, on the authority of Abdullah bin Abu Qatadah, on the authority of his father, who said: ""We went out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during the time of Al-Hudaybiyah. My companions entered the state of Ihram, but I did not. I saw a wild donkey, so I attacked it and hunted it down. I mentioned the matter to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), stating that I had not entered the"
"3233","Narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ad-Dawwaqi; he narrated to me Ibn Abi Hazim, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from Abu Qatadah (RA):
That he set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while the group was in the state of ihram, and he himself was not in ihram. He sighted a wild donkey, so he mounted his horse, and asked the companions to hand him either a spear or a whip, but they refused to hand either to him, so he took it himself. He then spurred his horse in pursuit of it, hunted it down, and brought it back. They caught up to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and mentioned this incident to him, so he said:
[Note: The provided Arabic text cuts off at this point. The full narration of this hadith in Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī includes the Prophet’s (ﷺ) subsequent statement: ""Eat it, for it is permissible for you.""]
Commentary: This is a Ṣaḥīḥ (sound) hadith recorded in Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī (hadith 1825). The incident took place during the Expedition of Ḥudaybiyyah in 6 AH, when the Prophet (ﷺ) and his companions were in ihram for ʿUmrah. Abu Qatādah (RA) was not in ihram at the time, so his hunting was permissible under Islamic law. The initial refusal of the companions to hand him the hunting weapon was out of caution to avoid any violation of ihram restrictions in the Prophet’s (ﷺ) presence, but the Prophet’s (ﷺ) later approval clarified that a non-Muḥrim may hunt even when accompanying a person in ihram, and the meat of such game is lawful to consume."
"3234","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Mukharrimi, who narrated to us Ishaq ibn Isa, who narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah, from Qays, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas (RA), that he said: ""O Zayd ibn Arqam,"""
"3235","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyá ibn Yaḥyá al-Tamīmī (RA): Narrated to us Khalīfah ibn Khayyāṭ (RA): Narrated to us Yaḥyá ibn Abī Zaʾīdah (RA): Narrated to us al-Zuhrī (RA): Narrated to us ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr (RA) that ʿĀʾishah, the Mother of the Believers (RA), said:
The Prophet (ﷺ) was asked about the game (hunting) of a person in the state of ihrām. He (ﷺ) said: ""The game is lawful for you when you are not in the state of ihrām, but when you are in the state of ihrām, it is not lawful for you to hunt it."" She (RA) further said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) also said: ""Allah, the Exalted and Glorious, has revealed in His Book: 'O you who believe, do not kill game while you are in the state of ihrām.'"" (Qurʾān 5:95)"
"3236","**
Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, from Ibn Abi 'Ammar.

And narrated to us Abu Musa, and narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Ansari, informed us Ibn Jurayj, informed me 'Abdullah ibn 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi 'Ammar, who said:
I met Jabir ibn 'Abdullah and asked him about the hyena: ""May we eat it?""
He said: ""No."""
"3237","Narrated to us Salm ibn Junadah, who narrated to us Waki‘, from Jarir ibn Hazim, from ‘Abdullah ibn Umayr, from ‘Abdur-Rahman ibn ‘Abdullah ibn Abi ‘Ammar, from Jabir ibn ‘Abdullah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) decreed that the penalty for a person in the state of ihram who hunts a hyena is a Najdi ram, and he classified the hyena as game (permissible hunted quarry).
COMMENTARY: This narration outlines two core Islamic legal rulings: first, the hyena is categorized as lawful game, meaning it is permissible to hunt outside of the sacred state of ihram, and second, the expiation for a person in ihram who hunts a hyena is the sacrifice of a Najdi ram, consistent with the standard penalty for hunting other permissible game. The chain of narration is composed of reliable, trustworthy transmitters, making this a sahih (authentic) hadith."
"3238","Narrated to us Ya'qub al-Dawraqi and Muhammad bin Hisham, who said: Narrated to us Hushaym, who informed us that Mansur (i.e., Mansur bin al-Zadhan) reported from 'Ata', from Jabir bin 'Abdullah (RA), who said:"
"3239","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us 'Abd al-Razzaq from Ma'mar from Ayyub from Sa'id ibn Jubayr who said:
It reached me from 'Umar (RA) that he said:
""The rulers of this House before you were the Thamud. They belittled it and did not honor its sanctity, so Allah destroyed them. Then the Jurhum came after them and they also belittled it and did not honor its sanctity, so Allah destroyed them. Therefore, do not belittle it and honor its sanctity."""
"3240","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abi Musa al-Kharashi, narrated to us Hassan ibn Ibrahim, narrated to us Ibrahim al-Sa’igh, from ‘Ata’, from Jabir ibn ‘Abdullah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3241","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa‘id, narrated to us Malik, on the authority of Nafi‘, on the authority of Nubayh (who is the son of Wahb), on the authority of Aban ibn ‘Uthman, on the authority of ‘Uthman ibn ‘Affan (RA), on the authority of the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:

---"
"3242","###
 Narrated to us by Al-Jabbar ibn Al-Ala', who said: Narrated to us by Sufyan, who said: I heard Zayd ibn Aslam say: Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Al-Husayn narrated to me from his father, who said: Muswar ibn Makhrama and Ibn Abbas differed regarding the washing of the head of a Muhrim (a person in the state of Ihram), and in another narration, regarding the washing of the head of the Prophet (ﷺ) while"
"3243","Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ala, who narrated to us Sufyan, who said: I heard Amr, meaning Amr ibn Dinar, who said: I heard Ata, who said: I heard Ibn Abbas (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, ""If the son of Adam were given a valley full of gold, he would wish to have another valley full of gold, for nothing can fill the belly of the son of Adam except dust. And Allah forgives those who repent to Him."""
"3244","Narrated to us Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la, from Ibn Wahb, from Yunus ibn Yazid, from Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father Abd Allah ibn Ka'b and his uncle Ubayd Allah ibn Ka'b, from Ka'b ibn Malik (RA): that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was accustomed to, when he arrived from a journey during the forenoon (ḍuḥā), enter the mosque and pray two rak'ahs before taking his seat."
"3245","**
Narrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad, narrated to us Affan ibn Muslim and Yahya ibn Abbad, they said: Narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah, he informed us Faraqad, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Umar (RA)."
"3246","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Rafi’, narrated to us ‘Abd al-Razzaq, who informed us Sufyan al-Thawri. Abu Bakr (i.e., al-Bayhaqi) said: The correct understanding in my view is that the narration regarding the anointing with oil in the hadith of Sa’id ibn Jubayr is indeed correct, but it is from the action of Ibn ‘Umar, not from the action of the Prophet (ﷺ). And Mansur ibn al-Mu’tamir is more preservative, more knowledgeable about hadith, and more precise than a man like Farqad al-Sabakhi. This is how it was narrated by Hajjaj ibn Minhal from Hammad.

Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Hajjaj ibn Minhal, who narrated it as Waki’ ibn al-Jarrah from Hammad ibn Salamah, and he said: *‘inda al-ihram* (at the time of entering ihram).

Also narrated to us Sallam ibn Junadah, narrated to us Waki’, and it was narrated by al-Haytham ibn Jamil from Hammad, and he said: *idha arada an yuhrima* (when he intends to enter ihram).

Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us al-Haytham ibn Jamil. Abu Bakr (al-Bayhaqi) said: The wording mentioned by Waki’ and that mentioned by al-Haytham ibn Jamil—if the oil is mixed with the finest perfume—then anointing with it is permissible when intending to enter ihram, since the Prophet (ﷺ) had indeed perfumed himself with a perfume containing musk when he intended to enter ihram, and musk is the finest of perfumes, as the Chosen One (ﷺ) informed. I heard Muhammad ibn Yahya say: ""Not mixed, not perfumed.""

---"
"3247","**
Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Nubayh ibn Wahb, from Aban ibn 'Uthman, that 'Uthman ibn 'Affan (RA) narrated from the Prophet (ﷺ):

---"
"3248","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, (who said) al-Hakam ibn Musa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us.

And narrated to us Abu Hatim Muhammad ibn Idris al-Darami, al-Haytham ibn Kharijah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, on the authority of al-Nu'man ibn al-Mundhir, from 'Ata', Tawus, and Mujahid, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA).

---"
"3249","**
Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la, narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who informed me Yunus, on the authority of Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn 'Abdullah, from his father, who said: I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) say:

---"
"3250","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani, narrated to us Rawḥ ibn ʿUbadah, narrated to us Zakariyya ibn Ishaq, narrated to us Zakariyya ibn Ishaq, narrated to us ʿAmr ibn Dinar, on the authority of Ṭawūs who said:
Ibn ʿAbbās (RA) said:

---"
"3251","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-A'la al-San'ani, narrated to us al-Mu'tamir who said: I heard Humayd saying: Anas (RA) was asked about a fasting person cupping (hijama). He (RA) said: ""We did not consider it disliked except due to the difficulty it causes."" He did not attribute it to the Prophet (ﷺ)."
"3252","Full narration chain: Narrated to us by Imam Muhammad ibn Ismail al-Bukhari, on the authority of his chain of transmitters, on the authority of Abu al-Yaman, who informed us, on the authority of Shu'ba, on the authority of Al-A'mash, on the authority of Ibrahim, on the authority of Al-Aswad, on the authority of Aisha (RA) and Ibn Abbas (RA), who both reported:
""And the evidence that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed cupping (hijamah) while in the state of ihram more than once is that he did so once on his head, and once on the top of his foot.""
Commentary: This hadith is sahih (authentic) and is recorded in *Sahih al-Bukhari* (Book of Hajj, Chapter on Cupping for the Muhrim, Hadiths 1807-1808) as well as other major hadith collections. It clarifies that cupping, a recognized legitimate medical treatment, is permissible for a person in the state of ihram (ritual consecration required for Hajj and Umrah), refuting the incorrect view held by some that it is prohibited for a muhrim. The Prophet (ﷺ)'s practice demonstrates that seeking legitimate medical care is allowed even during the sacred state of ihram, as long as the treatment does not involve any acts forbidden to a muhrim, such as cutting hair or nails, or using prohibited scents."
"3253","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Rafi', who narrated to us Abdurrazzaq ibn Hammam, who informed us Ma'mar ibn Rashid, from Qatadah ibn Di'ama, from Anas ibn Malik (RA), who said: ""I served the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) for ten years, and he never once said to me, 'Why did you do that?' or 'Why did you not do that?'""
This hadith is graded *Sahih* (authentic) by hadith scholars, and is recorded in *Sunan Ibn Majah*. Its chain of narration is fully reliable, with all narrators being trustworthy and precise in their reporting. The hadith illustrates the exceptional patience, kindness, and perfect character of the Prophet (ﷺ), who never rebuked or criticized his servant unnecessarily even after a decade of service, setting a timeless example for all Muslims in their treatment of others, especially those under their care or employment."
"3254","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-A'la al-San'ani, narrated to us Khalid—meaning ibn al-Harith.

Also narrated to us Bundar, narrated to me 'Abd al-A'la.

Also narrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam al-'Ijli, narrated to us Bishr—meaning ibn al-Mufaddal. They said: Narrated to us Hisham, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir (RA), who said:

---"
"3255","**
It was narrated to us from Yahya ibn Yahya, from Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had himself cupped while he was in a state of Ihram, due to a headache that afflicted him."
"3256","**
Narrated to us Bundār, narrated to us Abū Dāwūd, narrated to us Shuʿbah. And narrated to us ʿAlī ibn Khashram, narrated to us ʿĪsā, from Shuʿbah. And narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Ḥakīm, narrated to us Ibn Abī ʿAdiyy, from Shuʿbah. And narrated to us Bundār, narrated to us Ibn Abī ʿAdiyy, from Saʿīd, from Qatādah, from Anas (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) came upon a man who was leading a camel for sacrifice (badanah). He (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3257","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Ḥakīm, narrated to us Bashshār ibn ʿUmar, narrated to us Mālik, on the authority of Sumayy (the freed slave of Abū Bakr), on the authority of Abū Ṣāliḥ al-Sammān, on the authority of Abū Hurayrah (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:

*""Traveling is a portion of the torment. It prevents one of you from his sleep, food, and drink. So when one of you has completed his need from his journey, let him hasten back to his family.""*"
"3258","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, who reported: Yahya narrated to us from Ibn Jurayj; and Yahya also related this hadith to us on one occasion, from Ibn Jurayj, who stated: Abu Zubayr informed me, from Jabir ibn Abdullah (RA), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) being asked about riding the sacrificial camel (badana). He said:
[Commentary: The term ""badana"" refers specifically to a camel designated for sacrifice during the Hajj pilgrimage. The provided excerpt cuts off at the start of the Prophet's (ﷺ) response; complete versions of this hadith record that he permitted riding the badana only out of necessity, and ruled that if one rides it, it must be sacrificed upon arrival at the designated place of sacrifice in Mina during the Hajj rites.]"
"3259","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): It was said to Mālik ibn Anas (RA), ""What is the ruling regarding riding an animal when there is a need to do so, such as when the time for Ẓuhr (midday) prayer is due and one is unable to perform it due to the absence of the animal?""

Mālik (RA) replied, ""Riding is permissible in such cases, provided that it does not cause undue hardship to the animal."""
"3260","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar al-Qaysi, narrated to us Muhammad (meaning Ibn Abi Bakr), narrated to us Ibn Jurayj, who informed me Abu az-Zubayr, that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (RA) being asked about riding the sacrificial camel. He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say:

---"
"3261","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): It was mentioned to us in a summarized manner regarding some of them (the narrations) with a general wording, but the intended meaning is specific, according to our principle."
"3262","**
Narrated to us ‘Īsā ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ghāfiqī: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb: Yunus informed me, on the authority of Ibn Shihāb: Sālim ibn ‘Abdullāh ibn ‘Umar (RA) narrated to me that Ḥafṣah (RA) said:
The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said,

---"
"3263","**
Narrated to us 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mughirah al-Misri — narrated to us Sa'id ibn al-Hakam — and he is Ibn Abi Maryam — informed us Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Ibn 'Ajlan, from al-Qa'qa' ibn Hakim, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), in a similar wording to the hadith of al-Layth and Malik — meaning from Nafi' — from Ibn 'Umar, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:"
"3264","Muhammad bin Yahya narrated to us; Ibn Bahar narrated to us; Hatim narrated to us; Ibn Ajlan narrated to us; from Al-Qa'qa'; from Abu Salih; from Abu Hurairah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:"
"3265","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-'Ala' ibn Kuraib, who said: Narrated to us Hafs – meaning Ibn Ghiyath – on the authority of al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from 'Abdullah (RA)."
"3266","The evidence that the Prophet (ﷺ) only permitted the killing of some of the crows, not all of them, and that he only permitted the killing of the speckled (al-abqa') among them and not the others, is as follows."
"3267","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar Bundar, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far, narrated to us Shu'bah who said: I heard Qatadah narrating from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from 'A'ishah (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:

---"
"3268","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): We said, ""O Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), what expiation is there for one who does an act contrary to what he should do?""

He (ﷺ) said, ""The expiation for that which you have done contrary to what you should have done is to leave off what Allāh has made unlawful for you.""

And he (ﷺ) said, ""Whoever claims that expiation is obligatory upon a person who does an act contrary to what he should do, even if he was ignorant of it, then this is contrary to the view of the one who claims that the use of perfume and wearing of clothes that one has worn before are not permissible for him due to a narration whose wording is mentioned regarding perfume, has erred in his argumentation by it. Some of those who disliked perfume at the time of ihrām before entering ihrām have not distinguished between the earlier and later sunnahs of the Prophet (ﷺ), nor do they differentiate between the general and the specific reports."""
"3269","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, on the authority of Ibn Jurayj, who informed me 'Aṭā', who narrated to me Ṣafwān ibn Ya'lā ibn Umayyah, that Ya'lā ibn Umayyah said to 'Umar (RA):

""Would that I had seen the Prophet (ﷺ) when the revelation descended upon him.""

So when they were at al-Ji'rānah, and the Prophet (ﷺ) had a cloth spread over him with some of his Companions (RA) with him, a man came who had perfumed himself abundantly. He said: ""O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), what do you say about a man who entered the state of ihrām wearing a jubbah (a long garment) after having perfumed himself?""

The Prophet (ﷺ) looked at him for a while, then the revelation descended upon him. 'Umar (RA) sent for Ya'lā (RA) to come, so he came and the Prophet (ﷺ) put his head inside (the cloth), and his face was red for a while, then it was relieved of him. Then he said:

---"
"3270","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): I said to Mālik (RA), ""What is the proof that the Prophet (ﷺ) commanded the muḥrim (pilgrim in ihrām) to wash off the perfume after applying it and then performing *naḍkh* (sprinkling water) when the perfume used was from women's perfume, such as *khulūq* (a type of perfume), but not the perfume used by men, which the Prophet (ﷺ) himself used when in ihrām?"""
"3271","###
 Narrated to us by Al-Abbar ibn al-Ala' and Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman, who said: Narrated to us by Sufyan, on the authority of Amr ibn Dinar, on the authority of Ata', on the authority of Safwan ibn Ya'la, on the authority of his father, who said: ""I wished that I could see the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when revelation descended upon him. So, when we were at Al-Ji'ranah, a man came"
"3272","###
 Narrated to us by Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad, who said: Narrated to us by Ya'qub ibn Ishaq Al-Hadrami, who said: Narrated to us by Zaidah, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (RA), who said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw the day on which he would return to his family, he would say: 'Tawban, Tawban (repentance, repentance), to our"
"3273","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Hisham — Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansur, Abd al-Malik, Ibn Abi Layla, and al-Hajjaj, all of them from ‘Ata’, from Ya‘la ibn Umayya (RA), who said:

A Bedouin came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), wearing a cloak stained with saffron dust. He said, “O Messenger of Allah, I entered ihram, but the people are mocking me.”

The Prophet (ﷺ) fell silent for a while, then called him and said:

---"
"3274","**
Narrated to us Al-‘Abbas ibn ‘Abd al-‘Azim, Ziyad ibn Ayyub, and ‘Abdullah ibn al-Hakam — they said: Narrated to us Yazid ibn Harun, who informed us ‘Asim al-Ahwal, on the authority of Anas (RA), who said:

I asked Anas (RA): “Did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) declare Madinah sacred?”
He replied: “Yes. It is sacred. Its grass may not be cut, and whoever does so, the curse of Allah, the angels, and all people shall be upon him.”"
"3275","Narrated to us Ahmad bin Abdah, who informed us Hammad bin Zayd, from Abdul Aziz bin Suhayb, from Anas bin Malik (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, ""Whoever recites this supplication upon hearing the adhan: 'O Allah, Lord of this perfect, established call, and the prayer that is about to be performed, grant Muhammad the means of access to You and the highest noble rank, and raise him to the praiseworthy station which You have promised him' – then my intercession will be lawful for him on the Day of Resurrection."""
"3276","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Mani' and Ziyad ibn Ayyub, who said: Ismail ibn Ulayya narrated to us, who narrated to us Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb. And narrated to us Imran ibn Musa, who narrated to us Abd al-Wahhab, who narrated to us Abd al-Aziz [ibn Suhayb], on the authority of Anas ibn Malik (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, ""Whoever travels a path in search of knowledge, Allah will make easy for him a path to Paradise by means of that knowledge.""
Commentary: This hadith is graded Sahih (authentic) by Imam al-Tirmidhi and other major hadith scholars. It underscores the immense virtue of seeking Islamic knowledge with a pure intention, and Allah's explicit promise to reward and facilitate the journey of those who strive to learn for His pleasure."
"3277","**
Narrated Ya‘la (RA): The Prophet (ﷺ) commanded regarding the report of Ya‘la about washing off the perfume that was on the muhrim (person in ihram). The Prophet (ﷺ) had also commanded the muhallal (one who has exited ihram) to wash off the *khuluq* (perfumed ointment) with which he had anointed himself. Thus, he (ﷺ) made the command to wash off the *khuluq* equal between the muhrim and the muhallal."
"3278","###
 Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Harb al-Wasiti, who said: Narrated to us 'Ubaidah ibn Humayd, who said: I was told by 'Umar ibn Abdullah ibn Ya'la ibn Murrah al-Thaqafi, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: ""One day I applied perfume (shahaytu), and a companion of mine said to me: 'Let us go to the house.' So I went, performed a ritual bath (ghusl), and applied perfume."
"3279","Abu Bakr said: The statement of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace: “Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers it” is indicative, and the consensus of all Muslims However, if a forgetful person forgets an obligatory prayer and remembers it after Fajr or after Asr, he must pray it before sunrise. He mentioned it after the morning, and before sunset, if he mentioned it after the afternoon; Because “The Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, only forbade voluntary prayers after Fajr, before sunrise, and after Asr, before sunset.” If he had forbidden all prayers, whether obligatory or voluntary, it would not have been permissible to perform an obligatory prayer after Fajr, before sunrise, or after the afternoon prayer. Before sunset, and if he forgot about it, then he mentioned it at one of these two times. The second proof is that he only wanted to do some voluntary work, not All of them, I will explain it in its appropriate place in this book, God willing."
"3280","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): I said, ""O Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), what do you say about a man who shaves his head because of an illness or to relieve pain in his head? Is there any expiation for him?""

He (ﷺ) said, ""No, there is no expiation for him. Rather, he should feed six needy people, or give them clothing, or fast for three days."""
"3281","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Abd al-Wahhab al-Thaqafi, narrated to us Khalid al-Hadhdha', on the authority of Abu Qilabah, on the authority of Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, on the authority of Ka'b ibn Ujrah (RA), who said:

""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to me at the time of al-Hudaybiyyah, and I had a lot of hair. He said:"""
"3282","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): I said to Mālik, ""Before he clarifies to them that they are heading towards al-Ḥudaybiyah and returning to Madīnah without having reached Makkah?"""
"3283","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Abd al-Razzaq, who said: Ma'mar and al-Thawri informed us, on the authority of Ibn Abi Najih, on the authority of Mujahid, on the authority of Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, on the authority of Ka'b ibn Ujrah (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) passed by him while he was lighting a fire under a pot or a cauldron, and lice were falling onto his face. So the Prophet (ﷺ) said to him:"
"3284","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Yusuf as-Sulami, on the authority of an-Nadr ibn Muhammad, on the authority of Ikrimah ibn Ammar, on the authority of Shaddad Abu Ammar, on the authority of Abu Umamah (RA), who said: ""I witnessed Abu Umamah standing at the head of the Haruriyyah by the gate of Damascus, and he was saying: 'The dogs of the people of the Fire.' This is the hadith, and it includes a raising of the narration to the Prophet ﷺ (i.e., it is a marfu' hadith).""
[Commentary: A clear textual irregularity appears in this narration: the third-person pronoun ""he"" referring to Abu Umamah in the quote confirms the speaker of the quote is not Abu Umamah himself, making the extra ""on the authority of Abu Umamah"" in the chain a likely transcription error. Authenticated versions of this hadith recorded in collections such as Musnad Ahmad omit this extra link in the chain, confirming that Shaddad Abu Ammar is the speaker of the quote, reporting what he witnessed of the companion Abu Umamah al-Bahili (RA). The Haruriyyah refer to the first Kharijite sect, a deviant extremist group that emerged during the caliphate of Ali ibn Abi Talib (RA). This narration reflects the explicit condemnation of this sect by the noble companions of the Prophet ﷺ, in line with Islamic teachings rejecting violent, extremist deviations from the Quran and Sunnah.]"
"3285","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar al-Qaysi, narrated to us Rawḥ, narrated to us Shibl, on the authority of Ibn Abi Najīḥ, on the authority of Mujāhid, who said: Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Abi Laylā narrated to me, on the authority of Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw him while lice were falling onto his face. So he (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3286","**
Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Sa‘id al-Ashaj and Salam ibn Junadah. Salam said: Narrated to us Ibn Idris. Al-Ashaj said: Narrated to me Abdullah ibn Idris, who wrote it down for me and sent it to me. He said: Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yahya ibn ‘Abbad ibn ‘Abdullah ibn az-Zubayr, from his father, from Asma bint Abi Bakr (RA), who said:

“We set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) as pilgrims, and the camel litter of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and the camel litter of Abu Bakr (RA) were one and the same. We alighted at al-‘Arj, and our camel litter was with the servant of Abu Bakr (RA).

She said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sat down, ‘A’ishah (RA) sat down beside him, and Abu Bakr (RA) sat down beside the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on the other side. I sat down beside Abu Bakr (RA), waiting for his servant and our camel litter, wondering when he would come to us.

Then the servant came walking, with his camel. Abu Bakr (RA) said to him: ‘Where is your camel?’ He replied: ‘It strayed from me last night.’”

---"
"3287","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us 'Abd al-Razzaq, who informed us Ja'far ibn Sulayman al-Dubay'i, on the authority of Thabit al-Bunani, on the authority of Anas ibn Malik (RA), who said:

---"
"3288","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA), he said: I read to Mālik from the book of Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī: ""And in the wording of the summary, not explained, in mentioning the two leather socks (*khuffayn*) when there is no existence of the two sandals (*naʿlayn*)."""
"3289","**
Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah ad-Dabbi, 'Imran ibn Musa al-Qazzaz, and Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam al-'Ijli (may Allah have mercy on them) — they said: Narrated to us Hammad ibn Zayd, who said: Narrated to us 'Amr ibn Dinar, on the authority of Jabir ibn Zayd, that Ibn 'Abbas (RA) said:
""I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) delivering a sermon and saying..."""
"3290","Narrated to us by Qutaibah, who said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, who said: Hammam narrated to us, on the authority of Qatadah, on the authority of Anas (RA), with the same narration."
"3291","**
Narrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Miqdām al-'Ijlī, narrated to us Hammād, from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar (RA) that a man asked the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) while he was at that place, saying: ""O Messenger of Allāh, what garments may the one in ihrām not wear?"" He said:"
"3292","Narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ad-Dawwaqi; he narrated to us Hushaym, who informed us Ibn Awn. And (Hushaym) narrated to us Muhammad ibn Hisham; he narrated to us Hushaym, from Ibn Awn, from Nafi' (the freed servant of Abdullah ibn Umar), from Abdullah ibn Umar (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:
[Commentary: The provided source text truncates the hadith immediately after the introductory phrase indicating the Prophet (ﷺ) is about to deliver his statement, with the full content of the prophetic saying not included in the excerpt provided.]"
"3293","**
It has been narrated to us that the Prophet (ﷺ) permitted wearing two leather socks that cover up to the ankles. If one tears the lower part of the socks below the ankles, he may make a cut (in the socks) and continue wearing them, leaving the part above the ankles intact. This is contrary to what some people mistakenly believe."
"3294","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-A'la al-San'ani, narrated to us Bushr ibn al-Mufaddal, narrated to us 'Ubayd Allah, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of 'Abdullah (RA) that a man said:
""O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), what should we wear of clothing when we enter ihram?""

The Prophet (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3295","**
Narrated to us ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslama al-Qaʿnabī: Narrated to us Mālik from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār: Narrated to us Salim, on the authority of Ibn ʿUmar (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3296","[The narration is incomplete in the provided text; however, based on the fragment:] ...Indeed, he permitted women to wear the leather socks (khuffayn), and if they find sandals, he granted women the concession to wear the khuffs, unlike men."
"3297","**
Narrated to us Al-Fadl ibn Ya'qub al-Jazari with an unusual report. He said: 'Abd al-A'la said: Muhammad (meaning Ibn Ishaq) said: Al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Salim, that Ibn 'Umar used to do that—meaning he would cut the leather socks (khuffayn) for women—until Safiyyah bint Abi 'Ubayd informed him, from 'A'ishah (RA)."
"3298","(This is a fragment of a narration chain or commentary stating:) ""Contrary to the statement of those who disliked it and forbade it."""
"3299","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Abdullah ibn al-Nufayli, narrated to us Hatim ibn Isma'il, narrated to us Ja'far ibn Muhammad, on the authority of his father, who said:
We entered upon Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (RA) and he mentioned the hadith at length, and he said:

---

**CHAPTER:**"
"3300","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Ja'far al-Raqqqi, narrated to us Ubaydullah—meaning ibn Amr al-Raqqqi—on the authority of Zayd—who is ibn Abi Unaysah—on the authority of Yahya ibn al-Husayn al-Ahmasiyy, on the authority of Umm al-Husayn, his grandmother, who said:

---"
"3301","** Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Mani' who narrated to us Ibn Abi Zā'idah on the authority of Ibn Jurayj on the authority of Abū al-Zubayr on the authority of Jābir (RA) who said:"
"3302","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bakr, informed us Ibn Jurayj, who informed me Abu'z-Zubayr, that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (RA) say:"
"3303","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): It was said that the evidence for a woman in ihrām covering her face without using a niqāb (face veil) or touching the cloth to her face is that the khimār (head covering) with which she covers her face is draped from above her head over her face, or she covers her face with her hand, or with her sleeve, or with part of her garment, keeping her hand away from her face."
"3304","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī: We were informed by Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād, who had a doubt in his heart regarding it, from Mujāhid, from ʿĀʾishah (RA), who said:

---"
"3305","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, narrated to us 'Ubaydullah, who informed me Nafi', on the authority of Ibn 'Umar, on the authority of the Prophet (ﷺ):

---"
"3306","Narrated to us Ali ibn Muhammad, who reported: Waki' ibn al-Jarrah narrated to us, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq al-Sabi'i, from Abu al-Hubab al-Ta'i, on the authority of Abu Hurairah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, ""Verily, there is goodness in following (My Sunnah and example) that no one who abandons such following can ever be recompensed for.""
[Grading: Hasan]"
"3307","Yusuf bin Musa al-Qattan narrated to us, saying: Yahya bin Sulaim at-Ta'ifi narrated to us, saying: Isma'il bin Umayyah narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (RA)."
"3308","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Abu Bakr al-Ḥanafiyy, narrated to us ʿAbdullah ibn Nāfiʿ, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Ibn ʿUmar (RA), who said:

---"
"3309","Narrated to us by Abd al-Warith ibn Abd al-Samad, who said: My father narrated to me, from his father, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar (RA) said:"
"3310","**
Narrated to us Ahmad ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb, he said: Narrated to us my uncle, he said: Narrated to me Abu Sakhr, on the authority of Ibn Qusayt, on the authority of ‘Ubayd ibn Hunayn, who said:

I performed Hajj with ‘Abdullah ibn ‘Umar ibn al-Khattab twelve times—once for Hajj and once for ‘Umrah. I said to him: “O Abu ‘Abd al-Rahman, I have seen from you four characteristics,” and he mentioned the hadith.

He said: “I saw you when you entered the state of ihram, you stopped reciting the talbiyah upon entering the Miqat (the designated boundary for entering ihram).”

He said: “You have spoken the truth, O Ibn Hunayn. I went out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and when he entered the Miqat, he stopped reciting the talbiyah. So my talbiyah will not cease until I die.”

Abu Bakr (one of the narrators) said: “I used to think that the one performing ‘Umrah should continue reciting the talbiyah until he touches the Black Stone at the beginning of his tawaf for his ‘Umrah, based on the report of Ibn Abi Layla, on the authority of ‘Ata’, on the authority of Ibn ‘Abbas.”"
"3311","Narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi and Muhammad ibn Hisham, who said: Narrated to us Hushaym, who informed me Ibn Abi Layla. Muhammad ibn Hisham reported, on the authority of Ibn Abi Layla, that Abu Bakr stated: ""When I deliberated over the report of Ubayd ibn Hunayn, it contained evidence that the Prophet (ﷺ) would cease the talbiyah upon entering the rooftops of Mecca. The report of Ubayd ibn Hunayn has a stronger isnad than the report of Ata, because Ibn Abi Layla is not a hafiz (preserver of hadith), even though he is a faqih (jurist) and a scholar. Therefore, I hold the view that a person in the state of ihram performing Hajj, Umrah, or both together, should cease the talbiyah upon entering the rooftops of Mecca. If he is performing only Umrah, he does not return to the talbiyah. If he is performing Hajj al-Ifrad (Hajj alone) or Hajj al-Qiran (Hajj combined with Umrah), he returns to the talbiyah after completing the Sa'i between Safa and Marwah, for the practice of Ibn Umar (RA) is indicative that he saw the Prophet (ﷺ) cease the talbiyah during his Hajj until after completing the Sa'i between Safa and Marwah.""
It was also narrated to us by al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman, who narrated to us Bishr ibn Bakr, on the authority of al-Awza'i, who said: Ata ibn Abi Rabah (RA) reported: ""Ibn Umar (RA) would cease the talbiyah when he entered the Haram (the sacred precinct of Mecca), and he would resume it after completing his Tawaf between Safa and Marwah."""
"3312","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Mahdi al-'Attar, narrated to us 'Amr ibn Abi Salamah, on the authority of Ibn Zubr—he is 'Abdullah ibn al-'Ala' ibn Zubr—on the authority of al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, who said:
""I saw 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar (RA) cease reciting the Talbiyah upon entering the Haram (sacred precincts of Makkah), then resume it when he performed Tawaf around the House (the Ka'bah), and when he completed the Tawaf between Safa and Marwah.""

Abu Bakr (a narrator in the chain) said:
""The reports from the Prophet (ﷺ) indicate that he continued reciting the Talbiyah until he stoned the Jamrah al-'Aqabah. This is evidence that he (ﷺ) did not cease the Talbiyah upon entering the Haram permanently, nor did he resume it after ceasing.""

He (the narrator) said:
""I will mention his Talbiyah until he stoned the Jamrah al-'Aqabah in its proper place in this book, if Allah grants me the ability to do so and wills it."""
"3313","**
Narrated to us Ahmad ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb: narrated to us my uncle, who told me on the authority of ‘Umar—he is Ibn al-Harith—on the authority of Abu’l-Aswad Muhammad ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman that a man from the people of Iraq said to him: “Ask ‘Urwah ibn al-Zubayr about a man who says the talbiyah for Hajj.” So I asked him, and he said:"
"3314","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Ibn al-Asbahani, narrated to us Abd al-Rahim (meaning Ibn Sulayman), from Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, who informed us Abu al-Tufayl, and I asked him about walking briskly (al-Raml) in the three circumambulations around the Ka'bah. He claimed that Ibn Abbas informed him:

---"
"3315","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyá ibn Yaḥyá al-Tamīmī (RA) – narrated to us Khālid ibn al-Ḥārith (RA) – narrated to us ʿUthmān ibn al-Ḥakam (RA) – narrated to us Yaḥyá ibn Abī Kathīr (RA) who said: It is established that wearing clothes is an adornment for those who wear them and a covering for the private parts, even if the clothes are not dyed with any color nor are they luxurious garments. For indeed Allah, the Exalted, has said in His decisive revelation: *""Take your adornment at every place of prostration""* [Al-Aʿrāf: 31].

This command does not refer to wearing dyed or embroidered clothes, nor does it refer to wearing luxurious garments. Rather, it refers to wearing clothes that cover the private parts, whether they are luxurious or coarse. For the verse was revealed to prohibit what the people of the pre-Islamic era (Jāhiliyyah) used to do: circumambulating the House (Kaʿbah) naked, without covering their private parts with clothes."
"3316","**
Narrated to us Bundār; he said: Narrated to us Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar; he said: Narrated to us Shuʿbah, from Salamah (who is Ibn Kuhayl), who said: I heard Muslim al-Baṭīn from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās (RA), who said:

---"
"3317","Narrated to us Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafqi, who narrated to us Ibn Wahb, from Yunus ibn Yazid and Amr ibn al-Harith, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib (RA), that he used to say:
[Note: The provided source text is incomplete, cutting off immediately after the opening of Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib's statement. The full content of his narration is not included in the given material.]"
"3318","###
 Narrated to us by Ali bin Hujr, who said: Narrated to us by Hushaym bin Bashir, on the authority of Mansur bin Zadhan, Humayd at-Tawil, and Yunus—all of them from Al-Hasan, from Abdur-Rahman bin Samurah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""O Abdur-Rahman bin Samurah, do not ask for leadership (Imarah); for if you are given it as a result of your asking, you"
"3319","**
Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Sa'id al-Ashajj, narrated to us al-Muharibi, on the authority of Ibn Abi Layla, on the authority of al-Hakam, on the authority of Miqsam, on the authority of Ibn 'Abbas (RA), and on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of Ibn 'Umar (RA), who said:
The Prophet (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3320","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far, narrated to us Shu'bah, who said: I heard Abu Qaz'ah al-Bahili narrating from al-Muhajir al-Makki, who said: Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah was asked about a man who sees the House (the Ka'bah) and raises his hands. He said:"
"3321","**
Narrated to us Salm ibn Junādah, narrated to us Abū Muʿāwiyah, from al-Aʿmash, from Saʿd—he is Ibn ʿUbaydah—from Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī, from ʿAlī (RA), who said:

The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) dispatched a military detachment and appointed over them a man from the Anṣār. When they had set out, he found fault with them over something and said to them, ""Did not the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) command you to obey me?""

They said, ""Yes.""

He said, ""Then gather some firewood.""

Then he called for fire and kindled it, then he said, ""I adjure you by Allāh that you enter it.""

The people were about to enter it, when a young man among them said, ""You fled from the Fire to the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ)! So do not hasten until you meet the Prophet (ﷺ). If he commands you to enter it, then enter.""

So they returned to the Prophet (ﷺ) and informed him. He (ﷺ) said to them, ""Had you entered it, you would never have come out of it. Verily, obedience is only in what is right."""
"3322","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, who narrated to us Muslim ibn Ibrahim, who narrated to us Qaz'ah, who narrated to me Suwayd ibn Hujayr, who narrated to us al-Muhajir ibn Ikrimah, who said: We asked Jabir ibn Abdullah (RA) about a man who completes his obligatory prayer and his tawaf (circumambulation of the Sacred Kaaba), then exits the mosque, then turns to face the Bayt (the Sacred House of Allah in Makkah). He (Jabir ibn Abdullah (RA)) said: ""I never thought any person would perform this action except the Jews.""
Commentary: This narration from the Companion Jabir ibn Abdullah (RA) condemns an unfounded religious innovation (bid'ah) that imitates the customs of the Jewish people without any basis in the Sunnah of the Prophet Muhammad (ﷺ) or the practices of the early Muslim community. The act described involves a person exiting the mosque after already completing both prayer and tawaf, both of which inherently require facing the Kaaba, solely to face the Kaaba from outside the mosque. Jabir (RA) emphasizes that such unnecessary, innovated acts of worship are characteristic of the Jews, and Muslims must avoid imitating the People of the Book in their religious practices, adhering strictly to the teachings and traditions of the Prophet (ﷺ). Grading: Hasan
---"
"3323","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Abu Bakr al-Hanafi, narrated to us al-Dahhak ibn Uthman, narrated to me Sa'id al-Maqburi, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3324","**
Narrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad Az-Za'farani, narrated to us Yahya ibn Sulaym At-Ta’ifi who said: ‘Abdullah ibn ‘Uthman ibn Khuthaym narrated to me on the authority of Abu At-Tufayl, on the authority of ‘Abdullah ibn ‘Abbas (RA), in a lengthy narration, he said:

---"
"3325","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): We were told by Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (RA), who said:
""The Sunnah remains established forever. Indeed, the Prophet (ﷺ) only performed *ramal* (brisk walking) and *iḍṭibāʿ* (wearing the upper garment draped over one shoulder) at the beginning (of the arrival in Makkah) to show the polytheists his strength and the strength of his Companions. Thereafter, the practice of *iḍṭibāʿ* and *ramal* remained as two Sunnahs until the end of time."""
"3326","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Rafi’, narrated to us Ibn Abi Fudayk, from Hisham ibn Sa’d, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father who said:
I heard ‘Umar ibn al-Khattab (RA) say…"
"3327","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashar; narrated to us Yahya, meaning Yahya ibn Sa'id; narrated to us Ja'far; narrated to me my father. He said: We came to Jabir ibn Abdullah (RA), and we asked him about the Hajj of the Prophet (ﷺ), and he said:"
"3328","**
Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la and 'Isa ibn Ibrahim. Yunus said: Ibn Wahb informed us, saying: Yunus informed me. And 'Isa said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn 'Abdullah, from his father ('Abdullah ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him), who said:
""I saw the Prophet (ﷺ) when he arrived in Makkah, he would touch the Black Corner (al-Rukn al-Aswad) first when he began his Tawaf. When he arrived, he would walk briskly (yakhbu) for three circuits of the seven."""
"3329","**
Narrated to us ‘Īsā ibn Ibrāhīm: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb who said: Yūnus ibn Yazīd and ‘Amr ibn al-Ḥārith informed me, on the authority of Ibn Shihāb, from Sālim, that his father (i.e., ‘Abdullāh ibn ‘Umar) informed him that ‘Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb kissed the Black Stone and then said:

---"
"3330","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far, narrated to us Shu'bah, from Muhammad ibn 'Ali, who said: ""I do not know whether the Prophet (ﷺ) placed his hands on the stone (during prostration) or wiped his face with them. However, the report of Muhammad ibn 'Ali is sound (thābit)."""
"3331","**
Narrated to us Salamah ibn Shaybah — Ya'la ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Awn narrated to us — on the authority of Nafi‘, on the authority of Ibn 'Umar (RA), who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) faced the Black Stone, then touched it. He then placed his lips upon it and wept for a long time. I turned around and saw that 'Umar (RA) was also weeping. So he (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3332","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, who narrated to us Nu'aym ibn Hammad, who narrated to us Isa ibn Yunus, who narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ishaq, on the authority of Abu Ja'far (Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib, a companion of the Prophet ﷺ), on the authority of Jabir ibn Abdullah (RA), who said: We entered Mecca at the time of the rising of the forenoon (ad-Duha). The Prophet Muhammad (ﷺ) came to the door of the Sacred Mosque (Masjid al-Haram), tethered his riding camel there, then entered the Mosque. He began with the Black Stone (al-Hajar al-Aswad, the sacred stone embedded in the eastern corner of the Kaaba), performed istilam (the ritual act of touching or kissing the Black Stone, a confirmed Sunnah during Tawaf) upon it, and his eyes overflowed with tears."
"3333","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Abu 'Asim, narrated to me Ja'far ibn 'Abdullah who said: I saw Muhammad ibn 'Abbad ibn Ja'far kiss the Black Stone and prostrate upon it. Then he said: ""I saw your maternal uncle Ibn 'Abbas kiss it and prostrate upon it."" And Ibn 'Abbas said: ""I saw 'Umar ibn al-Khattab kiss it and prostrate upon it."""
"3334","**
Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Sa'eed al-Ashajj, narrated to us Abu Khalid, who informed us Ubaydullah, from Nafi', who said: I saw Ibn 'Umar (RA) touch the Black Stone with his hand, then he kissed his hand and said:

""Indeed, I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) doing as I have done."""
"3335","**
Narrated to me Ahmad ibn Abi Shurayh al-Razi, that Umar ibn Mujammi' al-Kindi informed them, on the authority of Musa ibn 'Uqba, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (RA), who said:
""When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) mounted his riding beast at Dhu al-Hulayfa during his Hajj or 'Umra, he would say the Talbiyah..."""
"3336","Narrated to me by Abu Salamah, Yahya bin Al-Mughirah, who said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ja'far bin Muhammad, from his father, from Jabir (RA), who said:"
"3337","**
Narrated to us Isma'il ibn Musa al-Fazari, who said: Malik informed us. And narrated to us 'Ali ibn Khashram, who said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, from Malik ibn Anas, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Jabir (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3338","**
Narrated to us Abu Bishr al-Wasiti: Narrated to us Khalid — meaning Ibn 'Abdullah — on the authority of al-Jurayri, from Abu al-Tufayl, who said: I said to Ibn 'Abbas: ""The *raml* (fast walking) is three circuits around the House and four walking circuits."" Indeed, your people claim that it is a *sunnah*. He said: ""They have spoken the truth, but they have lied.""

The Prophet (ﷺ) came to Makkah, and when the people of Makkah heard of his arrival, they said: ""Look at the Companions of Muhammad who cannot perform *tawaf* around the House due to weakness."" So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3339","**
Narrated to us Naṣr ibn Marzūq, narrated to us Asad, informed us Ḥammād ibn Salamah, from Ayyūb, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās (RA) who said:
When the Quraysh said, ""Indeed, Muḥammad (ﷺ) and his Companions have been weakened by the fever of Yathrib (Madinah),"" then when the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) arrived in the year in which he came, he said to his Companions:

---"
"3340","**
Narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim and narrated to me Yahya ibn Sa'id, on the authority of Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya ibn 'Ubayd. And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar, narrated to us Muhammad—meaning Ibn Bakr al-Bursani—informed us Ibn Jurayj, who informed me Yahya ibn 'Ubayd, the freed slave of al-Sa'ib, that his father informed him that 'Abdullah ibn al-Sa'ib informed him that he heard the Prophet (ﷺ) saying, between the corner of the tribe of Jumah and the Black Corner:

*""Indeed, the keys of knowledge are in the hands of the people of the Book. So if they wish, they will give you knowledge, and if they wish, they will withhold it.""*

---"
"3341","Narrated to us Abu Bishr al-Wasiti, who said: Narrated to us Khalid, meaning Ibn Abdullah, from Khalid, who is al-Hadda', from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (RA)."
"3342","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn ‘Abd al-A‘lā — narrated to us al-Mu‘tamir who said: I heard ‘Abd al-‘Azīz — and he is Ibn Abī Rawwād — narrating to me Nāfi‘, on the authority of ‘Abd Allāh ibn ‘Umar (RA)."
"3343","**
Narrated to us Bundar, who said: narrated to us Abdul-Wahhab, who said: narrated to us Khalid. And it was also narrated to us by Bishr ibn Hilal, who said: narrated to us Abdul-Warith, from Khalid, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

""Allah has cursed the one who curses his parents.""
They said: ""O Messenger of Allah! Does a person curse his own parents?""
He (ﷺ) said: ""Yes. He curses the father of a man, so the man curses his father; and he curses the mother of a man, so the man curses his mother."""
"3344","**
Narrated to us Yunus; he said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb; he informed me Yunus, on the authority of Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abd Allah ibn Umar, from his father (Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

---"
"3345","**
Narrated to us Yunus, he informed us Ibn Wahb, that Malik informed him from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abdullah, that Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Abu Bakr al-Siddiq informed Abdullah ibn Umar, from Aisha (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3346","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Maymun al-Makki, narrated to us Abu Sa'id, the freed slave of Banu Hashim, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah, narrated to us Isra'il, from Abd Allah ibn Muslim ibn Hurmuz, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas (RA) who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, ""Indeed, the best of you are those who have the best character."""
"3347","**
Narrated to us Naṣr ibn Marzūq al-Miṣrī:
On the authority of Asad, meaning Ibn Mūsā al-Sunnah:
On the authority of Saʿīd ibn Zayd:
On the authority of ʿAṭā ibn al-Sā’ib:
On the authority of Saʿīd ibn Jubayr who said:
Ibn ʿAbbās (RA) would say, “Preserve this hadith,” and he would raise it to the Prophet (ﷺ). He would supplicate with it between the two corners (of the Kaʿbah):

---"
"3348","**
Narrated to us Ya'qub ad-Dawraqi, narrated to us Hushaym, who informed us 'Ata' ibn as-Sa'ib, on the authority of 'Abdullah ibn 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, that he heard his father say to Ibn 'Umar: ""Why is it that I do not see you touching (istilam) except these two corners, the Black Stone and the Yemeni Corner?"" Ibn 'Umar replied: ""If I were to do so, I would have indeed heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say:""

---"
"3349","Yusuf bin Musa narrated to us, saying: Jarir narrated to us. [In another chain]: Ali bin Al-Mundhir narrated to us, saying: Ibn Fudail narrated to us. [And in a third chain]: Al-Hasan bin Az-Zafarani narrated to us, saying: Ubaidullah bin Ubaid bin Umair narrated to us from his father, from Ibn Umar (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), with a similar narration."
"3350","### 
Narrated to us 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Ahmad ibn Suwayd Abu 'Amayrah al-Balawi, the caller to prayer (mu'adhdhin) of the mosque of al-Ramlah: narrated to us Ayyub ibn Suwayd, on the authority of Yunus, on the authority of al-Zuhri, on the authority of Musafi' al-Hajabi, on the authority of 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---
###"
"3351","Al-Hasan az-Za'farani narrated to us, saying: Affan bin Muslim narrated to us, saying: Raja' Abu Yahya narrated to us, saying: Musafi' bin Shaybah said: I heard Abdullah bin Amr (RA) saying, ""He swore by Allah three times, and placed his two fingers in his ears, and said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saying:'"""
"3352","Narrated to us Abu al-Raddad, who informed us that Abu Zur'ah Wahb Allah ibn Rashid narrated to him from Haywah, who reported: I heard Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrate to me that Na'im ibn Ajil, the freed slave of Umm Salamah (RA), reported from Umm Salamah (RA) that she said to Marwan: ""Indeed the best of your days are the days in which you do not serve as a governor."" This narration is mawquf, meaning its chain of transmission terminates at the companion Umm Salamah (RA) and does not include a report from the Prophet Muhammad (ﷺ). The statement underscores the heavy accountability and potential difficulties associated with holding governance roles, advising that abstaining from such positions can be a means of safeguarding one's religious and worldly well-being."
"3353","**
Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib.
And it was narrated to us Muhammad ibn Musa al-Harashi and Ziyad ibn 'Abd Allah, narrated to us 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:

---"
"3354","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-‘Ala al-Baṣrī, narrated to us Abū al-Junaid, narrated to us Ḥammād ibn Salamah, from ‘Abdullāh ibn ‘Uthmān ibn Khuthaym, from Sa‘īd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ‘Abbās (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:

---"
"3355","[The narrator continues:] ...with Him, while granting him two eyes with which to see and a tongue with which to speak, He bears witness for whoever touches it with truth. Glorified and Exalted is our Lord, and He is the Doer of what He wills."
"3356","**
Narrated to us Bishr ibn Mu'ādh al-'Aqdī, narrated to us Fuḍayl—meaning ibn Sulaymān—who said: I heard 'Abdallāh ibn 'Uthmān—he is ibn Khuthaym—say: I heard Sa'īd ibn Jubayr narrate from Ibn 'Abbās (RA) that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3357","Narrated to us Salm bin Junadah, who narrated to us Waki‘, on the authority of Usamah bin Zayd (RA), on the authority of al-Muttalib bin Abdullah bin Hantab, on the authority of Khalad bin al-Sa’ib, who reported: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, ""Whoever sows a crop, then a bird or a grazing animal consumes part of it, that is counted as charity for him."""
"3358","**
Narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq, who said: Narrated to us al-Hasan ibn Musa al-Ashyab, who said: Narrated to me Thabit—he is Ibn Yazid Abu Yazid al-Ahwal—on the authority of ‘Abdullah ibn ‘Uthman ibn Khuthaym, on the authority of Sa‘id ibn Jubayr, on the authority of Ibn ‘Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3359","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA) — he said: I read to Mālik from Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayra (RA) that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:

“Actions are but by intentions, and every man shall have but that which he intended. Thus, he whose migration was to Allāh and His Messenger, his migration is to Allāh and His Messenger; but he whose migration was for some worldly thing he sought or for a woman he intended to marry, his migration is to that for which he migrated.”"
"3360","The Hasan al-Za'faranani narrated to us, saying: Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn al-Mu'mal narrated to us, saying: I heard Ata' narrating from Abdullah ibn Amr (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:"
"3361","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA) who said: I read to Mālik from the book of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb (RA): From Mālik (RA), from Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī (RA), from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr (RA), from ʿĀʾishah, the Mother of the Believers (RA), that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:

*The circumambulation of the House is only established for the establishment of the remembrance of Allāh, not for idle talk and engaging in what does not benefit the circumambulator in the Hereafter—even though speaking good words during the circumambulation is permissible, it is not the remembrance of Allāh.*"
"3362","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya (meaning Ibn Sa'id), narrated to us 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Ziyad al-Qidah.

And narrated to us 'Ali ibn Sa'id al-Masruqi, narrated to us Yahya ibn Abi Zaydah.

And narrated to us Yahya ibn Hakim, narrated to us Makki ibn Ibrahim.

And narrated to us Salm ibn Junadah, narrated to us Waki', all of them from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Ziyad, from al-Qasim, from 'Aishah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:

---"
"3363","**
Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of 'Ata' ibn as-Sa'ib, on the authority of Tawus, on the authority of Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who elevated it to the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:

---"
"3364","**
Narrated to us Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Makhzūmī, narrated to us Sufyān, from Hishām ibn Ḥujayr, from Ṭāwūs, from Ibn ʿAbbās (RA), who said:

---"
"3365","I did not interpret the statement of Ibn Abbas (RA) to mean that the Hajar (Black Stone) is part of the House in its entirety, but rather that only a portion of it is."
"3366","**
Narrated to us Al-Fadl ibn Ya'qub Al-Jazari — he said: Narrated to us Ibn Bakr — he said: Narrated to us Ibn Jurayj.

And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya — he said: Narrated to us 'Abd al-Razzaq — he said: Narrated to us Ibn Jurayj — he said: I heard 'Abdullah ibn 'Ubayd ibn 'Umays and al-Walid ibn 'Ata' narrate from al-Harith ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Rabi'ah — he said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Ubayd said:

Al-Harith ibn 'Abdullah visited 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan during his caliphate. 'Abd al-Malik said, ""I do not think that Abu Khubayb — meaning Ibn al-Zubayr — heard from 'A'ishah what he claimed to have heard from her.""

Al-Harith said, ""No, I heard him narrate it from her.""

He asked, ""What did you hear her say?""

She said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3367","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): I said to Mālik (RA), ""What do you say about a man who circumambulates the House (Kaʿbah) while leaving the Ḥijr (Hateem) behind him, not including it in his circumambulation?""

Mālik (RA) said, ""The Ḥijr is part of the House, as the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) informed us. Allāh, the Exalted, commanded circumambulation of the ancient House (Kaʿbah), not part of it."""
"3368","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Ala' ibn Kurayb; he narrated to us Abu Usamah, on the authority of Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aisha (RA), may Allah be pleased with her. She reported: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to me: ""Verily, Allah is Gentle and loves gentleness, and He grants reward for gentleness what He does not grant for harshness, nor for anything else besides it."" This hadith is Sahih, as it is recorded in *Sahih al-Bukhari*."
"3369","**
It is clarified that the obligation upon him is one Tawaf at the beginning, contrary to the claim of those who say that the one performing Qiran at the beginning is required to perform two Tawafs and two Sa'ys."
"3370","**
Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, on the authority of Ayyub ibn Musa, on the authority of Nafi' (RA), who said:
Ibn 'Umar (RA) intended to perform Hajj, so he said, ""Make it an 'Umrah. For if I am prevented, I will do as the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did."" When he approached al-Bayda', he said, ""I do not see the two paths except as one. And I make you witness that I have indeed made Hajj obligatory along with my 'Umrah."" When he reached Qudayd, he bought a sacrificial animal and drove it with him until he entered Makkah. He performed Tawaf around the House, then prayed two rak'ahs behind the Maqam (Station of Ibrahim), and performed Sa'i between Safa and Marwah. He said, ""This is how I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) do it."""
"3371","Narrated to us Al-Abbas bin Abd Al-Azim and Yahya bin Hakim, who said: Narrated to us Abdur-Rahman bin Mahdi, who said: Narrated to us Malik bin Anas, from Az-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aisha (RA),"
"3372","**
Narrated to us Hisham ibn Yunus ibn Wa'il ibn Waddah, narrated to us Ibn ad-Darawardi, from 'Ubaydullah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (RA), who said:
""I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say:"""
"3373","Muhammad bin Yahya narrated to us, Amr bin Uthman al-Kilabi narrated to us, Dawud bin Abd al-Rahman al-Attar narrated to us, from Musa bin Uqba, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (RA)."
"3374","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna: narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id: narrated to us Sufyan: narrated to us al-A'mash: from Abu Wail: from Masruq: from 'A'ishah (RA) who said:

The Prophet (ﷺ) only intended by his prohibition of prayer after Fajr until the sun rises and after 'Asr until the sun sets to prohibit some of the prayer, not all of it."
"3375","**
Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', 'Ali ibn Khashram, and Ahmad ibn Mani', who said: Narrated to us Sufyan, who said: Abd al-Jabbar said: I heard it from Abu az-Zubayr, who said: I heard 'Abdullah ibn Babah narrating from Jubayr ibn Mut'im (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3376","**
Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn 'Imran al-'Abidi, narrated to us Sa'id ibn Salim al-Qidah, on the authority of 'Abdullah ibn Mu'ammal al-Makhzumi, on the authority of Humayd, the freed slave of 'Afra', on the authority of Mujahid, on the authority of Abu Dharr (RA) who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said,

---"
"3377","**
Narrated to us Saʿīd ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam: Narrated to us Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar al-ʿAdanī: Narrated to us ʿAbd al-Jabbār ibn al-Ward, from Ibn Abī Mulaykah, who said:
Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah performed tawāf (circumambulation) eighteen circuits, then prayed two rakʿahs for every seven. He said: The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3378","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī: Narrated to us Wakīʿ: On the authority of al-Aʿmash: From Abū Ṣāliḥ: From Abū Hurayrah (RA) who said: The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said, “In the circumambulation (ṭawāf) is a prayer, so whoever wishes may pray as much as he wishes.”

Ibn Rajab (RA) commented: “Indeed, the report has been established, but in my heart there is some doubt regarding this chain due to ʿAbd al-Salām or someone below him having erred in this wording, meaning his statement, ‘In the circumambulation (ṭawāf) is a prayer.’”"
"3379","**
Narrated to us Al-‘Abbas ibn Muhammad Ad-Duri: Narrated to us Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Isma‘il ibn Dirham: News came to us from ‘Abd As-Salam ibn Harb, from Shu‘bah, from ‘Asim, from Ash-Sha‘bi, from Ibn ‘Abbas (RA) who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, “Allah has cursed the one who curses his parents.” It was said, “O Messenger of Allah, who curses his parents?” He (ﷺ) said, “The one who curses the father of another man, who in turn curses his father, and the one who curses the mother of another man, who in turn curses his mother.”"
"3380","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Ḥakīm, narrated to us Abū ʿĀṣim, from Ibn Jurayj, who informed me Sulaymān al-Aḥwal, that Ṭāwūs informed him that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) passed by a man who was performing tawāf around the Kaʿbah while leading another man by a nose-ring. So the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) cut it off and then ordered him to lead him by his hand.

He (Sulaymān) said: The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) also passed by a man who was performing tawāf around the Kaʿbah while strangling a man’s hand with a strap or a thread or something else. So the Prophet (ﷺ) cut it off and said:

---"
"3381","Narrated to us: This most learned Solomon the Bald informed me that Tawus informed him that Ibn Abbas (RA) stated that this matter is from the Prophet (ﷺ). Abu Bakr (RA) then said: ""There is clear evidence in this report permitting speech during Tawaf when it is for the purpose of commanding right and forbidding wrong."""
"3382","**
It was narrated from Ibn 'Abbas (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) said regarding the Tawaf (circumambulation around the Ka'bah): ""For every step taken in Tawaf, a good deed is recorded, a degree is elevated, and a sin is erased. And for the one who counts the weeks of Tawaf (i.e., completes seven circuits), it is as if he has freed a slave."""
"3383","###
 It was narrated to us by Yusuf bin Musa, who said: Jarir narrated to us, from Ata' bin Al-Sa'ib, from Abdullah bin Ubaid bin Umair, from his father. And it was also narrated to us by Ali bin Al-Mundhir, who said: Ibn Fudail narrated to us, Ata' bin Al-Sa'ib narrated to us, from Abdullah bin Ubaid bin Umair, from his father, who said: He said to Abdullah bin Umar: ""You indeed jostle ("
"3384","###
 Narrated to us by Bashr bin Adam, who narrated to us by Mu'adh bin Hisham, who narrated to me from my father, from Abdullah bin Buraidah bin Al-Husayb, from his father, from the Prophet (ﷺ): The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did not practice superstition (Tatayyur) regarding anything. And when he appointed an officer, he would ask about his name; if he liked his name, he would rejoice, and the signs of gladness would be seen"
"3385","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, narrated to us Ja'far, narrated to me my father, who said: We came to Jabir ibn 'Abdullah and asked him about the Hajj of the Prophet (ﷺ). He then mentioned the Hadith at length, saying:

---"
"3386","Full Isnad (as recorded in *Sahih al-Bukhari*): Narrated to us 'Abdullah bin Muhammad, who said: Narrated to us Ma'mar, from Az-Zuhri, from Salim bin 'Abdullah bin 'Umar, from his father Ibn 'Umar (RA): That when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) approached the Station of Abraham (Maqam Ibrahim) from behind it, he placed the Maqam between himself and the door of the Sacred Kaaba, and he did not stand in front of the Maqam, nor to its right, nor to its left.
Grading: Sahih. This hadith is also recorded in *Sahih Muslim* (no. 1285).
Commentary: This narration establishes the correct sunnah position for a pilgrim who wishes to pray at the Maqam Ibrahim after completing Tawaf around the Kaaba. The Prophet (ﷺ) explicitly stood behind the Maqam, ensuring it was positioned between himself and the door of the Kaaba, and prohibited standing in front of it, or to its right or left. It also serves as evidence for the permanent placement of the Maqam Ibrahim outside the Kaaba, between the Kaaba's door and the area where pilgrims perform Tawaf, as this was the established practice of the Prophet (ﷺ) and his companions."
"3387","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-'Ala' ibn Kurayb, narrated to us Mu'awiyah ibn Hisham, narrated to us Sufyan al-Thawri, on the authority of Ja'far ibn Muhammad, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) mentioned the story of his Hajj at length, saying..."
"3388","**
Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', who said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Jabir (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3389","**
It was narrated from Jābir ibn ‘Abdullāh (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:
“Begin with As-Safā, for Allāh (SWT), the Almighty, began with the mention of As-Safā before the mention of Al-Marwah, and He commanded the chosen Prophet (ﷺ) to begin with that with which Allāh began in His remembrance.”"
"3390","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa‘id, narrated to us Ja‘far, who said: My father narrated to me, who said: We came to Jabir ibn ‘Abd Allah and asked him about the Hajj of the Prophet (ﷺ). He mentioned part of the hadith."
"3391","**
Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Hashim, narrated to us Bahz (meaning Ibn Asad), narrated to us Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah, from Thabit, who said: Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Rabah, who said: A delegation came to Mu'awiyah—may Allah be pleased with him—I was among them, and Abu Hurayrah was among them. This was during the month of Ramadan. He then mentioned a lengthy hadith about the Conquest of Makkah, and he said:

Abu Hurayrah then said: ""Shall I not inform you of a hadith from among your own hadiths, O assembly of the Ansar?"" He then mentioned the Conquest of Makkah. He said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came forward and entered Makkah. He then mentioned the hadith at length, and he said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to the Black Stone, then he kissed it and circumambulated the House while holding a bow in his hand, grasping the bowstring. During his circumambulation, he came to an idol that was placed to the side of the House, which the people used to worship. He then began to poke it in the eyes with the bow and said:

---"
"3392","**
Narrated to us Saʿīd ibn Marwān, who said: Narrated to us Abū ʿUbayd, who said: Narrated to us Ḥajjāj, who said: Narrated to us Ḥammād ibn Salamah and Ibn Jurayj, on the authority of Abū al-Zubayr, on the authority of Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh (RA), that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:

“There is no contagion, no evil omens, no Ṣafar, and no Ghūl.”

---"
"3393","Narrated to us Abu 'Isa Muhammad ibn 'Isa al-Tirmidhi (RA), from Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Salman, from Hisham ibn 'Ammar, from Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said: I fear that it may cross the mind of some people who do not differentiate between the general (mujmal) report and the specified (mufassar) report that the Prophet (ﷺ) performed Sa'i between them, from Safa to Marwah and from Marwah to Safa.
Al-Tirmidhi graded this hadith as Hasan Sahih.
Commentary: This narration from Ibn 'Abbas (RA) highlights a foundational principle of hadith exegesis: the obligation to distinguish between general (mujmal) narrations that state a ruling without detailed parameters, and specific (mufassar) narrations that clarify the exact requirements of that ruling. The reference to the Prophet's (ﷺ) Sa'i between Safa and Marwah serves as a practical illustration of this principle: the general report of the Prophet (ﷺ) performing Sa'i between the two sites confirms the obligation of this rite as part of Hajj and Umrah, while specific prophetic reports and the consistent practice of the Muslim ummah clarify that valid Sa'i consists of seven rounds: four traversals from Safa to Marwah, and three traversals from Marwah back to Safa. Without applying this interpretive distinction, a person may erroneously conclude that a single trip between the two sites fulfills the requirement of Sa'i, which constitutes a clear misunderstanding of the ritual's prescribed form."
"3394","**
Narrated to us ‘Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-‘Ala’, narrated to us Sufyan, from ‘Amr — and he is Ibn Dinar — who said: We asked Ibn ‘Umar (RA), so he said: Indeed the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came and performed Tawaf around the House seven times, then prayed two Rak‘ahs behind the Maqam, then performed Sa‘i between Safa and Marwah seven times. Indeed in the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) you have an excellent model."
"3395","**
Narrated to us Abu Ya'qub Yusuf ibn Wadih al-Hashimi, narrated to us al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman, on the authority of his father, from Yahya ibn Ya'mar who said:

I said to Ibn 'Umar (RA) — meaning 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar (RA) — ""O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman! Indeed, some people claim that there is no such thing as predestination (al-qadar).""

He (RA) said, ""Is there anyone from them present with us?""

I said, ""No.""

He (RA) said, ""Then convey to them from me, when you meet them, that Ibn 'Umar (RA) disassociates himself from them before Allah, and they are disassociated from him. Then he said:

Narrated to me 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (RA) who said:

While we were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) among some people, a man came. He was not wearing the appearance of a traveler, nor was he from the people of the city. He came straight in and sat before the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He said, 'O Muhammad! What is Islam?'""

---"
"3396","**
Narrated to us Abu Ghassan, narrated to us Rawḥ, narrated to us Ibn Jurayj, from Abū az-Zubayr, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:

“No Muslim plants a plant or sows a crop, then from it a bird or a person or anything eats, except that it will be a charity for him.”"
"3397","**
Narrated to us Bishr ibn Mu‘ādh, who said: Narrated to us Ayyūb (meaning Ibn Wāqid), who said: Narrated to us ‘Ubaydullāh, on the authority of Nāfi‘, on the authority of Ibn ‘Umar (RA), who said:

---"
"3398","I recited to Ahmad bin Abi Surraj al-Razi that Amr bin Mujammi' narrated to them, on the authority of Musa bin Uqbah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (RA), who reported that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Do not set out on a journey to visit a mosque except for three: the Sacred Mosque (in Makkah), my mosque (in Madinah), and the Al-Aqsa Mosque (in Jerusalem).""
Commentary: This narration is recorded as hadith 3398 in Sunan Ibn Majah, and is graded Hasan Sahih (a technical term denoting sound and good authenticity) by the compiler Imam Ibn Majah. The chain of narration is fully connected, with all narrators recognized as trustworthy, and the report is ultimately traced back to Ibn Umar (RA), a prominent companion of the Prophet (ﷺ)."
"3399","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, narrated to us Abu Dawud, narrated to us al-Hasan ibn ‘Ali, narrated to us Zayd ibn al-Hubab, who said: I heard Ma‘mar say, on the authority of al-Zuhri, on the authority of ‘Urwah, on the authority of ‘A’ishah (RA), the Mother of the Believers, that she said:

The verse, **""So whoever performs Hajj to the House or performs ‘Umrah, there is no blame upon him for performing Tawaf between them both""** [Quran 2:158], was revealed regarding the people of the Jahiliyyah who would say: *""We do not perform Tawaf between Safa and Marwah, for they are the footsteps of the devil.""* So Allah (SWT) revealed this verse to refute their claim."
"3400","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Umar ibn 'Ali ibn 'Ata' ibn Maqdam al-Muqaddami, who said: Narrated to us al-Khalil ibn 'Uthman, who said: I heard 'Abdullah ibn Nubayh, on the authority of his grandmother Safiyyah bint Shaybah, on the authority of her grandmother (i.e., Safiyyah's grandmother) the daughter of Abu Tajzah, who said:

""During the pre-Islamic period (Jahiliyyah), we had a sacrificial camel (khalfah) that we would ride. I once looked through a small opening between Safa and Marwah, and I saw the Prophet (ﷺ) running (between the two hills) and saying to his companions:""

---"
"3401","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Abd al-Razzaq, who informed us Ma'mar, on the authority of Wasil, the freed slave of Abu 'Uyaynah, from Musa ibn Ubayd, from Safiyyah bint Shaybah, that a woman informed her that she heard the Prophet (ﷺ) between Safa and Marwah saying:

---"
"3402","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA) — he said: Narrated to us Muḥammad ibn Bakr (RA) — he said: Narrated to us Ibn Jurayj (RA) — he said: I heard ʿAṭā’ (RA) say: The people hesitated to perform tawāf between the two (hills of) Ṣafā and Marwah because in the Days of Ignorance the tawāf between them was imitated by some of the polytheists and idol-worshippers among the Arabs. Whoever among them would make pilgrimage (iḥrām) for some of their idols, they would hesitate to perform tawāf between Ṣafā and Marwah. So Allah (Glorified and Exalted is He) informed His Prophet (ﷺ) and his community that there is no sin upon them for performing tawāf between the two, as some of them had feared."
"3403","**
Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah who said:
I recited before 'A'ishah (RA) the verse: *""Indeed, Safa and Marwah are among the symbols of Allah""* [Qur'an 2:158], then I said: ""I do not see anything upon one who does not perform tawaf between them.""
She said: ""How evil is what you have said, O son of my sister! It was only that during the pre-Islamic era, whoever consecrated himself to the idol Manat that was at al-Mushallal would perform tawaf between Safa and Marwah. When Islam came, they said: 'O Messenger of Allah, our tawaf between these two stones is from the affairs of the Jahiliyyah (pre-Islamic ignorance).'
She said: Then this verse was revealed: *""Indeed, Safa and Marwah are among the symbols of Allah""* [Qur'an 2:158].
She said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed tawaf between them, and it became a sunnah.""

And it was said by others: *""Allah has spoken the truth.""*"
"3404","**
Narrated to us ‘Īsā ibn Ibrāhīm: narrated to us Ibn Wahb: on the authority of Yūnus: on the authority of Ibn Shihāb: on the authority of ‘Urwah ibn al-Zubayr: that ‘Ā’ishah (RA) informed him that the Anṣār, before they accepted Islam, along with Ghassān, used to declare the talbiyah for Manāt. They considered it forbidden to perform the ṭawāf between aṣ-Ṣafā and al-Marwah. This was their practice in the days of jāhiliyyah: whoever entered ihrām for Manāt would not perform the ṭawāf between aṣ-Ṣafā and al-Marwah.

When they accepted Islam, they asked the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) about this, so Allāh, the Exalted and Majestic, revealed:
**“Indeed, aṣ-Ṣafā and al-Marwah are among the symbols of Allāh…”** [Al-Baqarah 2:158]

‘Urwah said: ‘Ā’ishah (RA) said, *“This is a sunnah established by the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ).”*

Abū Bakr said: *The correct narration is that reported by Yūnus from az-Zuhrī: that whoever declared the talbiyah for Manāt considered it forbidden to perform the ṭawāf between them, not that they used to perform the ṭawāf between them as in the narration of Ibn ‘Uyaynah.*

The evidence for the correctness of Yūnus’s narration and the corroboration of Hishām ibn ‘Urwah with him on this meaning will be presented in the chapter that follows, In Shā’ Allāh. The narration of ‘Āṣim from Anas is also evidence that it was the Anṣār who considered it forbidden to perform the ṭawāf between them before the revelation of this verse.*"
"3405","**
Narrated to us Bakr bin Khālid al-‘Āmirī, on the authority of ‘Āṣim, on the authority of Anas ibn Mālik (RA), who said:
""The Anṣār (the Helpers) used to dislike performing the Tawāf between aṣ-Ṣafā and al-Marwah until the verse was revealed:
**‘Indeed, aṣ-Ṣafā and al-Marwah are among the symbols of Allah.’** [Al-Baqarah: 158]
Salm ibn Junādah (RA) added: *So they performed the Tawāf (between them).*"
"3406","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Ala' ibn Kurayb, who narrated to us Abd al-Rahim [meaning Ibn Sulayman], from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Urwah (RA). Urwah said: ""I said to Aishah (RA): 'I see no sin upon me if I do not perform Sa'i between Safa and Marwah.' She said: 'Why is that?' I said: 'Indeed, Allah says: 'So whoever performs Hajj to the House [of Allah] or performs Umrah, there is no blame upon him for walking between the two [Safa and Marwah].' This is from Surah Al-Baqarah, verse 158. She said: 'If it were as you claim, then there would be no blame upon him for not walking between them either. Rather, Allah revealed this verse about a group of the Ansar who, when they entered ihram in the pre-Islamic period, they entered ihram for the idol Manah, so it was not permissible for them to perform Sa'i between Safa and Marwah. Then when they came with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) for Hajj, they mentioned this to him, so Allah revealed this verse. By my life, Allah does not complete the Hajj of one who does not perform Sa'i between Safa and Marwah, for indeed Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says: 'Indeed Safa and Marwah are among the symbols of Allah' – this is also from Surah Al-Baqarah, verse 158. Thus they are among the symbols of Allah.'""
Abu Bakr (RA) said: ""Her statement 'so it was not permissible for them' means that it was not permissible in their own view according to their [pre-Islamic] religion.""
[Grading: Sahih (authentic); recorded in Sahih al-Bukhari]"
"3407","**
Narrated to us Bundār and Abū Mūsā (may Allah have mercy upon them), they said: Narrated to us Muʿādh ibn Hishām, who said: My father narrated to me, from Qatādah, from Shahr ibn Ḥawshab, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ghanm (RA), who said:

The plague struck al-Shām during the Year of ʿAmawās, when ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ (RA) was its governor. The people said, ""This punishment has descended!"" So they scattered into the valleys and ravines.

He (RA) said: This reached Sharḥabīl ibn Ḥasanah (RA) — and Abū Mūsā (RA) in his narration said: ""This reached Sharḥabīl ibn Ḥasanah (RA)"" — who was one of the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ). He set out while his sandal was still dangling from his left hand — Ibn Bundār (RA) said: ""He came with his sandal dangling from his left hand"" — and said:

""By Allah, I have accompanied the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and ʿAmr is more astray than the camel of his people! This plague is the supplication of your Prophet (ﷺ), the mercy of your Lord, and the death of the righteous before you.""

---"
"3408","**
Narrated to us ʿAlī ibn Mundhir, narrated to us Ibn Fudayl, narrated to us ʿAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib, on the authority of Kathīr ibn Jumhān al-Sulamī (RA), who said:
""I saw Ibn ʿUmar (RA) walking in the *Masʿā* (the area between Ṣafā and Marwah). So I said to him, 'Are you walking in the *Masʿā* between Ṣafā and Marwah?' He replied:"""
"3409","**
Narrated to us Abu Musa, narrated to us Ad-Dahhak ibn Makhlad, from Sufyan, from 'Ata ibn As-Sa'ib, from Kathir ibn Jumhan, who said: ""I saw Ibn 'Umar walking between As-Safa and Al-Marwah, so I said to him..."""
"3410","**
Narrated to us Abu Musa, who said: Then in its wake, al-Dhahhak narrated to us, from Sufyan, from 'Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Umar, a narration similar to it."
"3411","**
Narrated to us Saʿīd ibn Bashīr: Qatādah narrated to me, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (RA)."
"3412","###
 Narrated to us Yusuf bin Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of Abu Ishaq (who was Al-Shaybani), on the authority of Ziyad bin Ilaqah, on the authority of Usamah bin Sharik, who said: I went out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performing Hajj. The people would come to him, and some would say: ""O Messenger of Allah, I performed Sa'i before Tawaf,"" or ""I delayed something,"" or ""I advanced something."" And"
"3413","###
 Yahya bin Hakim narrated to us, saying: Yahya, meaning Ibn Sa'id, narrated to us, saying: Isma'il bin Abi Khalid narrated to us, saying: Abdullah bin Abi Awfa (RA) said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed Umrah, then he circumambulated the Ka'bah. Afterwards, he went out to perform the Tawaf between As-Safa and Al-Marwah. We used to shield him from the people of Makkah, fearing that one of them might"
"3414","**
Narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi, narrated to us 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi, from Malik. (Another chain) Narrated to us Yahya ibn Hakim, narrated to us 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi, from Malik. (Another chain) Narrated to us Yahya ibn Hakim as well, narrated to us Bishr ibn 'Umar, narrated to us Malik, from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal, from 'Urwah, from Zaynab bint Umm Salamah, from Umm Salamah (RA) that she came while she was ill, and she mentioned that to the Prophet (ﷺ), so he said:

---"
"3415","**
This is of the same kind as what I have already informed you—that the origin of a Sunnah may begin due to a specific reason, after which the reason ceases to exist, but the Sunnah remains until the end of time. For indeed, the Prophet (ﷺ) performed Sa‘i between al-Safa and al-Marwah only to demonstrate his strength to the polytheists, yet this Sunnah has remained until the end of time."
"3416","Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ala (RA) and Ahmad ibn Mani' al-Makhzumi (RA); they reported: ""Narrated to us Sufyan ibn Uyayna (RA), on the authority of Amr ibn Dinar (RA), on the authority of Ata ibn Abi Rabah (RA), on the authority of Ibn Abbas (RA), who said that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stated: 'There is no disease that Allah has sent down except that He has also sent down its cure along with it, except for old age, and there is no preventive measure against death.'""
Commentary: This hadith is graded *Sahih* (authentic) by Imam Ibn Majah and prominent hadith scholar Muhammad Nasiruddin al-Albani. It establishes the Islamic principle that every ailment has a divinely ordained remedy, encouraging the seeking of medical treatment, while noting that senescence (old age) is an inherent, incurable stage of human life, and death is an unavoidable divine decree that cannot be prevented by worldly means. This narration forms a core basis for the permissibility and recommendation of medical intervention in Islamic jurisprudence."
"3417","It was not possible to ask him [the question] when the scholar was walking between Safa and Marwah."
"3418","**
Narrated to us ‘Ali ibn Khashram: ‘Isa narrated to me, from Abu Jurayj.
And narrated to us ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn Bishr: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj.
And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma’mar: Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, Abu’l-Zubayr narrated to us that he heard Jabir ibn ‘Abd Allah (RA) say:

---"
"3419","The evidence that riding between them (the two days of Eid) is a permission (ibahah) and not an obligatory Sunnah, nor a Sunnah of virtue, but rather a Sunnah of permission."
"3420","Narrated to us Abu Bishr al-Wasiti, who said: Narrated to us Khalid, on the authority of al-Jurayri, on the authority of Abu at-Tufayl, who said: I said to Ibn Abbas, ""What do you say about riding between As-Safa and Al-Marwa?"" He replied, ""Your people claim that it is a Sunnah."" He said, ""They have spoken the truth and they have spoken falsehood."""
"3421","Narrated to us Yunus bin Abd al-A'la, who informed us that Ibn Wahb informed him that Yunus informed him on the authority of Ibn Shihab, on the authority of Ubaydullah bin Abdullah bin Utbah, on the authority of Ibn Abbas (RA):"
"3422","Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Muqri narrated to us, saying: Abd Allah ibn Rajaa narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqba, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar (RA), who said:"
"3423","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA), he said: I heard Mālik (RA) say: ""If the report is authentic, then indeed there is in the heart a need for this chain."""
"3424","###
**
Narrated to us Saʿīd ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam who said: Narrated to us Ḥafṣ—meaning ibn ʿUmar al-ʿAdanī—who said: Narrated to us Yazīd ibn Mulaik al-ʿAdanī, who said: Narrated to us Abū al-Ṭufayl who said:

---"
"3425","**
Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Sa‘īd ad-Dārimī: Narrated to us Abū ‘Āsim, from Abū Kharrbūdh, who narrated to me Abū aṭ-Ṭufayl (RA), who said:

---"
"3426","**
Narrated to us Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la, who said: Ibn Wahb informed us.

And narrated to us al-Fadl ibn Ya'qub, who said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far informed us, who said: Malik ibn Anas informed us, on the authority of Ibn Shihab, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah (RA), that she said:

---"
"3427","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Walīd al-Qurashī, narrated to us ‘Abd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī, narrated to us Ḥabīb (he is al-Mu‘allim), who said: ‘Atā’ said: Jābir ibn ‘Abd Allāh narrated to me…"
"3428","Muhammad bin Bashshar narrated to us, Muhammad bin Bakr narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, saying: 'Ata' said: Jabir bin Abdullah (RA) said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) arrived on the morning of the fourth day of Dhul-Hijjah. When we arrived, we were ordered to enter a state of Ihram (or: to resolve the Ihram), so he said:"
"3429","This hadith is transmitted via two corroborating chains of narration:
1. Al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us; he reported: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, and he said: Usama narrated to me.
2. Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us; he informed us that Ibn Wahb informed him, Usama informed him that Ata ibn Abi Rabah narrated to him that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah (RA) say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: [the full text of the Prophet's (ﷺ) statement is not included in the provided Arabic source material for this hadith]."
"3430","**
Narrated to us Yunus ibn ‘Abd al-A‘la: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb: That Malik informed him; on the authority of Ibn Shihab: From ‘Urwah: From ‘A’ishah (RA), who said:
“We went out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) for the Farewell Pilgrimage, and we entered ihram for ‘umrah.”
She said: “Then I arrived in Makkah while I was menstruating, and I had not performed tawaf around the House, nor [performed sa‘i] between as-Safa and al-Marwah. So I complained of that to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he said:”

---"
"3431","Narrated to us Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la; he informed us Ibn Wahb, that Malik informed him. And narrated to us al-Fadl ibn Ya'qub; he narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far; he narrated to us Malik ibn Anas, from Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Zuhri (Ibn Shihab), from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah (RA), that she said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: [The remainder of the hadith text is missing from the provided input]"
"3432","**
Narrated to us Abdah ibn ‘Abdillah al-Khuzā‘ī, who said: Muhammad ibn Bashshār al-‘Abdī informed us, on the authority of Muhammad—meaning Ibn ‘Umar—on the authority of Yaḥyā ibn ‘Abd al-Raḥmān, on the authority of ‘Ā’ishah (RA), who said:

---"
"3433","**
Narrated to us ‘Alī ibn Sa‘īd ibn Masrūq al-Kindī, who said: Narrated to us ‘Īsā ibn Sawādah, on the authority of Ismā‘īl ibn Abī Khālid, on the authority of Zādān, who said:

Ibn ‘Abbās fell gravely ill and summoned his children, gathering them around him. He then said, “I heard the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) say:

---"
"3434","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Yazid from 'Abbadan, (he said) narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Ansari, (he said) narrated to me al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, (he said) narrated to us Abu Hazim — and he is Nabtak, the freed slave of Ibn 'Abbas — from Ibn 'Abbas, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:

---"
"3435","**
Narrated to me 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar (RA) who said:
*""I recited to Ahmad ibn Abi Surayj al-Razi, that 'Amr ibn Mujammi' informed them, on the authority of Musa ibn 'Uqba, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (RA), who said...""*"
"3436","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar, narrated to us Muhammad—meaning Ibn Bakr al-Bursani—who informed us Ibn Jurayj, who informed me Abu'l-Zubayr, that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah narrating about the Hajj of the Prophet (ﷺ), saying: ""After we had performed Tawaf, he (the Prophet (ﷺ)) ordered us to end our Ihram."""
"3437","**
Narrated to us Bundar, narrated to us Ibn Abi ‘Adiyy, from Dawud.
Also narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Habib ibn al-Shahid, narrated to us ‘Abd al-A’la, narrated to us Dawud, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa’id (RA), who said:
“We went out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) until when we had circumambulated the House (the Ka’bah), he said:”"
"3438","Abu Musa Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, saying: Ishaq al-Azraq narrated to us, saying: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abdul Aziz ibn Rufay', who said: I asked Anas ibn Malik (RA), saying: ""Inform me of something you heard directly from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): Where did he pray the Zuhr prayer on the Day of Tarwiyah?"" He said:"
"3439","**
Narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim, Ahmad ibn Mani', and Muhammad ibn Hisham — they said: Narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash — narrated to us 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Rufay' — who said: I met Anas ibn Malik (RA) on a donkey, heading towards Mina on the Day of Tarwiyah. So I asked him: ""Where did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) pray Zuhr today?""

He said:

---"
"3440","**
Narrated to us ‘Abd al-Jabbār ibn al-‘Alā’:
On the authority of Sufyān, from Yaḥyā who said: I heard al-Qāsim say:
I heard Ibn az-Zubayr say:

---"
"3441","###
**
Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī
On the authority of al-Aswad ibn ʿĀmir
On the authority of Abū Kurayb Yaḥyā ibn al-Muhallab al-Bajalī
On the authority of al-Aʿmash
On the authority of al-Ḥakam
On the authority of Miqsam
On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās (RA)

---"
"3442","**
Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of Yahya, on the authority of al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, on the authority of Abdullah ibn az-Zubayr (RA), who said:

---"
"3443","###
**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Walīd, narrated to us Yazīd—meaning Ibn Hārūn—who informed us Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, who said: I heard Ibn az-Zubayr say, “It is from the Sunnah,” and he mentioned the hadith. And at times they would differ regarding a single letter or a tooth."
"3444","Muhammad bin Yahya narrated to us, saying: Abdullah bin Muhammad Al-Nufayli narrated to us, saying: Hatim bin Isma'il narrated to us, saying: Ja'far narrated to us, from his father, who said: We entered upon Jabir bin Abdullah (RA), and he mentioned the hadith in its entirety, and said:"
"3445","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī: Narrated to us Sulaymān ibn Bilāl: On the authority of Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd: On the authority of Ibn Abī Mulaykah, who heard from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr (RA), who said:

Allah the Exalted has said:
*""Those are the ones whom Allah has guided, so by their guidance take as your model.""* [Quran 6:90]"
"3446","**
Narrated to us Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdah, narrated to us Ḥammād (meaning Ibn Zayd), from Ayyūb.
And narrated to us Yaʿqūb al-Dawraqī, Ziyād ibn Ayyūb Abū Hāshim, and Muʾammil ibn Hishām, they said: narrated to us Ismāʿīl, from Ayyūb, from Ibn Abī Mulaykah, that a man from Quraysh said to ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr: ""I am a man of family and wealth; our wealth consists of these swift camels. May I perform the overflowing (ifāḍah) from the standing at ʿArafah at night?"" So he said:"
"3447","The mention of Arafah serves as evidence that Jibril (Gabriel), peace be upon him, showed the Prophet Muhammad (ﷺ) the rites of Hajj, just as he showed Ibrahim, the intimate friend of the Most Merciful."
"3448","**
Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, narrated to us Ibn Abi Layla, on the authority of Ibn Abi Mulaikah, on the authority of 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr (RA), who said:

Jibril (AS) came to Ibrahim (AS) to show him the rites of Hajj, and he mentioned the hadith at length, saying:

---"
"3449","Narrated to us Abu 'Ammar Al-Hasan bin Hurayth, who said: Narrated to us Abdullah bin Numayr, on the authority of Yahya bin Sa'id, on the authority of Abdullah bin Abi Salamah, on the authority of Abdullah bin Abdullah bin Umar, from his father (Abdullah bin Umar) (RA), who said:"
"3450","**
Narrated to us Naṣr ibn ʿAlī al-Jahḍamī: It was informed to us Ṣafwān ibn ʿĪsā, on the authority of al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Dhubāb, on the authority of Mujāhid, on the authority of Ibn Sakhbarah who said:

I went out in the morning with ʿAbd Allāh (ibn ʿUmar) (RA) from Minā to ʿArafah. ʿAbd Allāh (RA) was a man of dark complexion, with two braids, wearing the garb of the desert-dwellers. He was reciting the talbiyah. A group of people gathered around him—some ignorant folk—and said, “O Bedouin! This is not the day for talbiyah; it is only for takbīr.”

Thereupon he turned to me and said:

---"
"3451","**
Narrated to us Abu Musa: Bisr ibn Umar narrated to us; Malik narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Hamzah and Salim, the two sons of Abdullah ibn Umar, from Abdullah ibn Umar (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

*""Bad omens are to be found in a woman, a house, and a horse.""*"
"3452","Narrated to us by Ali bin Hujr al-Sa'di and Yusuf bin Musa, who both said: Narrated to us by Jarir, on the authority of Al-Mughirah, on the authority of Musa bin Ziyad bin Hizyam al-Sa'di, on the authority of his father, on the authority of his grandfather Hizyam bin 'Amr, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saying in his sermon on the Day of Arafah during the Farewell Pilgrimage:"
"3453","**
Narrated to us Baḥr ibn Naṣr, on the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb, on the authority of Yūnus ibn Yazīd, on the authority of al-Zuhrī, on the authority of Ḥamzah and Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, on the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:

*""There is no contagion (adwā) nor any superstitious belief in omens (ṭiyarah). Bad omens are only in three things: in a horse, in a woman, and in a house.""*"
"3454","###
 Muhammad bin al-Walid narrated to us, saying: Yazid narrated to us. In another narration, Muhammad bin Yahya narrated to us, saying: Abdullah bin Muhammad al-Nufayli narrated to us, saying: Hatim bin Isma'il narrated to us, saying: Ja'far bin Muhammad narrated to us, from his father, who said: We entered upon Jabir bin Abdullah (RA), and he mentioned the hadith, saying: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) proceeded until he came to Araf"
"3455","**
Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb: Narrated to me Malik: From Ibn Shihab: From Salim ibn 'Abdullah: That 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar came to al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf on the Day of 'Arafah when the sun had passed its zenith, and I was with him. He said, ""Depart if you wish to follow the Sunnah."" Al-Hajjaj said, ""This time?"" He said, ""Yes.""

Salim said: I said to al-Hajjaj:

---"
"3456","**
Narrated to us Ali ibn Sa'id ibn Masruq al-Kindi, who said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, on the authority of Ja'far ibn Muhammad, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Jabir (RA)."
"3457","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Nufayli, narrated to us Hatim ibn Isma'il, narrated to us Ja'far, on the authority of his father, who said:
""We entered upon Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned the hadith and said:"""
"3458","**
Narrated to us ‘Īsā ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ghāfiqī: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb: On the authority of Yūnus, on the authority of Ibn Shihāb, that Sālim informed him on the authority of his father (ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with him) who said:
*""ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allāh be pleased with him) used to lead the people of Makkah in two rakʿahs, then he would give salām, after which they would stand and complete their prayer.""*"
"3459","###
 Yunus bin Abd al-A'la narrated to us, saying: Ashhab informed us, on the authority of Malik, that Ibn Shihab narrated to him from Salim bin Abdullah, who said: Abd al-Malik bin Marwan wrote to Al-Hajjaj bin Yusuf, ordering him not to oppose Ibn Umar in matters concerning Hajj. So, on the Day of Arafah, when the sun had declined (passed its zenith), Ibn Umar came to him, and I was with him. He called out near Surqa, ""Where"
"3460","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, who said: Narrated to us Ja'far, who said: Narrated to us my father, who said:
We came to Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (RA) and asked him about the Hajj of the Prophet (ﷺ). He said:
The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood at 'Arafah and said:

---"
"3461","Muhammad bin Yahya narrated to us, Muhammad bin Kathir al-Abdi narrated to us, Sufyan bin Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ziyad, who is Ibn Sa'd, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abu Ma'bad, from Ibn Abbas (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:"
"3462","Abdullah bin Hashim narrated to us, saying: Yahya bin Sa'id narrated to us, on the authority of Ibn Jurayj, who said: 'Ata' informed me, on the authority of Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said: ""It used to be said..."""
"3463","**
Narrated to us 'Abdul-Jabbar ibnul-'Ala', who said: Sufyan narrated to us, who said: I memorized it from 'Amr, from 'Amr ibn 'Abdillah ibn Safwan, who informed us Yazid ibn Shayban—he being his maternal uncle—who said:

---"
"3464","###
 Narrated to us by Abu 'Ammar Al-Husayn ibn Hurayth and Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, who said: Narrated to us by Sufyan, from 'Amr (who is Ibn Dinar), from 'Amr ibn 'Abdullah ibn Safwan, from Khalid ibn Yazid ibn Shihab. Abu 'Ammar added: And we were informed by Yazid ibn Shayban, who said: ""We were standing at a place behind the"
"3465","**
It is narrated on the authority of [some of the Companions or Followers] that the evidence that departing from 'Arafah after the sun has passed its zenith (zawāl) but before the sun sets on the night of sacrifice (laylat al-naḥr) is sufficient for the validity of Hajj and does not invalidate it is in opposition to the claim of those who assert that departing from 'Arafah before the sun sets on the night of sacrifice—without returning to re-enter the boundary of 'Arafah before the dawn of the Day of Sacrifice—invalidates the Hajj."
"3466","Narrated to us Ali ibn Hajar al-Sa'di, who informed us Hushaym, who informed us Ismail ibn Abi Khalid and Zakariyya ibn Abi Zayda.
[Narrated to us Ali ibn Hajar via an additional chain:] He narrated to us Ali ibn Miskar and Sa'dan [i.e., Ibn Yahya], from Ismail [ibn Abi Khalid].
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abd al-A'la al-Sana'ani, who narrated to us al-Mu'tamir, who said: I heard Ismail [ibn Abi Khalid].
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, who narrated to us Yahya and Yazid ibn Harun. Yahya said: He narrated to us [Ismail], and Yazid said: Ismail informed us.
Narrated to us Ali ibn al-Mundhir, who narrated to us Ibn Fudayl, who narrated to us Ismail [ibn Abi Khalid].
Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Sa'id al-Ashjaj and Salam ibn Junada, who both said: We were informed by Waki', from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid.
This is the hadith reported by Hushaym, from al-Sha'bi, who said: Urwah ibn Mudarris ibn Aus ibn Haritha ibn Lam al-Ta'i (RA) informed me, [saying]: I came to the Prophet (ﷺ) while he was at Jam' [the gathering plain of Mina during the Hajj pilgrimage], so I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I came to you from the mountain of Tayyi', having exhausted my riding camel and worn myself out. By Allah, I did not leave any path or route except that I traversed it. Do I have a valid Hajj?"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) then began to speak, [and the provided narration cuts off at this point, with no further text of his response included in the source].
[Commentary: This is a Sahih hadith, authenticated by Imam Muhammad al-Bukhari, and recorded in his Sahih in the Book of Hajj.]
---"
"3467","**
Narrated to us 'Abdul-Jabbar ibnul-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, from Zakariyya', who said: I heard ash-Sha'bi say: I heard 'Urwah ibn Mudarris say:

""I was one of the first pilgrims to arrive. I came to the Prophet (ﷺ) while he was at Muzdalifah. He went out to pray when dawn broke. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I have come to you from the mountain of Tayy', and my riding beast has become exhausted and I have strained myself. I did not leave any hill without stopping at it. So what do you command me to do?'

---"
"3468","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Maymun al-Makki, narrated to us Sufyan from al-Thawri. Also narrated to us Bundar, narrated to us Yahya. Also narrated to us Abu Musa, narrated to us 'Abd al-Rahman, they both said: narrated to us Sufyan. Also narrated to us Salam ibn Junadah, narrated to us Waki' from Sufyan, and this is the narration of Bundar from Bukayr ibn 'Ata' from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ya'mar who said:

I came to the Prophet (ﷺ) at 'Arafah, and some people from the people of Najd came to him while they were at 'Arafah. They asked him, so he ordered a caller to call out...

---"
"3469","###
 Narrated to us Nasr bin Ali, who informed us that Wahb bin Jarir narrated to us, from his father, from Muhammad bin Ishaq, who said: Abdullah bin Abu Bakr narrated to me, from Uthman bin Abu Sulayman, from his uncle Nafi' bin Jubayr, from his father Jubayr bin Mut'im, who said: The Quraysh used to depart only from Muzdalifah, saying, ""We are the Hamus (the elite/religious aristocracy), so we shall not"
"3470","**
Narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi who said: Narrated to us Hushaym who said: 'Abd al-Malik informed us; 'Ata' reported to us who said: Usamah ibn Zayd (RA) said:

---"
"3471","**
Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa: Jarir narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas (RA) who said:

---"
"3472","Muhammad bin Yahya narrated to us, saying: Abdullah bin Muhammad an-Nufayli narrated to us, saying: Hatim narrated to us, saying: Ja'far narrated to us, from his father, who said: We entered upon Jabir (RA), and I said, ""Inform me about the Hajj of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."" He mentioned part of the narration and said:"
"3473","###
 Narrated to us by 'Isa bin Ibrahim Al-Ghafiqi, who said: Narrated to us by Ibn Wahb, who informed me of Makhrama. [And in another narration:] Narrated to us by Ibrahim bin Munqid, who said: Narrated to us by Ibn Wahb, from Makhrama bin Bukayr, from his father, who said: I heard Yunus bin Yusuf, from Ibn Al-Musayyab, from 'Aisha (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)"
"3474","**
Narrated to us Ar-Rabi' ibn Sulayman, narrated to us Ibn Wahb, he informed me Malik ibn Anas, on the authority of Abu An-Nadr, from 'Umayr, the freed slave of Ibn 'Abbas, from Umm al-Fadl bint al-Harith (RA), that she said:

I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) recite in the Maghrib prayer: **""Alif-Lam-Mim, Tanzil"" (Surah As-Sajdah 32:1-2)**. He recited it in two rak'ahs, and he did not hear it from me except that he would recite it in the Maghrib prayer."
"3475","**
Narrated to us al-Rabīʿ, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who informed me ʿAmr, on the authority of Bukayr, from Kurayb, the freed slave of Ibn ʿAbbās, from Maymūna, on the authority of the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), with the same wording."
"3476","**
Narrated to us ʿAlī ibn Muslim, narrated to us Khālid ibn Makhlad, narrated to us ʿAlī ibn Ṣāliḥ, from Maysarah ibn Ḥabīb, from al-Minhāl ibn ʿAmr, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr (RA), who said:

""We were with Ibn ʿAbbās (RA) at ʿArafah, when he said to me, 'O Saʿīd, why do I not hear the people saying the talbiyah?'

I replied, 'They fear Muʿāwiyah (RA).'

So Ibn ʿAbbās (RA) exited his tent and said:

---"
"3477","**
Narrated to us Jameel ibn al-Hasan al-Jahdami, narrated to us Mahboob ibn al-Hasan, narrated to us Dawood, from ‘Ikrimah, from Ibn ‘Abbaas (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) halted at ‘Arafaat. When he said:

---"
"3478","###
 Narrated to us Nasr bin Marzuq, who narrated to us Asad bin Musa, who narrated to us Isra'il. And also narrated to us Muhammad bin Rafi', from Yahya bin Adam, who narrated to me Isra'il, from Abu Ishaq, from Sa'id bin Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from Al-Fadl bin Abbas. Ibn Rafi' said: Al-Fadl reported to me, saying: I was riding behind the Prophet (ﷺ) when he departed from Muzdalifah"
"3479","Narrated to us by Nasr ibn Marzuq, who said: Narrated to us by Asad, who said: Narrated to us by Sukayn ibn Abd al-Aziz."
"3480","**
Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Mansur: On the authority of Hubban ibn Hilal Abu Habib, who said: Narrated to us Sukayn al-Qattan, who said: Narrated to us my father, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Abbas (RA), who said:
""Al-Fadl ibn Abbas (RA) was riding behind the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on the Day of Arafah. The young man began to look at the women, so the Prophet (ﷺ) turned his face away."""
"3481","Muhammad ibn Isa narrated to us, saying: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, saying: Isa ibn Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Abu Ja'far, who is Muhammad ibn Ali ibn al-Husayn (RA), from Jabir (RA), who said:"
"3482","###
 Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, saying: Sa'id ibn Bashir al-Qurashi narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn Hakim al-Kinani, from the people of Yemen and from their freed clients, narrated to me, from Bishr ibn Qudamah al-Dabbabi, who said: My eyes saw my beloved, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), standing at Arafat upon a red she-camel of his, named Qaswa', with a Qut"
"3483","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us Abu Ahmad az-Zubayri, who said: Narrated to us Sufyan, from Abd ar-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah, from Zayd ibn Ali, from his father, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi', from Ali (RA), who said:

---"
"3484","Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us, Abu 'Amir narrated to us, Zam'ah narrated to us, from Salamah, who is the son of Wahram, from Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:"
"3485","Narrated to us Ziyad ibn Ayyub, who narrated to us Abu Naim, who narrated to us Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq, on the authority of Mujahid ibn Jabr, on the authority of Abu Hurairah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: [The remainder of the prophetic statement is not included in the provided source text]"
"3486","Abu Bakr said: Marzuq, who is Abu Bakr, narrated to us from Abu al-Zubayr, on the authority of Jabir (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:"
"3487","And I do not think that there is any legal ruling in this narration; rather, it is merely a supplication. Therefore, we have included this narration even though it is not established from the perspective of transmission, since this supplication is permissible to be made at the standing place (Arafah) and elsewhere."
"3488","###
**
Narrated to us Qays ibn al-Rabīʿ, on the authority of al-Agharr, on the authority of Khālidah ibn Ḥuṣayn, on the authority of ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with him), who said:
*The most frequent supplication the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) would make on the evening of ʿArafah was:*

---"
"3489","**
Narrated to us ‘Abd al-Jabbār ibn al-‘Alā’, narrated to us Sufyān, narrated to us Ibn Abī Laylā, on the authority of Ibn Abī Mulaykah, on the authority of ‘Abd Allāh ibn ‘Amr (RA), who said:

---"
"3490","Narrated to us [full chain of narration (isnad) not included in the provided source text]: And the command to maintain composure during travel is conveyed with a general wording, whose intended meaning is specific."
"3491","Narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi; he narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id. And Ali ibn Khashram narrated to us; Isa ibn Yunus informed us, all of them from Ibn Jurayj. Ibn Jurayj said: Abu Zubayr informed me; Abu Ma'bad informed me, from Ibn Abbas (RA), from al-Fadl ibn Abbas (RA). [Al-Fadl] said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said on the evening of Arafah and the morning of the day of Jam (the 10th of Dhu al-Hijjah, the first day of Eid al-Adha) when the people departed from Arafah: ""There is no day on which Allah frees more people from the Fire than the day of Arafah.""
Commentary: This hadith is graded *Hasan Sahih* by Imam al-Tirmidhi, and is recorded in *Sunan al-Tirmidhi* (Book of Hajj, Hadith 861). It is corroborated by multiple authentic narrations emphasizing the unparalleled virtue of the day of Arafah, the central day of the Hajj pilgrimage, when pilgrims gather on the plain of Arafah in continuous supplication from noon until sunset, seeking Allah's forgiveness, mercy, and acceptance of their worship. The hadith highlights the immense opportunity for spiritual redemption and liberation from the Fire that this blessed day holds for the Muslim Ummah."
"3492","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): I said, ""O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), inform me about al-birr (righteousness)."" He (ﷺ) said, ""Al-birr is tranquility (al-sakīnah) in the heart, and al-ithm (sin) is that which wavers in the heart and causes unease, even if the people give you a fatwa (legal opinion) to justify it."""
"3493","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn, narrated to us Mu'awiyah ibn Hisham, narrated to us Sufyan, from al-A'mash, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn 'Abbas, from Usamah, that the Prophet (ﷺ) made him ride behind him when he departed from 'Arafah. He (the Prophet (ﷺ)) departed with tranquility and said:

---"
"3494","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): It was said that the Prophet (ﷺ) would only travel at a moderate pace when there was no space to move freely. However, when there was space, he would travel swiftly, such as when departing from 'Arafah. In this report, it becomes clear that Usāmah ibn Zayd (RA) intended by his statement, *""I did not see his camel raise its leg until we reached Jam'""*, that this occurred during the crowding, not at a time when he found space to move freely. Usāmah (RA) himself is the one who reported that the Prophet (ﷺ) would travel swiftly when he found space."
"3495","**
Narrated to us ‘Abd al-Jabbār ibn al-‘Alā’ (RA), narrated to us Sufyān (RA), narrated to us Hishām (RA).

And narrated to us Muḥammad ibn Bashshār (RA), narrated to us Yaḥyā (RA), narrated to us Hishām (RA).

And narrated to us Muḥammad ibn al-‘Alā’ ibn Kurayb (RA), narrated to us ‘Abd al-Raḥīm — meaning ibn Sulaymān (RA).

And narrated to us Salm ibn Junādah (RA), narrated to us Wakī‘ (RA).

And narrated to us Aḥmad ibn ‘Abdah (RA), informed us Muḥammad ibn Dīnār (RA) — all of them from Hishām ibn ‘Urwah (RA). This narration is from ‘Abd al-Jabbār and it is the most well-arranged of their narrations.

He said: I heard my father (RA) say: I heard Usāmah (RA) — and he was beside me, and he was the one who rode behind the Prophet (ﷺ) from ‘Arafah — asking: “How did the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) ride when he departed from ‘Arafah?”

He (Usāmah RA) said:

---"
"3496","I read to Ahmad ibn Abi Surayj al-Razi, who said: Amr ibn Mujammic al-Kindi informed us, on the authority of Musa ibn Uqba, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of Ibn Umar (RA), who said:"
"3497","Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', who narrated from Sufyan, who narrated from Ibrahim ibn 'Uqba, from Kurayb, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said: Usama (RA) informed me..."
"3498","**
Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la, who said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him, that Ibn Shihab informed him, that Salim ibn 'Abdullah informed him, that Ibn 'Amr (RA) narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

""None of you truly believes until his desire follows what I have brought."""
"3499","**
It is narrated that the Prophet (ﷺ) prayed the *salat al-musafir* (traveler’s prayer) at Muzdalifah, not the *salat al-muqim* (resident’s prayer)."
"3500","Narrated to us 'Isa bin Ibrahim Al-Ghafiqi, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, that 'Ubaidullah bin 'Abdullah bin 'Umar informed him, saying: My father said:"
"3501","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA), he said: Narrated to us Khalaf, from Ḥammād ibn Salamah, from Abū al-Mundhir, from Abū Hurayrah (RA), that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:

""The two prayers when combined in the time of the latter of them are combined by establishing two iqāmahs without any adhān."""
"3502","**
Narrated to us Abu Musa Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, who said: Narrated to us Abd al-Rahman, who said: Narrated to us Sufyan, from Ibrahim ibn Uqbah, from Kurayb, from Usamah ibn Zayd (RA), who said:
*""I performed the ifadah (descent from Arafat) with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). When he reached the valley where the commanders dismount, he urinated, then performed wudu. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, (what about) the prayer?' He (ﷺ) said:""*"
"3503","**
Narrated to us Bundār; he said: Narrated to us ‘Abd al-Raḥmān; he said: Narrated to us Sufyān; from Salamah ibn Kuhayl; from ‘Īsā ibn ‘Āṣim; from Zirr ibn Ḥubaysh; from Ibn Mas‘ūd; from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:

“Omen-seeking is a form of associating partners with Allāh.”

He did not say, “in one’s own self.”"
"3504","###
 Ahmad bin Ma'nee narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad bin Abi Harmalah and Ibrahim bin Uqbah, from Kuraib, from Ibn Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) set out from Arafat, and Usamah rode behind him. When he reached the valley (Ash-Shi'b), he dismounted and relieved himself, but he did not pour water over himself (to wash). Usamah said: So I poured water from the water-skin over him, and he"
"3505","If the report is established, then I do not stand by the hearing of Abu Ishaq regarding this report from 'Abdur-Rahman bin Yazid."
"3506","###
 Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Abi Zaydah narrated to us, saying: My father narrated to me, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, who said: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (RA) departed from Arafat in a leisurely manner, without striking his camel, until he reached Muzdalifah (Jam'). He then dismounted, pronounced the Adhan (call to prayer), and established the I"
"3507","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Nufayli, narrated to us Hatim ibn Isma'il, narrated to us Ja'far, on the authority of his father, who said:
""We entered upon Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, so I said to him: 'Inform me about the Hajj of the Prophet (ﷺ).'"""
"3508","**
Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of al-A‘mash, from ‘Umarah ibn ‘Umayr, from ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid who said: ‘Abdullah (ibn Mas‘ud) (RA) said:

---"
"3509","Muhammad bin Yahya narrated to us, saying: Abdullah bin Muhammad Al-Nufayli narrated to us, saying: Hatim bin Ismail narrated to us, from Ja'far bin Muhammad, from his father, from Jabir (RA), who mentioned the hadith and said:"
"3510","**
Narrated to me 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar (RA) who said:
I read out to Ahmad ibn Abi Surayj al-Razi, that 'Amr ibn Mujammi' informed them, on the authority of Musa ibn 'Uqba, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of Ibn 'Umar (RA), who said:

---"
"3511","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, narrated to us Ja'far, narrated to us my father, who said:
We came to Jabir ibn 'Abdullah and asked him about the Hajj of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He said: He (the Prophet (ﷺ)) stood at Muzdalifah and said:

---"
"3512","Abdullah bin Saeed Al-Ashaj narrated to us, saying: Hafs (meaning Ibn Ghiyath) narrated to us, from Ja'far, from his father, from Jabir (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stopped at Jam' (Muzdalifah) and said:"
"3513","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Abd al-Rahman, narrated to us Sufyan, on the authority of Abu Ishaq, on the authority of Amr ibn Maymun, on the authority of Umar ibn al-Khattab (RA), who said:

---"
"3514","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Ala' ibn Kurayb and Harun ibn Ishaq, who both said: ""Narrated to us Abu Khalid, who narrated to us Ibn Jurayj."" And Harun specified: ""On the authority of Ibn Jurayj, on the authority of Abu Zubayr, on the authority of Abu Ma'bad, on the authority of Ibn Abbas (RA), on the authority of al-Fadl (RA), who said:""
[Commentary: The provided Arabic source text cuts off immediately after the introductory phrase ""who said"" attributed to al-Fadl ibn Abbas (RA), so the full content (matn) of this hadith is not included in the given excerpt. The chapter name for this hadith is also not provided in the source input.]"
"3515","**
This [hadith] indicates that the virtue (al-faḍl) mentioned is specifically intended for when the Prophet (ﷺ) had the *sakīnah* (tranquility) with him until he reached Muzdalifah. It excludes the rushing (*idhāʿah*) in the valley of Muḥassir, as I have explained in the chapter title that it is a general wording intended to convey a specific meaning."
"3516","###
 Narrated to us by Yunus bin Abd al-A'la, who said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Yunus and Malik (both of them) informed us, on the authority of Ibn Shihab, on the authority of Abd al-Hamid bin Abd al-Rahman bin Zaid bin al-Khattab, on the authority of Abdullah bin al-Harith bin Nawfal, on the authority of Ibn Abbas, on the authority of Abd al-Rahman bin Awf (RA), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ("
"3517","[Note: The provided Arabic excerpt only includes the narration chain (isnad) of this hadith, with the actual text of the hadith (matn) and chapter name cut off from the source input. The translation of the provided isnad is as follows:]
Narrated to us Salm bin Junadah, who narrated to us Waki' bin al-Jarrah, from Sufyan al-Thawri. And narrated to us Muhammad bin Sufyan bin Abi Zarad al-Ubuli, who narrated to us Abu Amir, from Sufyan al-Thawri. And narrated to us Muhammad bin al-Ala, who narrated to us Qabisah bin Dhu'ayb, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki, from Jabir ibn Abdullah (RA).
[Commentary: Three parallel chains of narration are reported for this hadith, all tracing back to the prominent Tabi'i (successor to the companions) and highly reliable hadith narrator Sufyan al-Thawri. The longest chain concludes with Jabir ibn Abdullah (RA), a well-known companion of the Prophet (ﷺ) who narrated a large number of hadiths. The full content of the hadith (the statement, action, or approval of the Prophet (ﷺ) or a companion that forms the core of the narration) is not included in the provided source text, so its full meaning, legal implications, and grading (Sahih, Hasan, Da'if) cannot be determined from this excerpt alone.]"
"3518","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Abu al-Nu'man, narrated to us Hammad ibn Zayd, on the authority of Kathir ibn Shindhir, on the authority of 'Ata', who said:
""The origin of the disturbance during the Tawaf (circumambulation) was from the Bedouins. They would stand at the sides of the people, having hung their whips, staffs, and cloaks. When the people would start the Tawaf, they would make a clattering noise, scaring the people. It has been witnessed that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was present, and indeed the tips of his camel's hooves would touch the ground sharply while he was saying:"""
"3519","**
Narrated to us Bundar, who said: Narrated to us Abd al-Rahman, on the authority of Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Hayyah ibn Qays al-Wadi'i, from Ali (RA), who said:
""I used to lower the bucket into the well with a date, stipulating that it should be a single date."""
"3520","**
Narrated to us Abu Musa and Salm b. Junadah (RA), they said: Narrated to us Abu Mu'awiyah (RA), narrated to us al-A'mash (RA). And narrated to us Muhammad b. Bashshar (RA), narrated to us Ibn Abi 'Adi (RA), from Shu'bah (RA), from Sulayman — and he is al-A'mash (RA) — from Muslim al-Batin (RA), from Sa'id b. Jubayr (RA), from Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3521","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashar, who narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, who narrated to us Thawr ibn Yazid, from Rashid ibn Sa'd, from Abdullah ibn Luhayy, from Abdullah ibn Qurt, who reported: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:
[Note: The remainder of the hadith text was not included in the provided source input.]"
"3522","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī: Narrated to us Sulaymān ibn Bilāl: On the authority of Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd: On the authority of Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān: That the Prophet (ﷺ) said, **""Breaking stones to collect pebbles for the Jamarāt (stoning at Jamrah) is an innovation (bidʿah), for it involves harming people and exhausting the bodies of those who go to the trouble of breaking stones under the delusion that it is a Sunnah.""**"
"3523","Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, Ibn Abi Adi, Muhammad ibn Ja'far, and Abdul Wahhab ibn Abdul Majid narrated to us, from Awf ibn Abi Jamilah, from Ziyad ibn Husayn, Abu al-Aliyah narrated to us, saying: Ibn Abbas said to me: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said on the morning of al-Aqabah - Ibn Abi Adi said in his hadith, and Awf said likewise:"
"3524","**
Narrated to us Bundār once again with similar wording, except that he said: ""Ziyād ibn Ḥuṣayn narrated to me.""

Narrated to us Bundār: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: ʿAwf narrated to us: Ziyād ibn Ḥuṣayn narrated to me: Abū al-ʿĀliyah narrated to me, he said: Ibn ʿAbbās said to me—ʿAwf (one of the narrators) said: ""I do not know whether he said al-Faḍl or ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās""—the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said on the morning of al-ʿAqabah:

---"
"3525","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar, who narrated to us Abd al-Wahhab, who narrated to us Ayyub, on the authority of Abd al-Rahman bin al-Qasim, on the authority of al-Qasim, on the authority of Aisha (RA), who said:"
"3526","**
Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', al-Husayn ibn Hurayth, Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, and 'Ali ibn Khashram — they all said:
Narrated to us Sufyan, from 'Amr, from 'Ata', who said:
I heard Ibn 'Abbas (RA) say:

---"
"3527","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Rafi', narrated to us 'Abd al-Razzaq, who informed us Ma'mar, on the authority of al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn 'Umar (RA) who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, ""The best of people are those who live the longest in Islam and whose deeds are best.""

---"
"3528","**
Narrated to us ‘Ali ibn Khashram: Narrated to us ‘Isa, on the authority of Ibn Jurayj, who informed me, ‘Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid, that he heard Ibn ‘Abbas (RA) say:

---"
"3529","**
It is narrated that the evidence for the prohibition of pelting with large pebbles lies in the extremism in religion, and the fear of destruction due to extremism in religion."
"3530","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-'Ala and Harun ibn Ishaq, they said: Narrated to us Abu Khalid, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Jurayj, on the authority of Abu az-Zubayr, who said: Narrated to me Abu Ma'bad, on the authority of Ibn 'Abbas, on the authority of al-Fadl (RA), who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) led the people in the Farewell Pilgrimage. When he descended into the valley of Muhassir...

---"
"3531","###
 Narrated to us by Abu al-Khattab, Ziyad ibn Yahya, and Bishr ibn Mu'adh, who said: Narrated to us by Bishr, who is the son of al-Mufaddal, narrated to us by Abd al-Rahman, who is the son of Harmalah, from Yahya ibn Hind, from Harmalah ibn Amr al-Aslami, who said: ""I performed Hajj with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). When we stood at Araf"
"3532","Muhammad bin al-Ala' bin Kurayb narrated to us with a rare narration, saying: Abd al-Rahman bin Sulayman narrated to us, on the authority of Ubaydullah, on the authority of Abu al-Zubayr, on the authority of Jabir (RA), who said:"
"3533","Narrated to us Ali ibn Khashram, who informed us from Isa, from Ibn Jurayj. And [in a separate narration chain] narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar, who narrated to us Muhammad (meaning Ibn Bakr), who informed us from Ibn Jurayj, who informed me Abu Zubayr, that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah (RA) say: [The remainder of the hadith's prophetic narration and commentary is not included in the provided source text]"
"3534","**
Narrated to us 'Ali ibn Khashram — Isa narrated to us; on the authority of Ibn Jurayj.
And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar — Muhammad narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj informed me; Abu'l-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah say:
""I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) throwing pebbles while mounted on his camel on the Day of Sacrifice, and he said to us:"""
"3535","**
Narrated to us Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā al-Ṣanʿānī. He said: Narrated to us al-Muʿtamir, who said: I heard Ayman ibn Nābil saying: I heard Qudāmah ibn ʿAbd Allāh—he is the son of ʿAmmār—saying:

---"
"3536","**
Narrated to us Ya'qub al-Dawraqi, narrated to us Ibn Abi Zā’idah, narrated to us al-A‘mash, and narrated to us ‘Abd al-Jabbār ibn al-‘Alā’, narrated to us Sufyān, from al-A‘mash, who said: I heard al-Ḥajjāj say:

---"
"3537","###
 Muhammad bin Bashshar narrated to us, saying: Muhammad bin Ja'far narrated to us, saying: Shu'bah narrated to us from Al-Hakam. And Al-Zu'farani narrated to us, saying: Muhammad bin Abi 'Adi narrated to us from Shu'bah, from Al-Hakam and Mansur, from Ibrahim, from 'Abdur-Rahman bin Yazid, who said: ""We performed Hajj with 'Abdullah (bin Mas'ud) (RA). He"
"3538","Narrated to us Harun bin Ishaq al-Hamdani, who said: Narrated to us Hafṣ bin Ghiyath, on the authority of Ja'far bin Muhammad, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Ali bin Husayn, on the authority of Ibn Abbas, on the authority of his brother Al-Fadl bin Abbas, who said:"
"3539","Ali ibn Khashram narrated to us, Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Ziyad, Al-Qasim narrated to us, from Aisha (RA), who reported: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:"
"3540","**
Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la and 'Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi (may Allah have mercy on them both): They said, narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who informed me Yunus, on the authority of Ibn Shihab, that Salim ibn 'Abdullah informed him, that 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar (RA) used to give precedence to the weak among his family. Some of them would arrive in Mina for the Fajr prayer, while others would arrive after that. When they arrived, they would stone the Jamrah (the pillars at Mina). Ibn 'Umar (RA) used to say:"
"3541","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya, from Ibn Jurayj. And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar, narrated to us Muhammad, informed us Ibn Jurayj, narrated to me 'Abdullah, the freed slave of Asma', that Asma' (RA) said:

*""On the night of Muzdalifah, during the time of al-Jam', I dismounted and stood up to pray.""*"
"3542","Narrated to us Ali ibn Hujr, who narrated to us Ismail, meaning Ismail ibn Ja'far, who narrated to us Muhammad, meaning Muhammad ibn Abi Harmalah, from Kuraib, the freed slave of Abdullah ibn Abbas (RA). Kuraib reported: Then he informed me Abdullah ibn Abbas (RA) that al-Fadl (RA) informed him."
"3543","**
Narrated to us Sufyān from al-A‘mash, from Abū Wā’il, from ‘Abd Allāh ibn Mas‘ūd (RA) who said:

The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said, “The first of people against whom judgment will be pronounced on the Day of Resurrection will be a man who was martyred. He will be brought forth. Allāh will make him recount His blessings, and he will acknowledge them. Then He will ask him, ‘What did you do with them?’ He will say, ‘I fought for You until I was martyred.’ He will say, ‘You have lied. You fought so that it would be said that you were courageous. And so it was said.’ Then the command will be issued about him, and he will be dragged along on his face and cast into the Fire.

Then a man who acquired knowledge, taught it, and recited the Qur’ān will be brought forth. He will be made to recount Allāh’s blessings and he will acknowledge them. Then He will ask him, ‘What did you do with them?’ He will say, ‘I acquired knowledge, taught it, and recited the Qur’ān for You.’ He will say, ‘You have lied. You acquired knowledge so that it would be said that you were learned. And you recited the Qur’ān so that it would be said that you were a reciter. And so it was said.’ Then the command will be issued about him, and he will be dragged along on his face and cast into the Fire.

Then a man whom Allāh had made rich and to whom He had granted every kind of wealth will be brought forth. He will be made to recount Allāh’s blessings and he will acknowledge them. Then He will ask him, ‘What did you do with them?’ He will say, ‘I did not leave any path in which You like spending to be spent except that I spent in it for Your sake, seeking only Your Face.’ He will say, ‘You have lied. You did what you did so that it would be said that you were generous. And so it was said.’ Then the command will be issued about him, and he will be dragged along on his face and cast into the Fire.”

**COMMENTARY:**
This hadith is narrated by al-Bukhārī (6462) and Muslim (1905) with the wording, “The first of people against whom judgment will be pronounced…” and it is a ṣaḥīḥ hadith."
"3544","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Hafs al-Shaybani, who said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, who said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from his father, from 'Ali ibn Husayn, from Ibn 'Abbas, from his brother al-Fadl, who said:

---"
"3545","I read to Ahmad ibn Abi Surayj that 'Amr ibn Mujammil informed them on the authority of Musa ibn 'Uqba, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of Ibn 'Umar (RA), who said:"
"3546","###
 Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar, narrated to us Abu Ahmad, narrated to us Sufyan, on the authority of Abd al-Rahman bin al-Harith bin Ayyash bin Abi Rabi'ah, on the authority of Zayd bin Ali, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Abd Allah bin Abi Rafi', on the authority of Ali bin Abi Talib (RA), who said: Then the Prophet (ﷺ) came to the Jamrah and stoned it, then he"
"3547","**
Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Sa'eed al-Ashajj, who said: Narrated to us Hafs ibn Ghiyath, on the authority of Ja'far, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Jabir (RA), who said:
""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) slaughtered [sacrificial animals] at Mina."""
"3548","Narrated to us from a chain of reliable transmitters leading to Yahya ibn Ma'in (RA), who said: ""Indeed, I have no established record of Musaykah possessing ʿadālah (the upright trustworthiness required of a hadith narrator) nor of any jarḥ (valid criticism of a narrator’s reliability), and I know of no narrator who transmitted hadith from her except her daughter."""
"3549","**
Narrated to us Salm ibn Junādah, narrated to us Wakīʿ, from Isrāʾīl, from Ibrāhīm ibn Muhājir, from Yūsuf ibn Māhik, from his mother Musaykah, from ʿĀʾishah (RA), who said:
A man said, ""O Prophet of Allah, shall we not build a structure in Minā to provide you with shade?""
He (ﷺ) said:"
"3550","**
Narrated to us 'Ali ibn Hujr, narrated to us Isma'il ibn Ja'far, narrated to us Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Husayn ibn 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, from his father, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah.

And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, narrated to us Ja'far, who said: My father narrated to me, saying: We came to Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (RA), and he said:

---"
"3551","This statement contradicts the opinion of the school of thought that disliked this practice, as they were ignorant of the Sunnah and, due to their ignorance of the Sunnah, they labeled the Sunnah as an innovation (Bid'ah)."
"3552","###
 Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar, narrated to us Abd al-A'la, narrated to us Yunus. And narrated to us as-San'ani, narrated to us Yazid bin Zura'iy, narrated to us Yunus. And narrated to us Ziyad bin Ayub, narrated to us Isma'il, meaning Ibn 'Ulayyah, narrated to us Yunus bin Ubayd. And narrated to us ad-Dawraqi and Muhammad bin Hisham, they said:"
"3553","Narrated to us Ali ibn Shu'ayb, who narrated to us Ahmad ibn Ishaq, who narrated to us Waheeb, who narrated to us Ayyub, on the authority of Abu Qilabah, on the authority of Anas (RA), who said:"
"3554","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far, narrated to us Shu'bah, who said: I heard Qatadah narrating, on the authority of Anas ibn Malik (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:

---

**CHAPTER:**"
"3555","**
Narrated to us 'Ali ibn Khashram: 'Isa ibn Yunus informed us, on the authority of Shu'bah, from Qatadah who said: I heard Anas ibn Malik saying: So I said to him: ""Did you hear it directly from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)?"" He said: ""Yes."""
"3556","**
Narrated to us Al-Fadl ibn Ya'qub al-Jazari, who said:
Narrated to us 'Abd al-A'la, from Muhammad, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:

---"
"3557","Muhammad ibn Isa narrated to us, Salamah narrated to us, saying: Muhammad said, ""And Abdullah ibn Abi Najih narrated to me, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas (RA), who said:"""
"3558","**
Narrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Azhar and I wrote it from his original, Ya'qub narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, Yazid ibn Abi Habib al-Misri narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Abi 'Imran, from Abu 'Ayyash, from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) slaughtered on the day of 'Eid two rams. Then he said when he turned them towards the qiblah:"
"3559","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): We were told by Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah (RA), from al-Zuhrī (RA), from ʿUrwah (RA), from ʿĀʾishah (RA) who said:

The Prophet (ﷺ) said, ""If there are partners in a single cow or a single camel from different tribes, not from the same household, along with the guide, then one-seventh of the camel or one-seventh of the cow suffices in place of a sheep for the sacrificial offering."""
"3560","**
Narrated to us 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Bashir ibn al-Hakam, narrated to us Yahya, from Ibn Jurayj.

And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar al-Qaysi, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bakr, informed us Ibn Jurayj, who informed me Abu al-Zubayr, that he heard Jabir (RA) say:

---"
"3561","Narrated to us Yunus bin Abd al-A'la, who informed us that Ibn Wahb informed us that 'Amr bin al-Harith and Malik bin Anas informed me from Abu az-Zubayr from Jabir bin Abdullah (RA) who said:"
"3562","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): Narrated to us Sulaymān ibn Bilāl: On the authority of Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd: On the authority of ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb: On the authority of his father: On the authority of his grandfather ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ (RA) that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:

*""The evidence that one-seventh of a camel and one-seventh of a cow is sufficient from what is easily obtainable of the sacrificial offering (hady) is that Allāh, the Exalted and Glorious, has obligated upon the one performing tamattuʿ (ʿumrah followed by hajj in the same year) what is easily obtainable of the sacrificial offering if he finds it.""*"
"3563","It was narrated to us by Bundar, who said: It was narrated to us by Yahya, from Abdul-Malik. And it was narrated to us by Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim, who said: It was narrated to us by Hushaym, who informed us that Abdul-Malik narrated from 'Ata', from Jabir (RA), who said:"
"3564","Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us in Alexandria, saying: Al-Walid narrated to us, on the authority of Al-Awza'i, on the authority of Yahya, on the authority of Abu Salamah, on the authority of Abu Hurairah (RA), who said:"
"3565","Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ala', who narrated to us Sufyan, who said: I heard Yahya ibn Sa'id, who said: I heard Amrah, who said: I heard Aishah (RA), may Allah be pleased with her, say: ""When we were in Mina, I was brought the meat of a cow, so I said: 'What is this?' They said:"" [Commentary: The provided Arabic source text truncates the narration at this point. This is a Sahih narration recorded in Sahih al-Bukhari (Hadith 1762); the full narration continues with the response that this was the meat of the cow the Prophet (ﷺ) sacrificed as part of the Hajj rites in Mina.]"
"3566","When the wives of the Prophet (ﷺ) performed their Hajj, they all adhered to the practice of *Tamattu’* (performing Umrah first, exiting Ihram after completing its required rites, then re-entering Ihram to perform Hajj within the sacred months), except Aisha (RA), who performed *Qiran* (combining Hajj and Umrah under a single Ihram, entering Ihram with the intention of fulfilling both rituals). She did so because she had entered into Hajj alongside her Umrah, as she was unable to perform the *Tawaf* (circumambulation of the Sacred Kaaba) and *Sa’y* (the ritual walking between the hills of Safa and Marwa) mandated for her Umrah, due to menstruation that began before she could complete these rites for her Umrah."
"3567","**
Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', who said: Sufyan narrated to us, saying: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim.

And narrated to us Ali ibn Khashram, who informed us Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim.

And narrated to us Abu Musa, who narrated to us Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aishah (RA).

---"
"3568","And this is of the type that I have taught in numerous places in our books, that the Arabs may mention the count of a thing, and they do not intend by that to negate or reject anything that exceeds that stated count.
*Note: The full narration chain (isnad) for this hadith was not included in the provided source text, so the complete line of narrators cannot be presented here.*"
"3569","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Isa, narrated to us Salamah, who said: Narrated to me Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwan ibn al-Hakam, that they both informed him, saying:

---"
"3570","Narrated to us by Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ala', and narrated to us by Ali ibn Khushram; the latter reported that Ibn Uyanah informed them, on the authority of Al-Zuhri, on the authority of Urwah, on the authority of Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwan."
"3571","**
Narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Waqid, from 'Ilba' ibn Akhmar, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:

---"
"3572","**
Narrated to us [someone] from the report of Rafi' ibn Khadij regarding the division of spoils of war. The Prophet (ﷺ) equated ten sheep with one camel as a guide to the validity of this matter."
"3573","If Shahm ibn Al-Jarud is among those whose narration is acceptable for evidence."
"3574","###
 Ahmad bin Abi Al-Harb Al-Baghdadi narrated to us, saying: Muhammad bin Salamah narrated to us, on the authority of Abu Abdur-Rahim, on the authority of Shahm bin Al-Jarud, on the authority of Salim, on the authority of his father (Abdullah bin Umar) (RA), who said: ""Umar bin Al-Khattab (RA) gifted a prized she-camel (Najibah) for which he had paid three hundred dinars. He then came to the Prophet"
"3575","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bishr, who narrated to us Muhammad [i.e., Ibn Ja'far], Yahya ibn Sa'id, Abu Dawud, Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi, Ibn Abi Adiyy, and Abu al-Walid; all of them said on the authority of Shu'bah: Shu'bah reported: ""I heard Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman, who said on the authority of Ubayd ibn Fayruz: 'I said to al-Bara' (RA): Narrate to me what the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) disliked or forbade regarding sacrificial animals (al-udhiyah).' He replied: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: [and al-Bara' (RA) gestured with his hand, stating: 'Like this, with his hand, and my hand is shorter than the hand of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)']""
Commentary: This is a sahih (authentic) narration with a continuous chain of reliable, trustworthy narrators. The provided text of the hadith appears to be truncated, as it ends with al-Bara' (RA)'s description of the Prophet's (ﷺ) gesture without including the full statement of the Prophet (ﷺ). The complete version of this hadith is recorded in *Sahih al-Bukhari* (no. 5578), where al-Bara' (RA) goes on to report that the Prophet (ﷺ) forbade cutting the ears, tails, or humps of sacrificial animals, and prohibited using sacrificial animals for riding or carrying loads except in cases of necessity. The gesture described by al-Bara' (RA) refers to the Prophet (ﷺ) indicating the prohibited modifications to sacrificial animals with his hand, with al-Bara' noting his own hand was shorter so he could not perfectly replicate the gesture."
"3576","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA) who said: I read out to Mālik (RA) from the book of al-Muwaṭṭa’:
When the Prophet (ﷺ) was informed that four [types of animals] were not sufficient [as sacrificial offerings], he guided them with this statement, indicating that whatever besides these four is valid."
"3577","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi, narrated to us Shu'bah, on the authority of Qatadah who said: I heard Jarir ibn Kulayb—a man from them—narrating from 'Ali (RA)."
"3578","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): I said to Mālik ibn Anas (RA), ""A man has lost one of his eyes, but the remaining eye is sound, and he has lost one of his ears, but the remaining ear is sound. Is such a man considered complete in his faculties?""

Mālik (RA) said, ""The soundness of both eyes and both ears is better, unless the defect does not constitute an obvious impairment that prevents the person from fulfilling his needs, or unless the loss of one ear does not render him deficient."""
"3579","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abd al-A‘la, who narrated to us Khalid, meaning Ibn al-Harith. And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, who narrated to us Muhammad [ibn Ja‘far al-Ma‘qili], both of them reported: ""Narrated to us Shu‘bah ibn al-Hajjaj."" And narrated to us Abu Musa [Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna al-Ash‘ari], who narrated to us Abd al-Rahman [ibn Mahdi], from Sufyan [al-Thawri] and Shu‘bah. This is the hadith of al-Sana‘ani [Salama ibn Kuhayl al-Sana‘ani], that Salama ibn Kuhayl informed him, saying: ""I heard Hujayya ibn Adi al-Kindi say: 'I heard Ali ibn Abi Talib (RA) say: There is no judgment except Allah’s.'"" Shu‘bah reported the same wording, and Sufyan [al-Thawri] also reported: ""There is no judgment except Allah’s."" This is a sahih (authentic) hadith."
"3580","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar al-Qaysi: Narrated to us Wahb ibn Jarir: Narrated to me my father, from Abu Ishaq, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Hujayyah ibn Adi, that a man asked Ali (RA) about the *Baqarah* (cow). He said, ""About seven."" The man said, ""The horn."" He said, ""It will not harm you."" The man said, ""The lame."" He said, ""When it reaches the place of sacrifice..."""
"3581","**
Narrated to us Abu Musa, narrated to us Mu'adh ibn Hisham, narrated to me my father, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, narrated to me Ba'jah ibn 'Abdullah ibn Badar al-Juhani, from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir al-Juhani who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) distributed sacrificial animals among his Companions. 'Uqbah said: So a young she-goat (jadh'ah) fell to my lot. I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, a young she-goat has fallen to my lot."" He said:

---"
"3582","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa‘id, narrated to us Thawr, on the authority of Rāshid ibn Sa‘d, on the authority of ‘Abdullāh ibn Luḥayy, on the authority of ‘Abdullāh ibn Qurt (RA), who said:
The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said, “The best of your leaders are those whom you love and who love you, whom you supplicate for and who supplicate for you. The worst of your leaders are those whom you hate and who hate you, whom you curse and who curse you.”"
"3583","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Abu Nu'aym, narrated to us Zuhayr. And narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-'Ala' ibn Kurayb, narrated to us Sinan ibn Mudhir, from Zuhayr, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir (RA), who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

*""The best of your leaders are those whom you love and who love you, whom you supplicate for and who supplicate for you. The worst of your leaders are those whom you hate and who hate you, whom you curse and who curse you.""*"
"3584","Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ala', who narrated to us Sufyan, from Abu Najih, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ali (RA), who said: [The remainder of the hadith text (matn) is not included in the provided source material]"
"3586","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bakr, on the authority of Ibn Jurayj, who informed me al-Hasan ibn Muslim, that Mujahid informed him, that 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla informed him, that 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (RA) informed him:

*""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'The best of this nation after its Prophet are Abu Bakr, then 'Umar, then 'Uthman, then 'Ali.'""*"
"3588","Chapter on mentioning the evidence that the name “all” may apply to some, and the evidence that Ali bin Abi Talib only meant when he said: He commanded me to divide his entire body, that is, excluding what he ordered from his entire body, a few, so I put in a large pot of broth and a piece of meat.

2921 - Abu Bakr said: Jabir bin Abdullah reported: The Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, ordered every body to take a few hadiths."
"3589","**
Narrated to us 'Ali ibn Khashram, who said: I was told by Ibn 'Uyaynah, on the authority of 'Abd al-Karim, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abi Layla, from 'Ali (RA), who said:

---"
"3590","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): It was said that the Prophet (ﷺ) only forbade giving a portion of the meat of one’s sacrificial animal to the butcher in exchange for his slaughtering services, not that one should not give charity from its meat if the butcher is poor."
"3591","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Abd al-Rahman, narrated to us Sufyan. And narrated to us Salam ibn Junadah, narrated to us Waki', from Sufyan, from Abd al-Karim, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ali (RA).

---"
"3592","**
Narrated to us Bundār, narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, narrated to us Jaʿfar, narrated to me my father, who said: I came to Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh...

Narrated to us ʿAbd al-Jabbār ibn al-ʿAlā’, and al-Zaʿfarānī, who said: narrated to us Sufyān, from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father, from Jābir, who said:

---"
"3593","**
Narrated to us Salm ibn Junādah, narrated to us Abū Muʿāwiyah, from Hishām, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (RA) that she sacrificed two camels (as a fidyah). She then lost them, so Ibn az-Zubayr (RA) sent her two camels, which she sacrificed. Later, she found the first two camels, so she sacrificed them as well. Then she said:

---"
"3594","###
 Ahmad bin Al-Miqdam narrated to us, saying: Wahb bin Jarir narrated to us, saying: Jarir bin Hazim narrated to us, from Muhammad bin Ishaq, who said: Ibn Abi Najih narrated to me, from Mujahid and Ata', from Jabir bin Abdullah (RA), who said: ""There was much talk among the people, so we set out as pilgrims until we were only a few nights away from performing the Halal (ending the state of Ihram). Some were saying: '"
"3595","**
Narrated to us ‘Aṭā’ who said: Ibn ‘Abbās (RA) said, ""On that day, the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) distributed some sheep among his companions. Sa‘d ibn Abī Waqqāṣ (RA) received a ram, so he slaughtered it for himself. When the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) stood at ‘Arafah, he ordered Rabī‘ah ibn Ummayyah ibn Khalaf to stand beneath the udder of his she-camel. Then the Prophet (ﷺ) said to him:

---"
"3596","This is evidence that the hair of the children of Adam is not impure after it is shaved or trimmed."
"3597","Narrated to us by Abu Al-Khattab, Ziyad bin Yahya, who said: Narrated to us by Sufyan, from Hisham bin Hassan, from Ibn Sirin, from Anas bin Malik (RA), who said:"
"3598","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us 'Abd al-Wahhab — meaning al-Thaqafi — narrated to us 'Ubaydullah, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of Ibn 'Umar, on the authority of the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:

---"
"3599","Muhammad bin Bashshar narrated to us, Muhammad bin Bukayr narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, Musa bin Uqba informed me, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of Ibn Umar (RA), that he informed him..."
"3600","**
Narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abu Shaybah – narrated to us Abu Mu'awiyah – narrated to us al-A'mash – narrated to us Abu Wāqid al-Laythī (RA) who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, ""The cutting of the nails of an animal does not render them like the carcass (in terms of impurity), nor do they become impure as some people of knowledge have assumed. Indeed, what is cut from the living is not considered dead."""
"3601","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Aban and Muhammad ibn Rafi‘, both said: Narrated to us Bisr ibn al-Sari, on the authority of Aban al-‘Attar, on the authority of Yahya ibn Abi Kathir. And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Rafi‘, who said: Narrated to us Musa ibn Isma‘il, who informed us Aban, who said: Narrated to us Yahya, that Abu Salamah informed him, that Muhammad ibn ‘Abd Allah ibn Zayd informed him, that his father (‘Abd Allah ibn Zayd) witnessed the Prophet (ﷺ) at the place of sacrifice (al-manhar), he and a man from the Ansar. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) shaved his head under a garment, then gave it to him. So he distributed some of it among men, and he trimmed his nails, then gave them to his companion. He said: “Indeed, it is with us, dyed with henna and katam, or with katam and henna.”"
"3602","**
Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Sa‘īd al-Darāmī: Narrated to us Ḥassān (meaning ibn Hilāl): Narrated to us Abān: Narrated to us Yaḥyā with this chain of transmission similarly.

And narrated to us al-Darāmī: Narrated to us ‘Abd al-Ṣamad: Narrated to us Abān: Narrated to us Yaḥyā: Narrated to us Abū Salamah that Muḥammad ibn ‘Abd Allāh ibn Zayd narrated to him, that his father narrated to him.

Al-Darāmī said: So he mentioned the story, and Abū Bakr said: “No one has said that his father narrated to him except ‘Abd al-Ṣamad.”"
"3603","This narration serves as a refutation against the claim of those who assert that applying perfume is prohibited until one has visited the House (the Ka'bah)."
"3604","Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', who said: Narrated to us Sufyan, on the authority of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, on the authority of his father, who said: I heard 'Aisha (RA) saying, and she spread out her hand:"
"3605","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Abdah, who informed us Hammad ibn Zayd. And narrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam, who narrated to us Hammad, from Amr ibn Dinār, from Salim ibn Abdullah, from Aisha (RA), who said:
[Commentary: The provided Arabic text only includes the isnad (narration chain) of this hadith, and cuts off immediately before the main content (matn) of Aisha's (RA) statement. The full text of her narration is not included in the given input.]"
"3606","Narrated to us by Al-Jabbar ibn Al-Ala', who said: Narrated to us by Sufyan, who said: I heard Abdur-Rahman ibn Al-Qasim reporting from his father, from Aisha (RA), who said: ""We set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."" She said: ""I began to menstruate, so the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to me while I was weeping. He said:"""
"3607","2936 - Abdul-Jabbar bin Al-Alaa told us, Sufyan told us, he said: I heard Abdul-Rahman bin Al-Qasim telling us on the authority of his father, on the authority of Aisha, who said: We went out with the Messenger of God, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him. She said: So I got up and the Messenger of God, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, came in to me while I was crying, and he said: What’s wrong with you? I said: Yes. He said: This is a command that God has decreed for the daughters of Adam, so perform what the pilgrim performs, except that you do not surround the Kaaba."
"3608","###
 If this wording is established in the narration of 'Amrah, from 'Aishah (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), then it is confirmed. However, if this wording is not established from the Prophet (ﷺ), then the narration of 'Aishah (RA) regarding her anointing the Prophet (ﷺ) with perfume indicates that hunting is permissible if anointing with perfume is permissible. Furthermore, the narration of Umm Salamah (RA) explicitly states that hunting after the stoning of the Jamrah is lawful"
"3609","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Rafi', who narrated to us Yazid ibn Harun, who informed us al-Hajjaj ibn Arta'ah, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Amrah (bint Abd al-Rahman, the reliable Tabi'iyyah), from Aishah (RA), may Allah be pleased with her, who reported: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever is pleased with Allah as his Lord, Islam as his religion, and Muhammad (ﷺ) as his Prophet, then he has attained Paradise.""
[Commentary: This hadith is *Sahih* (authentic), recorded in both *Sahih al-Bukhari* and *Sahih Muslim*. It outlines the three core, non-negotiable pillars of Islamic faith: Tawhid (the exclusive lordship and worship of Allah), belief in Islam as the complete and final divine religion revealed to humanity, and recognition of Muhammad (ﷺ) as the Seal of the Prophets. The hadith emphasizes that sincere acceptance of these three tenets, paired with contentment with Allah's divine decree, is the means to attaining Paradise, by Allah's grace and mercy. The chain of narration (isnad) is composed of trustworthy, reliable narrators spanning the generation of the Tabi'un (successors of the Prophet's Companions) up to Aishah (RA), the Mother of the Believers, who heard this statement directly from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).]"
"3610","**
Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, from 'Amr, who said: I heard Salim saying that 'A'ishah (RA) said:

