---"
"3639","###
 Narrated to us Al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who said: Ibn Lahi'ah and Malik ibn Anas informed me, from Ibn Al-Had, from Abu Murrah, the freed slave of Aqil ibn Abi Talib, who said: ""I entered with Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Al-As upon his father during the days of Tashriq (the days of Eid al-Adha). He was having his midday meal, so he"
"3640","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): I said to Mālik (RA), ""What do you say about the resident of Makkah and the one who resides there temporarily, should they shorten the prayer?""

Mālik (RA) said, ""The resident of Makkah does not shorten the prayer, nor does the one who resides there temporarily. The prayer for the one who is not from the people of Makkah and the one who has not taken up permanent residence in Makkah is to be completed (i.e., not shortened).""

Some of the people of knowledge made a mistake in their argumentation regarding this matter, claiming that the Sunnah of the prayer in Minā is two rakʿahs for both the people of the horizons (i.e., travelers) and the people of Makkah, just like the prayer of a traveler."
"3641","**
Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Sa'id al-Ashajj, narrated to us Ibn Numayr. And narrated to us Ali ibn Khashram, informed us Isa. And narrated to us Salm ibn Junadah, narrated to us Abu Mu'awiyah. And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Abdur-Rahman, narrated to us Sufyan. And narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Abu Mu'awiyah and Jarir, all of them from al-A'mash, except that in the hadith of ath-Thawri, from Sulayman—he is al-A'mash—from Ibrahim, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Yazid, who said:

Uthman prayed four rak'ahs at Mina. So Abdullah (ibn Mas'ud) said:"
"3642","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Al-Ala, who said: Narrated to us Abu Khalid, from Ubaydullah bin Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (RA), who said:"
"3643","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Hanbal: Ahmad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, from Muhammad ibn Al-Munkadir, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aisha (RA), the Mother of the Believers, who said: When the Prophet (ﷺ) was a resident of Medina, and he had come to Mecca solely for the purpose of performing Hajj, he did not stay in Mecca for a duration that would make it obligatory for him to offer the complete (non-shortened) prayer."
"3644","2964 - Ibn Abbas reported: God imposed four rak’ahs on the tongue of your Prophet in the city and two rak’ahs when traveling, so he declared that enjoining four rak’ahs of prayer in Mina on a resident, and it is the same as on someone other than him, which is the same.
Abu Bakr said: Yahya bin Ishaq reported on the authority of Anas that the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, continued to pray two rak’ahs until he returned."
"3645","2965 - Aisha’s report: Prayer was first made obligatory as two rak’ahs, then it was added to the home prayer, declaring that the person present in Mina who has to complete the prayer does not have the right to shorten the prayer if he is present and not travelling.
Abu Bakr said: I had explained in the Book of Prayer the meaning of the news of Yahya bin Abi Ishaq on the authority of Anas
And in the reports of Ibn Abbas and Aisha there is clear evidence that what is required of the people of Mecca and whoever resides there from other than its residents is that he must complete the prayer in Mina since he is a resident and not a traveler, because the resident’s obligation is four, so it is not permissible for anyone other than a traveler or one who is not afraid of fighting to shorten the prayer, and the people of Mecca and whoever has resided in it without a permanent residence must complete the prayer if they go out to Mina intending to return to Mecca and are not travelling, so it is not permissible for them to shorten the prayer at Mina."
"3646","Muhammad bin Bashshar narrated to us, saying: Yahya narrated to us, saying: Thawr narrated to us, on the authority of Rashid bin Sa'd, on the authority of Abdullah bin Luhay', on the authority of Abdullah bin Qurt, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:"
"3647","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Sa'id al-Darrami, who narrated to us Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq, who narrated to us Abu Hamzah, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas (RA), who said: [The full text of Ibn Abbas' statement (the matn of the hadith) is not included in the provided source excerpt]"
"3648","**
