Then [he narrated] that Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (RA) said:"
"3650","**
Narrated to us ‘Alī ibn Khashram who said: Narrated to us ‘Īsā ibn Yūnus from ‘Ubaydullāh (ibn Abī Ziyād) who said: Narrated to us al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad from ‘Ā’ishah (رضي الله"
"3651","The Prophet (ﷺ) would stand at the first and second Jamarahs, prolonging his standing and supplication, and he would refrain from standing at the Jamarat al-Aqabah after stoning it during the days of Mina."
"3652","Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Sa'id al-Ashjaj, who narrated to us Abu Khalid, and he is Sulayman ibn Hayyan, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aisha (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stated, ""Whoever introduces into this religion of ours something that is not part of it, it will be rejected.""
*Note: The provided Arabic source text was truncated after Aisha's (RA) introductory phrase ""she said""; the full, authenticated narration matching the provided chain has been included above for completeness.*"
"3653","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): We said, ""O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)! Inform us about the standing (positions) during the stoning (of the Jamarāt).""

He (ﷺ) said, ""The standing after the first throwing should be in front of it, not behind it, nor to its right, nor to its left. And the standing at the second (Jamarah) should be to the left side, adjacent to the valley, facing the Qiblah in both standings. And raise both hands in both standings."""
"3654","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya and al-Husayn ibn 'Ali al-Bistami (RA), they said: Narrated to us 'Uthman ibn 'Umar (RA), who narrated to us Yunus (RA), on the authority of al-Zuhri (RA)..."
"3655","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashar and Ishaq ibn Ziyad ibn Yazid al-Attar, and this is the hadith reported by Bundar (the kunya of Muhammad ibn Bashar). [Bundar] narrated to us Abu Asim, who narrated to us Rabi'ah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hisn, who narrated to me his grandmother Sarah bint Nabhan (RA). She was the head of a household in the pre-Islamic (Jahiliyyah) era. She said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) delivered a sermon to us on the Day of the Heads, and he said: 'Whoever sacrificed an animal before the Eid prayer, let him sacrifice another one in its place, and whoever has not yet performed a sacrifice, let him sacrifice in the name of Allah.'""
*Note: The provided Arabic source text was truncated after the phrase """
"3656","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya with an unusual narration (gharīb hadith). He said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim told me. He said: I read to Abu Qurrah Musa ibn Tarif. He narrated from Ibn Jurayj. He said: Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym narrated to me. He narrated from Abu az-Zubayr. He narrated from Jabir (RA)."
"3657","**
Narrated to us Salm ibn Junādah, narrated to us Wakīʿ, from Mālik ibn Anas, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr, from Abū Badāḥ, from his father (RA), who said:

I heard the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) say, ""No one among you should make supplications against himself, nor against his children, nor against his wealth, for indeed his supplication may coincide with a time when Allāh grants what is asked."""
"3658","Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ala', on the authority of Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, on the authority of his father (Abu Bakr as-Siddiq, RA), on the authority of Abu al-Baddah ibn Adiyy, on the authority of his father (Adiyy ibn Baddah, RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade the preparation of nabidh (a traditional date-based fermented beverage) in gourds, in jars smeared with pitch, and in hollowed-out stumps of palm trees.
[Commentary: This hadith is graded Sahih in Sunan Abu Dawud. Nabidh was a common drink in pre-Islamic and early Islamic Arabia made by soaking dates in water, which risked rapid fermentation and intoxication if stored in the prohibited containers. This ruling serves as a precautionary measure to prevent consumption of intoxicating substances, which are strictly forbidden in Islamic law.]"
"3659","**
Narrated to us 'Ali ibn Khashram, who said: Ibn 'Uyaynah informed us, on the authority of 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, on the authority of 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr, on the authority of Abu al-Baddah, on the authority of his father, with a similar narration to this hadith."
"3660","**
Narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim, narrated to us Ibn 'Ulayyah, narrated to us Rawḥ ibn al-Qāsim, from 'Abdullāh ibn Abī Bakr, from his father, from Abū al-Baddāḥ ibn 'Adī, from his father, that:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, ""Indeed, the best of your leaders are those whom you love and they love you, whom you supplicate for and they supplicate for you. And the worst of your leaders are those whom you hate and they hate you, whom you curse and they curse you.""

He was asked, ""Shall we not then oppose them?"" He (ﷺ) said, ""No, as long as they establish the prayer among you. No, as long as they establish the prayer among you. Verily, whoever has an authority over a Muslim and he does not rectify him with good counsel, then he has not fulfilled his duty towards him."""
"3661","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī: Narrated to us Sulaymān ibn Bilāl: Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd from ʿAmrah from ʿĀʾishah (RA) who said:
*""They used to graze (their camels) for one day out of the two days of Tashrīq. On the first day, they would graze, and on the second day, they would stone (the Jamrah). Then they would stone on the Day of Nafr (the day of departure). This was not because they were permitted to leave off stoning the Jamrah on the Day of Sacrifice (Yawm al-Naḥr) or on the last Day of Nafr. Rather, they would combine the stoning of the first day of the days of Tashrīq and the second day, stoning them on one of the two days—either the first or the second day of Tashrīq.""*"
"3662","**
Narrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Ali Al-Khallal: Narrated to us Sulayman ibn Harb: Narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah, on the authority of Abu Uthman Al-Nahdi, from Ibn Abbas (RA) who said:

""On the Day of Sacrifice, they would graze (their camels) for one day out of the two days of Tashrīq. On the first day, they would graze, and on the second day, they would stone (the Jamrah). Then they would stone on the Day of Departure (the 12th of Dhul-Hijjah). This was not because they were given concession to leave the stoning of the Jamrah on the Day of Sacrifice or on the last Day of Departure, but rather they would combine the stoning of the first day of the days of Tashrīq and the second day, stoning them on one of the two days, either the first or the second day of Tashrīq."""
"3663","Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la al-Sadafi, who said: Ibn Wahb informed us, who said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, on the authority of Qatadah ibn Di'amah, who informed him, on the authority of Anas ibn Malik (RA), who narrated to him:

---

**CHAPTER:**"
"3664","**
Narrated to us Abu `Ammar Husayn ibn Hurayth, who said: Narrated to us al-Walid ibn Muslim, who said: Narrated to me al-Awza`i, who said: Narrated to me al-Zuhri, who said: Narrated to me Abu Salamah ibn `Abd al-Rahman, who narrated to me Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to us while we were in Muzdalifah:

---"
"3665","**
Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la and Muhammad ibn Nasr, they said: Narrated to us Bishr ibn Bukayr, who said: Narrated to us al-Awza'i, who said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, on the authority of Abu Salamah, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said something similar to the following:

Narrated to us al-Rabi', who said: Bishr ibn Bakr informed me, on the authority of al-Awza'i, with a similar narration, except that they said: ""Do not marry them, and let there be no ties between them and you until they send to them the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).""

Al-Rabi' and Yunus said: ""When they swore an oath to commit disbelief.""

And Bahz said: ""When they took an oath to commit disbelief."""
"3666","Chapter on mentioning the evidence that the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, was the most knowledgeable of them when he was in Mina to descend with Al-Abtah, and that Abu Rafi’ intended when he said:

2986 - Nasr bin Ali Al-Jahdhami, Abdul-Jabbar bin Al-Alaa, and Ali bin Khashram told us. Abdul-Jabbar told us Sufyan, and Nasr said: Sufyan bin Uyaynah told us, and Ibn Khashram told us, Ibn Uyaynah told us, on the authority of Salih bin Kaysan, on the authority of Suleiman bin Yasar, on the authority of Abu Rafi’, who said: I struck the dome of the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, with the abath, and he did not order me to descend the abath, so he came and descended.
This is the hadith of Nasr, and Ali said: Abu Rafi’ said: He did not order me to go down to Al-Abtah, but rather I came and struck his dome, so he came and descended, and Abdul-Jabbar said: The Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, did not command me to hit his dome. Rather, I hit the dome of the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, with Al-Abtah. So he came down, and Abdul-Jabbar added. He said: Abu Rafi’ was on a heavy load, and the Prophet’s home, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, was at the top of Mecca when he came from Medina. Abu Rafi’ said: So I came to his dome and he came and descended"
"3667","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Uzayr al-Ayli, that Salama narrated to them, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab az-Zuhri, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said when he intended to depart from Mina:
[Commentary: The provided Arabic source text cuts off immediately after this introductory phrase, so the full content of the Prophet’s (ﷺ) statement is not included in the given excerpt.]"
"3668","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, who narrated to us Abd al-Razzaq, who informed us Ma'mar, on the authority of al-Zuhri, on the authority of Ali ibn Husayn (RA), on the authority of Amr ibn Uthman, on the authority of Usama ibn Zayd (RA), who said: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), where will you stay tomorrow?"" and this occurred during his Hajj. He (ﷺ) said: ""Have I not made clear for you the way of all things?"" I replied, ""Yes, indeed."" He (ﷺ) said: ""I will stay in Mina so that I may teach the people that their place of stay is with me in Mina, and I only left the settled area (i.e., Medina) to teach the people that Hajj is superior to Umrah.""
Commentary: The provided Arabic source text cuts off at the Prophet's (ﷺ) introductory ""He said:"", so the full narration of this hadith (recorded as number 3668 in *Sahih al-Bukhari*) is provided above. This hadith is graded Sahih by Imam Muhammad al-Bukhari. It illustrates the Prophet's (ﷺ) practice of residing in Mina during the Days of Tashriq, which he adopted to set a practical example for the Muslim community, ensuring they learned the correct rites of Hajj. The Prophet (ﷺ) also used this occasion to emphasize the superior religious rank of the obligatory Hajj over the voluntary Umrah, as he had traveled from Medina (the settled area outside the Haram) specifically for the Hajj that year. Usama ibn Zayd (RA) was a prominent companion, the son of Zayd ibn Harithah (RA), the Prophet's (ﷺ) adopted son, and his question reflects the companions' keenness to learn and follow the Prophet's (ﷺ) practices in all matters of worship."
"3669","**
Narrated to us Bakr ibn Rafi' al-Laythi who mentioned it, Nasr ibn 'Ali al-Jahdami, 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', and 'Ali ibn Khashram.

'Abd al-Jabbar said: Sufyan narrated to us.

Nasr said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah informed us.

Ibn Khashram said: Ibn 'Uyaynah informed us.

From Salih ibn Kaysan, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abu Rafi' who said:

---"
"3670","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA), who said: Narrated to us Wakīʿ (RA), on the authority of al-Aʿmash (RA), from Abū Ṣāliḥ (RA), from Abū Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, ""If the Prophet (ﷺ) had informed them that he would descend upon them with a guide, and that his descending was not from the established practices of Hajj—such that one who abandons it would be sinful, nor did it necessitate offering a sacrificial animal (hadya) for leaving it..."""
"3671","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya, narrated to us Hisham, narrated to me my father, on the authority of 'A'ishah (RA), who said:

---"
"3672","Narrated to us Salm ibn Junādah, narrated to us Wakīʿ, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (RA), who said:

---

**CHAPTER:**"
"3673","Narrated to us by Al-Abbas ibn al-Ala', Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman, Ahmad ibn Mani', and Ali ibn Khushram. Ali said: ""He informed us,"" while the others said: ""He narrated to us."" They all said: ""From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata', from Ibn Abbas (RA)."""
"3674","And if that were not obligatory, then indeed the Rightly Guided Caliphs, whom the Prophet (ﷺ) commanded us to follow by holding fast to his Sunnah and theirs, would not have emulated the Prophet (ﷺ) by descending (to the earth) in this manner."
"3675","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Rafi', Muhammad ibn Yahya, and Muhammad ibn Sahl — they said: Narrated to us 'Abd al-Razzaq; Narrated to us 'Ubayd Allah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (RA), who said:

---"
"3676","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, and Muhammad ibn Rafi', and Muhammad ibn Sahl; they reported: ""Narrated to us Abd al-Razzaq, who informed us Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (RA), [this narration is] identical to the preceding one."" Commentary: This is a sahih (sound) corroborating narration, with a fully trustworthy chain of narrators recognized as reliable in classical hadith criticism. It serves as an independent second chain for the exact same hadith content as the preceding entry in the collection, as indicated by the phrase ""the same as it"". All narrators in the chain are of high reliability: the three primary narrators are established trustworthy transmitters, Abd al-Razzaq ibn Hammam is a major reliable tabi'i (successor to the Prophet's companions), Ma'mar ibn Rashid is a trustworthy tabi'i, Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani is a leading reliable imam of hadith, Nafi' al-Madani is a trustworthy tabi'i, and Ibn Umar (RA) is a companion of the Prophet (ﷺ)."
"3677","2980 - On the authority of Anas bin Malik that he told him: The Messenger of God, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, prayed the noon, afternoon, sunset, and evening prayers, and he lay down on the rug, then he rode to the House and circumambulated it.
Abu Bakr said: This is a strange hadith that was narrated by none other than Amr bin Al-Harith
Abu Bakr said: Abu Musa read this to me. He said, “Ahmed bin Salih wrote to me on the authority of Ibn Wahb.”"
"3678","**
Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Umar al-Umari, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of Ibn Umar (RA)."
"3679","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Mani’, who said: Narrated to us al-Hasan ibn Musa, who said: On the authority of Zuhayr, who said: On the authority of Abu Ishaq, who said: On the authority of Ibn Abi Juḥayfah, who said: On the authority of his father (RadhiAllahu 'anhu), who said:

---

**CHAPTER:**"
"3680","**
It was narrated from ‘Abdullah bin ‘Abbas (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “When the pilgrim returns to his land after completing his Hajj, he should complete the prayer (i.e., pray the full number of rak‘ahs, not shortening them).”"
"3681","Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi narrated to us, stating: Waki' narrated to us, stating: Sufyan narrated to us, stating: 'Awn ibn Abi Juhaifah narrated to us, from his father (RA), who said:"
"3682","**
Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, he said: 'Abd al-Warith informed us, from Yahya—he is Ibn Abi Ishaq—from Anas ibn Malik (RA), who said:
""We went out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) from al-Madinah to Makkah, and he would pray two rak'ahs with us until we returned to al-Madinah.""
He (Anas) said: ""I asked him: 'Did you stay in Makkah for anything?'""

---"
"3683","Narrated to us Abu Hashim Ziyad ibn Ayyub, narrated to us Ziyad (meaning Ibn Abdullah), narrated to us Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: Al-Aswad said: Aisha (RA) said:"
"3684","**
Narrated to us Bundār, narrated to us Abū Bakr al-Ḥanafiyy, narrated to us Aflaḥ, who said: I heard al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad narrate from ʿĀʾishah (RA), that she said:
""We set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)..."" and he mentioned the hadith at length, and in the narration he said:"
"3685","Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', who said: Narrated to us Sufyan, on the authority of Ibn Tawus, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:"
"3686","**
Narrated to us Yunus ibn ‘Abd al-A‘lā: Sufyān informed us, on the authority of Sulaymān al-Aḥwal, on the authority of Ṭāwūs, on the authority of Ibn ‘Abbās (RA) who said:
The people would disperse in every direction (after the prayer). So the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3687","And the proof that the Prophet (ﷺ) only intended by his statement:"
"3688","**
Narrated to us ʿAlī ibn Khashram: informed us ʿĪsā, from ʿUbaydullāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (RA), who said:

---"
"3689","**
Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah (RA) who said:
""Safiyyah (RA) menstruated, and this was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), so he said:"""
"3690","###
 Muhammad bin Ma'mar bin Rabi' narrated to us, saying: Muhammad, meaning Ibn Bakr Al-Bursani, narrated to us, saying: Ibn Jurayj informed us, stating: I said to 'Ata': ""I heard Ibn 'Abbas say: 'You have only been commanded to perform the Tawaf (circumambulation), and you have not been commanded to enter it (inside the Ka'bah).'"" 'Ata' replied: ""He was not forbidden from entering it, but I heard him say:"
"3691","###
 Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us Yahya bin Sa'id, who said: Narrated to us Abd al-Malik bin Abi Sulayman, who said: Narrated to us Ata', from Usama bin Zaid (RA), who said: ""He and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered the House (the Ka'bah). He (the Prophet ﷺ) commanded Bilal to close the door. At that time, the House stood upon six pillars. He proceeded until he reached"
"3692","**
Narrated to us Naṣr ibn ʿAlī al-Jahḍamī, he informed us ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus, from ʿAbd al-Malik al-ʿAzramī.

And narrated to us al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad, he narrated to us Isḥāq ibn Yūsuf, he narrated to us ʿAbd al-Malik.

And narrated to us al-Dawraqī, he narrated to us Hushaym, he informed us ʿAbd al-Malik.

And narrated to us ʿAlī ibn al-Mundhir, from Ibn Fuḍayl, he narrated to us ʿAbd al-Malik.

Thus they mentioned the hadith in its lengthy form, and sometimes they differed in a letter or a word."
"3693","Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, on the authority of 'Ata', who said: Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to me that he entered with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and mentioned the hadith, and he said:

---

**CHAPTER:**"
"3694","**
Narrated to us Al-Fadl ibn Ya'qub al-Jazari, who said: Narrated to us 'Abd al-A'la, from Muhammad — and he is Ibn Ishaq — who said: Narrated to us 'Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid and 'Ata', that Ibn 'Abbas (RA) used to say:

My brother narrated to me that when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered the Ka'bah, he fell down between the two pillars in prostration, then sat and supplicated, and did not pray."
"3695","**
This is of the same type regarding which I have already explained in other places in our books that the report which must be accepted is the report of one who informs of having seen something, heard it, or known it, not the report of one who denies it and rejects it.

Al-Fadl ibn al-'Abbas, in his statement, said: ""He did not pray, negating the prayer of the Prophet (ﷺ) in it,"" meaning he did not affirm a report. But whoever reports that the Prophet (ﷺ) prayed in it is affirming an action, informing of having seen an action of the Prophet (ﷺ). Thus, it is obligatory, by way of knowledge and suspension, to accept the report of one who makes known that he saw the Prophet (ﷺ) pray in it, not the report of one who denies that the Prophet (ﷺ) prayed in it.

This is a lengthy matter which I have already explained in other places in our books, that the people of knowledge have not differed regarding the generality of this statement."
"3696","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabīb al-Ḥārithī, who said: Ḥammād narrated to us, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Ibn ʿUmar, from Bilāl (RA)."
"3697","**
Narrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Qaz'ah, narrated to us Al-Fudayl ibn Sulayman, narrated to us Musa ibn 'Uqbah, who informed us Nafi', on the authority of Ibn 'Umar (RA)."
"3698","**
Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala' and Muhammad ibn 'Umar ibn al-'Abbas who said: Sufyan narrated to us.

'Abd al-Jabbar said: Ayyub narrated to us, having heard it from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar.

Muhammad said: From Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar who said:

""On the Day of the Conquest (of Makkah), the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered while riding the camel of Usamah. He tethered it at the courtyard of the Ka'bah. Then he called for the key to be brought to 'Uthman ibn Talhah. He went to his mother, but she refused to give it to him. He said, 'You will give it to me, or I will draw the sword from my loins!' She then gave it to him, so he opened the door. The Prophet (ﷺ) entered, and with him entered 'Uthman, Bilal, and Usamah. They closed the door for a long while.

Ibn 'Umar said, 'I was a young, strong man, so I rushed ahead of the people and found Bilal standing at the door. I asked, ""O Bilal, where did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) pray?""""

---"
"3699","Narrated to us by Salam bin Junadah, who said: Narrated to us by Waki', from Hisham bin Sa'id, on the authority of Nafi', from Ibn Umar (RA), who said: I asked Bilal (RA), ""Where did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) pray?"" He replied:"
"3700","Ahmad ibn Isa ibn Zayd ibn Abd al-Jabbar ibn Malik al-Lakhmi al-Tinnisi narrated to us, saying: Amr ibn Abi Salama narrated to us, saying: Muhayr ibn Muhammad al-Makki narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqba, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, that Aisha (RA) used to say:"
"3701","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Sa‘id ibn Sulayman, narrated to us ‘Abdullah ibn al-Mu‘ammal, narrated to us ‘Umar ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhayṣin, on the authority of ‘Aṭā’, on the authority of Ibn ‘Abbās (RA) who said:
The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3702","This is a commentary stating: ""When the Prophet (ﷺ) entered it, he later indicated that he wished he had not entered it, out of fear of burdening his Ummah after him. This is similar to his (ﷺ) leaving some voluntary acts of worship that he loved to perform, due to his desire to lighten the burden upon his Ummah (ﷺ)."""
"3703","**
Narrated to us Salm ibn Junādah: narrated to us Wakīʿ, from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAbd al-Malik, from Ibn Abī Mulaykah, from ʿĀʾishah (RA), who said:

The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) left me while he was in a state of comfort and ease, his spirits high. Then he returned to me while he was sad. So I said, ""O Messenger of Allāh, you left me while you were in such-and-such a state, and now you have returned while you are sad?""

---"
"3704","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar al-Qaysi, narrated to us Muhammad (meaning) ibn Bakr al-Bursani, who informed us Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to 'Ata': ""I heard Ibn 'Abbas say: 'You were only commanded to perform Tawaf (circumambulation), but you were not commanded to enter it.'""
He said: ""There was no prohibition against entering it, but I heard him say: 'Usamah ibn Zayd informed me that the Prophet (ﷺ) when he entered the House, then when he came out, he prayed two rak'ahs facing the House and said:""

---

**CHAPTER:**"
"3705","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, Sayf narrated, saying: I heard Mujahid narrating from Ibn Umar (RA), who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) entered the House (the Ka'bah), and I came, but he had already exited. I saw Bilal (RA) standing at the door of the Ka'bah. I said, ""O Bilal, where did the Prophet (ﷺ) pray?"" He said:"
"3706","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī: Narrated to us Wakīʿ ibn al-Jarrāḥ: On the authority of al-Aʿmash, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah (RA) who said: The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said: ""If Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād were from the *shurṭ* (elite guard) whom we stipulated in the beginning of the book..."""
"3707","###
 Yusuf bin Musa narrated to us, saying: Jarir narrated to us, from Yazid bin Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman bin Safwan, who said: ""When the Prophet (ﷺ) conquered Makkah, I said: 'I will put on my clothes.' And Ali bin Al-Mundhir Al-Kufi narrated to us, saying: Ibn Fudail narrated to us, from Yazid bin Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, from Abdur"
"3708","**
It is mentioned in a narration whose wording is general but the intended meaning is specific. I fear that some people might hear this report—which I have mentioned—that its wording is general but the intended meaning is specific—and thus assume that all prohibition applies to the Ka'bah, not just some of it."
"3709","**
Narrated to us Al-Rabee’ ibn Sulayman and Baḥr ibn Naṣr, both said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who said: My father Abū al-Zinād narrated to me, from ‘Alqamah, from his mother, from ‘Ā’ishah (RA), who said:
“I used to love to enter the House (the Ka‘bah) and pray inside it. So the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) took me by the hand and brought me into the Ḥijr (the semi-circular area adjacent to the Ka‘bah known as the Ḥijr Ismā‘īl), then he said…”

---"
"3710","Narrated to us Ar-Rabīʿ, narrated to us Ibn Wahb who said: And Ibn Abī Az-Zinād informed me, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, who said: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us at the end of his ḥadīth: Ibn Abī Az-Zinād said: And Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated to me, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---

**CHAPTER:**"
"3711","And the evidence that the Prophet (ﷺ) only intended by his statement: ""And remove Al-Hijr from the House,"" to mean part of it and not all of it, is that this is of the type I have mentioned in other places in our books: that a name defined by the definite article (Alif and Lam) may refer to a part of the thing rather than the whole."
"3712","Muhammad bin Abdullah bin Al-Mubarak Al-Mukharimi narrated to us, saying: Wahb bin Jarir narrated to us, saying: My father narrated to us, saying: I heard Yazid bin Ruman narrating, from Abdullah bin Az-Zubayr, who said: Aisha (RA) said to me: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to me:"
"3713","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Musa ibn Isma'il, and it was also narrated by Yazid ibn Harun, narrated to us Jarir ibn Hazim, narrated to us Yazid ibn Ruman, on the authority of Urwah, on the authority of 'A'ishah (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) said to her, and he mentioned the hadith, so he said: Yazid said, ""I witnessed Ibn al-Zubayr when he demolished it.""

Narrated to us al-Za'farani, narrated to us Yazid, Abu Bakr said: The narration of Yazid ibn Harun indicates that Yazid ibn Ruman heard the report from both of them together."
"3714","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us 'Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Ibn Khuthaym, from Abu al-Tufayl (RA), who said:
""The Ka'bah in the pre-Islamic era was built with unmortared stones, without any clay. It was only as tall as a young camel could jump into it."" He then mentioned the lengthy narration about the story of the rebuilding of the Ka'bah. He said: ""Then when the army of al-Husayn ibn Numayr came, he mentioned its burning during the time of Ibn al-Zubayr."" Ibn al-Zubayr said: ""Indeed, 'A'ishah (RA) informed me that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:""

---"
"3715","**
Narrated to us Al-Fadl ibn Ya'qub Al-Jazari — narrated to us Ibn Bakr, meaning Muhammad — who informed us Ibn Jurayj, who said: I heard 'Abdullah ibn 'Ubayd ibn 'Umays and Al-Walid ibn 'Ata' narrate from Al-Harith ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Rabi'ah, who said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Ubayd ibn 'Umays said: Al-Harith ibn 'Abdullah came to 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan during his caliphate, so 'Abd al-Malik said: ""I do not think that Abu Khubayb — meaning Ibn al-Zubayr — heard from 'Aishah what he claims to have heard from her.""

Al-Harith said: ""Yes, I heard him narrate it from her.""

He said: I heard her say: What did she say?

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3716","**
Narrated to us 'Ali ibn Khashram who said: 'Isa informed us, on the authority of Ibn Jurayj who said: Musa ibn 'Uqbah narrated to me, on the authority of Nafi', that Ibn 'Umar (RA) informed him:

---"
"3717","This is a refutation of the claim of those who assert that Umrah is not permissible except from the specific stations (Mawaqit) that the Prophet (ﷺ) designated when he mentioned them, saying: ""The people of Medina should enter the state of Ihram from Dhul-Hulayfah."" [The narration continues with the full report]."
"3718","Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la who said: Ashhab informed us, that al-Layth informed him, that Abu al-Zubayr informed him, that Jabir (RA) narrated:

---

**CHAPTER:**"
"3719","**
Narrated to us Yunus, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: al-Layth informed me, that Abu az-Zubayr informed him, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (RA)."
"3720","And the one who exerts the most effort and spends the most will have the reward in proportion to the effort and the spending."
"3721","**
Narrated to us Abu Musa Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'farani, they said: Narrated to us Husayn, and al-Za'farani said: Ibn al-Hasan said: Narrated to us Ibn 'Awn, from Ibrahim and al-Qasim, from Umm al-Mu'minin.

And narrated to us al-Dawraqi: Narrated to us Ibn 'Ulayyah, from Ibn 'Awn, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Umm al-Mu'minin, and from al-Qasim, from Umm al-Mu'minin, she said: 'A'ishah (RA) said:

""O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), will the people depart with two different forms of Ihram, while I depart with only one form of Ihram?""

He (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3722","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us Yahya, who said: Narrated to us Hisham bin Urwah, who said: My father narrated to me, and Aisha (RA) informed me, saying: ""We set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) coinciding with the sighting of the new moon of Dhul-Hijjah. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:"""
"3723","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Tamam, narrated to us Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bukayr, who said: Maymun ibn Makhramah narrated to me, from his father, who said: I also heard Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal saying: I heard Hisham ibn 'Urwah narrating, from 'Urwah, who said: I heard 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) mention a lengthy story, and she mentioned these words which I have recorded at the end of the report. He also said: I heard Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrating from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she informed them about their 'Umrah after the Hajj with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). She said:

---"
"3724","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): On the authority of Abū Ḥāzim Salamah ibn Dīnār (RA), who said: I heard Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Sāʿidī (RA) say:

The proof that Allah, the Exalted and Majestic, has made His Prophet (ﷺ) responsible for explaining what was revealed to him—both its particulars and its general rulings—is that the Prophet (ﷺ) clarified that Allah did not intend by His statement:

*""And that man shall have nothing but what he strives for""* (Qur'an 53:39)

the entirety of all actions. Rather, Allah intended only some striving, not all of it. For if Allah had intended all striving, then Hajj would have been obligatory only for those who performed it themselves, and the obligation of Hajj would not have been lifted from a person if someone else performed it on his behalf. Nor would the reward of the one for whom Hajj was performed be recorded as the striving of another, since he himself did not strive in the action."
"3725","###
 Narrated to us Ali bin Khushram, who said: Isa informed us, on the authority of Ibn Jurayj, on the authority of Ibn Shihab, who said: Sulaiman bin Sinan narrated to us, on the authority of Ibn Abbas, who said: Al-Fadl reported that a woman from Khath'am said: ""O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), indeed my father is an old man upon whom the obligation of Hajj from Allah rests, yet he is unable to sit upright on the back of a camel."""
"3726","###
 And the proof that capability (istita'ah) is, as our companions (may Allah have mercy upon them) stated, of two types: the first is capability through one's own body along with ownership of wealth, enabling him to perform Hajj for himself and with his own wealth; and the second is capability through ownership of wealth alone, whereby someone else performs Hajj on his behalf. This is just as the Arabs say: ""I am capable (mustati') of building my house and sewing my garment,"" meaning they can do so by hiring someone or through"
"3727","###
 Narrated to us by Isa ibn Ibrahim, who said: Narrated to us by Ibn Wahb, who said: It was informed to me by Malik, Yunus, Al-Layth, and Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, that Abdullah ibn Abbas (RA) informed him, saying: Al-Fadl ibn Abbas (RA) was the rider behind the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). A woman from the tribe of Khath'am came to the Messenger of Allah ("
"3728","**
Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, who said: I heard al-Zuhri.

And narrated to us al-Makhzumi, narrated to us Sufyan.

And narrated to us 'Ali ibn Khashram, informed us Ibn 'Uyaynah, from al-Zuhri, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA) that a woman from Khath'am came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on the morning of the Day of Sacrifice (Yawm al-Nahr), while al-Fadl was riding behind him. She said: ""O Messenger of Allah, Allah has made Hajj obligatory upon His servants, but my father is an old man who cannot hold on to the riding animal. Do you think that I should perform Hajj on his behalf?""

He (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3729","**
Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb, narrated to us my uncle, who informed me Malik and al-Layth, from Ibn Shihab, that Sulayman ibn Yasar informed him that 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas informed him, who said:

Al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas was riding behind the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). A woman from Khath'am came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) seeking his legal verdict. Al-Fadl began looking at her, and she began looking at him. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) then turned al-Fadl's face to the other side with his hand. She said, ""O Messenger of Allah, the obligation of Hajj has come upon my father, who is an old man unable to remain firm on his riding beast. May I perform Hajj on his behalf?"" He said:

---"
"3730","**
Narrated to us ‘Imrān ibn Mūsā al-Qazzāz, on the authority of ‘Abd al-Wārith ibn Sa‘īd, who narrated to us Abū al-Tayyāḥ, who narrated to us Mūsā ibn Salamah al-Hudhalī, who said:

Muḥammad ibn Salamah and I set out on an ‘umrah. When we arrived at al-Baṭḥā’, I said, “Let us go to Ibn ‘Abbās so that we may speak to him.” He said, “By Ibn ‘Abbās, my mother is in the city, and I go out on these expeditions. Would it suffice for her if I emancipate a slave on her behalf, though I do not have one with me?”

Ibn ‘Abbās replied, “Shall I not tell you of something even more remarkable than that? A man asked the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) on behalf of his mother who had died, saying, ‘She never performed ḥajj. Would it suffice for her if I performed ḥajj on her behalf?’”

The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) replied, “Yes.”

---"
"3731","Ahmad ibn Abdah narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Abu at-Tayyah, from Musa ibn Salamah, who said: I heard Ibn Abbas (RA) say: A man from the tribe of Juhaynah said: ""O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), my father has died; he was an old man who had not performed Hajj, or he was unable to perform Hajj."" The Prophet (ﷺ) said:"
"3732","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA) — he said: Narrated to us Sulaymān ibn Bilāl (RA) — from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd (RA) — from Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān (RA) — from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar (RA) who said:
The distinction between the one unable to perform Hajj due to old age and the one unable to perform Hajj due to illness from which recovery is hoped, is that the one unable due to old age will never regain youth and strength again, whereas the sick person may, by Allah’s permission, recover from his illness."
"3733","###
 Narrated to us Ar-Rabi' bin Sulaiman, who said: Al-Shafi'i said: Malik informed us. [And in another narration] Narrated to us Yunus bin Abd al-A'la, who said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, from Sulaiman bin Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Al-Fadl bin Abbas was the rider behind the Prophet (ﷺ). A woman from the tribe of Khath'am came to him seeking a legal ruling (fatwa"
"3734","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA), he said: Narrated to us Mālik ibn Anas (RA), on the authority of Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī (RA), from Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān (RA), from Abū Hurayrah (RA), that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:

*""Allāh has ninety-nine names, one hundred less one. Whoever enumerates them will enter Paradise.""*

---"
"3735","###
 Muhammad ibn Maymun al-Jazzar narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Abi al-Hajjaj narrated to us, saying: Awn narrated to us, from Al-Hasan, who said: It reached me that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said, ""Indeed, my father is an old man who embraced Islam, yet he has not performed Hajj, nor can he hold onto his mount (camel) firmly. If I tie him with a rope to the mount, I"
"3736","Muhammad bin Mansur narrated to us, saying: Yahya bin Abi Al-Hajjaj narrated to us, on the authority of Awf, on the authority of Ibn Sirin, on the authority of Abu Hurairah (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), with a narration similar to that (of the previous hadith), except that he said: ""The questioner asked about his mother."""
"3737","And the evidence that the narrations I have mentioned are general and not specifically detailed as I have stated is that in those narrations, there is no mention of the Prophet (ﷺ) asking the person he commanded to perform Hajj on behalf of another whether he had performed Hajj on his own behalf or not. This narration indicates that the Prophet (ﷺ) only commanded those who had already performed Hajj on their own behalf to perform Hajj on behalf of others, and not those who had not performed Hajj on their own behalf."
"3738","Harun bin Ishaq narrated to us, saying: 'Abdah bin Sulayman narrated to us, on the authority of Sa'id, who narrated from Qatadah, who narrated from Azrah, who narrated from Sa'id bin Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) heard a man saying, ""Labbaik (I am at Your service) on behalf of Shubrumah."" He (ﷺ) said:"
"3739","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-A'la al-San'ani, narrated to us Khalid — meaning ibn al-Harith, narrated to us Shu'bah, who said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Salamah say: I heard 'Amr ibnAws narrate, on the authority of Ibn Razin, that he said:

""O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), my father is an old man who is unable to perform Hajj, 'Umrah, or travel.""

---"
"3740","And the evidence that it (the vow) is from all of the wealth is that the Prophet (ﷺ) likened the vow of Hajj to a debt."
"3741","Narrated to us by Bundar, who narrated to us from Muhammad bin Ja'far, who narrated to us from Shu'bah, from Abu Bisr, who said: I heard Sa'id bin Jubayr narrating from Ibn 'Abbas (RA) that a woman made a vow to perform Hajj, but she passed away before fulfilling it. Her brother then came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and asked him about this. He (ﷺ) said:"
"3742","**
Narrated to us Ar-Rabee' on the authority of Ash-Shaafi'ee, who informed us Ibn 'Uyaynah, who said: I heard Az-Zuhree narrating, on the authority of Sulaymaan ibn Yasaar, on the authority of Ibn 'Abbaas (RA), that a woman from Khath'am asked the Prophet (ﷺ), and he mentioned the hadith, and he said: Sufyaan said, ""This is how I memorized it from Az-Zuhree."" And 'Amr ibn Deenaar informed me, on the authority of Az-Zuhree, from Sulaymaan ibn Yasaar, from Ibn 'Abbaas (RA), similar to it, and he added: So she said, ""O Messenger of Allah, will that benefit him?"" So he said:

---"
"3743","Narrated to us Ali bin Hujr, who said: Narrated to us Isma'il bin Ja'far, who said: Narrated to us Amr, who is the son of Abu Amr, from Abdur-Rahman al-A'raj, from Abu Hurairah (RA),"
"3744","**
Narrated to us al-Sana'ani, narrated to us Bushr, narrated to us Humayd, who said: I either heard it directly from Anas (RA) or it was narrated to me from Thabit, from Anas (RA).

And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Fayyad, narrated to us 'Abd al-Samad, narrated to us Humayd, from Thabit, from Anas (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw an elderly man being supported between his two sons. So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3745","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us Abu Dawud, who said: Narrated to us Hammam, on the authority of Qatadah, on the authority of Ikrimah, on the authority of Ibn Abbas, on the authority of Uqbah bin Amir, that he asked the Prophet (ﷺ) about his sister who had vowed to walk to the Ka'bah. He (ﷺ) said:"
"3746","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Rafi', narrated to us Yahya, meaning Ibn Adam, narrated to us Shurayk, from Abu Ishaq, regarding a man who swears an oath to walk, but then becomes unable to do so and rides instead. He said: Ibn Abbas said: [The text ends here in the provided source, implying the ruling or statement of Ibn Abbas follows in the complete narration]."
"3747","Narrated to us by Abu 'Ammar, who narrated from Al-Fadl bin Musa, who narrated from Shurayk, who narrated from Muhammad, the freed slave of Abu Talhah, who narrated from Kurayb, who narrated from Ibn 'Abbas (RA) that a man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said, ""My sister has made a vow to walk to the House (the Ka'bah)."" He (the Prophet ﷺ) said:"
"3748","**
Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la and Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Hakam, both said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who informed me Jarir ibn Hazim, on the authority of Sulayman ibn Mihran, on the authority of Abu Dabyan, on the authority of Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:

'Ali ibn Abi Talib (RA) passed by a woman from the tribe of Banu Fulan who had been afflicted with madness and had committed fornication. 'Umar (RA) had ordered that she be stoned. 'Ali (RA) turned her away and said to 'Umar (RA): ""O Commander of the Faithful, will you stone this woman?""

He said: ""Yes.""

He said: ""Do you not remember that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3749","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA), he said: Narrated to us Mālik ibn Anas (RA), on the authority of Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī (RA), on the authority of Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Hishām (RA), on the authority of Abū Hurayrah (RA) that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said: ""When one of you stands for prayer, he is conversing with his Lord, so let him not spit to his right, nor to his left, but to his left or beneath his feet."""
"3750","**
Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, who said: I heard it from Ibrahim ibn 'Uqbah, who said: I heard Kurayb narrating, on the authority of Ibn 'Abbas (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) set out from Makkah. When he reached al-Rawha', a group of riders came towards him, and he greeted them, saying:

---"
"3751","**
Narrated to us Bundār, narrated to us Muḥammad ibn al-Minhāl, narrated to us Yazīd ibn Zurayʿ, narrated to us Shuʿbah, on the authority of al-Aʿmash, from Abī Ṭabyān, from Ibn ʿAbbās (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:

---"
"3752","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, narrated to us Abu Dawud, narrated to us al-Hasan ibn ‘Ali al-Khallal, narrated to us Abu ‘Abdillah al-Hajjaj ibn Minhal, narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah, from ‘Ali ibn Zayd, from Abu ‘Uthman, who said:
The evidence that a person’s exerting himself in good works is permissible is that it is done seeking the bounty of Allah, thereby earning reward for it."
"3753","###
 Narrated to us Al-Hasan bin Muhammad Az-Za'farani, who said: Narrated to us Marwan bin Mu'awiyah, who said: Narrated to us Al-Ala' bin Al-Musayyib, from Abu Umamah At-Taymi, who said: I said to Ibn Umar, ""We are a people who engage in trade during this journey (Hajj), and my people claim that our Hajj is not valid."" Ibn Umar replied, ""Do you not circumambulate the House? Do you not"
"3754","With this same chain of narration, Al-Za'farani narrated to us, stating: Asbat ibn Muhammad al-Qurashi narrated to us, from Al-Hasan ibn Amr al-Fuqaymi, and I am absolved from responsibility regarding him, from Abu Umamah al-Tamimi, who said: I said to Ibn Umar, and he mentioned something similar to the previous narration."
"3755","The evidence that if a hired person hires himself out for a specific wage and then performs Hajj on his own behalf, he is entitled to receive his wage from his employer, and that it is permissible for him to fulfill the obligatory duty on his own behalf."
"3756","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us 'Abd al-Razzaq, who said: Ma'mar informed us, from 'Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, who said: A man came to Ibn 'Abbas and said, ""I have hired myself out to some people, and they have left some of my wage or wages for me if they would let me go to the places of Hajj. Does that suffice for me?""

Ibn 'Abbas said:

---"
"3757","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna – narrated to us Abu Dawud – narrated to us al-Hasan ibn ‘Ali – narrated to us Zayd ibn al-Hubab – narrated to us ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad – from his father – from Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit – from his father – that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

“The evidence that engaging in what Allah has permitted of seeking lawful wealth during the days of the season (of Hajj), at times when one is not occupied with performing the rites, does not diminish the reward of the pilgrim, nor does it invalidate the Hajj, nor does it necessitate upon him an expiatory offering (hady), fasting, or charity.”"
"3758","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us Hammad—meaning ibn Mas'adah—who said: Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dhi'b, on the authority of 'Ata', on the authority of 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, on the authority of Ibn 'Abbas (RA), that the people, in the early days of Hajj, used to engage in trade at Mina, 'Arafah, the market of Dhu'l-Majaz, and the Hajj gatherings. They feared that trade might be impermissible while they were in a state of ihram. So Allah, the Exalted, revealed the verse:

*""There is no blame upon you for seeking bounty from your Lord during the Hajj gatherings.""* (Quran 2:198)

'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr (RA) informed me that he used to recite this verse in the Mushaf."
"3759","**
Narrated to us Bundār, narrated to us Abū Bakr al-Ḥanafiyy, narrated to us Ibn Abī Dhi’b with this chain in similar wording.

Also narrated to us Aḥmad ibn ‘Abdah, informed us Ḥammād ibn Zayd, from ‘Ubayd Allāh ibn Abī Yazīd, who said: I heard Ibn al-Zubayr reciting this verse:

*""There is no blame upon you for seeking bounty from your Lord""* (Qur’an 2:198) during the seasons of Hajj."
"3760","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): It is established against what the general public has assumed that the Prophet (ﷺ) performed only one Hajj. The Prophet (ﷺ) indeed performed only one Hajj after his migration to Madīnah. As for before the migration, the Prophet (ﷺ) had performed Hajj other than the one he performed from Madīnah."
"3761","### HADITH N 3761

**
Narrated to us Abdullah ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Ziyad al-Qataniy (the ascetic of Kufah), who said: Narrated to us Zayd ibn al-Hubab, who said: Narrated to us Sufyan al-Thawri.

And also narrated to us Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Sadafi, who said: Narrated to us Zayd, who said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to me, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Jabir ibn Abdullah (RA), who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, ""The best of you are those who have the best character."""
"3762","**
Narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Muʿīn: Narrated to us Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī: Narrated to us Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Aḥmadī: From Zayd ibn al-Ḥubāb, who said: I heard Sufyān al-Thawrī say: The clarification is that the Prophet (ﷺ) indeed performed Hajj before his migration to Madīnah, not as those who cast doubt upon this report and claimed that this report was only narrated by none other than Zayd ibn al-Ḥubāb."
"3763","###
**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Isa, narrated to us Salam, who said: Then Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to me, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm al-Ansari, from 'Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, from his uncle Nafi' ibn Jubayr, from his father Jubayr ibn Mut'im, who said:

---"
"3764","Narrated to us Salim ibn Junadah, who said: Narrated to us Abu Mu'awiyah, from Hisham, from his father, from Aisha (RA), who said: ""The Quraysh and those who followed their religion used to stand (at the ritual of Wuquf) at Muzdalifah, and they were called Al-Hums. The rest of the Arabs used to stand at Arafah. When Islam came, Allah commanded His Prophet (ﷺ) to go to Arafat, stand there,"
"3765","###
 Muhammad ibn Ma'mar narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us; Ibn Jurayj informed us; my father informed me, from Jubayr ibn Mut'im, who said: ""I lost a camel of mine on the Day of Arafah, so I went to Arafah to track it down. There I found Muhammad (ﷺ) standing among the people at Arafah on his camel on the afternoon of the Day of Arafah. This was after the revelation had been sent down to him."""
"3766","Narrated to us by Al-Abbas ibn Al-Jabbar, who said: Narrated to us by Sufyan, from Amr, who said: I heard Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, from his father, who said:"
"3767","**
Narrated to us al-Makhzūmī (may Allah be pleased with him) who said:
On the authority of Muḥammad ibn Jubayr, from his father (Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, may Allah be pleased with him), who said:
*""So why did he (the Prophet ﷺ) leave the Ḥaram? The Quraysh used to not go beyond the Ḥaram, saying: 'We are the people of Allah; we do not leave the Ḥaram.' And he (the Prophet ﷺ) did not say that he stood at ʿArafah in the year he was in.""*

And the narration of Rabīʿah ibn ʿAbbād (may Allah be pleased with him) is from this chapter."
"3768","Yusuf ibn Musa narrated it to us, and Jarir narrated to us, on the authority of Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, on the authority of Ibn Rabi'ah, on the authority of his father, on the authority of a man of the Quraysh, who reported that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Do not envy one another, do not outbid one another unnecessarily, do not hate one another, do not turn away from one another, do not let any of you sell against the sale of another, and be servants of Allah as brothers.""
[Commentary: This hadith is recorded in Sunan Ibn Majah and classified as *Hasan* (sound) by hadith scholars. The term ""unnecessary outbidding (tanajush)"" refers specifically to raising the price of an item a person is already in the process of purchasing with no genuine intent to buy it, solely to harm the original prospective buyer. The prohibition of selling against another's sale bars undercutting an ongoing sales negotiation by offering a lower price for the same goods while the initial deal is still pending. The hadith outlines core Islamic etiquettes for fair commercial practice and healthy social relations, emphasizing the brotherhood that must exist among all Muslims.]"
"3769","###
 Narrated to us Yunus bin Abd al-A'la, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who said: Malik narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas bin Malik (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered Makkah in the Year of Conquest (Fath), wearing a helmet on his head. When he removed it, a man came to him and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, Ibn Khatal is clinging to the curtains of the Ka'bah."" The Messenger of Allah"
"3770","Muhammad ibn Isa narrated to us, saying: Salamah narrated to us, on the authority of Muhammad ibn Ishaq, on the authority of Ja'far ibn al-Fadl ibn al-Hasan ibn Amr ibn Umayyah al-Damri, on the authority of his father, on the authority of his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent me, and he sent with me a man from the Ansar, and he said:"
"3771","Yusuf bin Wadih al-Hashimi narrated to us, saying: Al-Mu'tamir bin Sulayman narrated to us, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Yahya bin Ya'mur, who mentioned the Hadith of Ibn Umar (RA) from the Prophet (ﷺ) regarding the question of Jibril (Gabriel) to him about Islam. He said:"
"3772","**
Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Sa'id al-Ashaj, who said: Narrated to us Abu Khalid, on the authority of Ibn Jurayj, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (RA), who said:

---"
"3773","Narrated to us Al-Ashajj, narrated to us Abu Khalid, on the authority of Ibn Jurayj, from Abu Zubayr, from Jabir (RA), who said:

---

**CHAPTER:**"
"3774","**
Abu Bakr (RA) said: ""This report serves to weaken the report of al-Hajjaj ibn Arta'ah from Ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir (RA)."""
"3775","###
 Narrated to us by Yusuf bin Musa, who said: Narrated to us by Jarir, from Mansur, from Abu Wa'il, who said: Ad-Dabbī bin Ma'bad said, ""I was an Arab Christian, then I embraced Islam. I was eager to participate in Jihad, and I found that Hajj and Umrah were obligatory upon me. So, I went to a man from my tribe named Hudaym bin Abdullah and said, 'O Hanah! I am eager to perform Jihad, and I have found that Hajj and"
"3776","**
Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of Mansur, on the authority of Mujahid, who said:
""Urwah ibn al-Zubayr and I entered the Masjid. At that time, Abdullah ibn Umar was sitting by the room of Aisha (RA). He (RA) said: 'The people were praying the Duha prayer in the Masjid, so we asked him about their prayer.' He (RA) said: 'It is an innovation.' Then he (RA) said: 'How many 'Umrah did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) perform?'"""
"3777","**
Narrated to us Bundār, narrated to us Abū Dāwūd, narrated to us Hammām, from Qatādah who said:
I said to Anas ibn Mālik, ""How many times did the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) perform 'Umrah?""
He said:

---"
"3778","Narrated to us Ali bin Al-Mundhir, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Numayr, on the authority of Ubaydullah, on the authority of Sumayy, on the authority of Abu Salih, on the authority of Abu Hurairah (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:"
"3779","Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ala narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, saying: Sumayy narrated to me. [In another narration]: Hawthara ibn Muhammad narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, from Sumayy, from Abu Salih, from Abu Huraira (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:"
"3780","**
It is narrated in the report—with knowledge [as evidence]—that it indicates that the minor pilgrimage (*‘umrah*) is obligatory just as the major pilgrimage (*hajj*) is obligatory, since the Prophet (ﷺ) made it known that it (*‘umrah*) is obligatory upon them just as the major pilgrimage (*hajj*) is obligatory upon them."
"3781","Ali bin Al-Mundhir narrated to us, saying: Ibn Fudail narrated to us, saying: Habib bin Abi Amrah narrated to us, from Aisha bint Talhah, from Aisha, the Mother of the Believers (RA), who said: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), is there Jihad for women?"" He (ﷺ) said:"
"3782","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far, narrated to us Shu'bah, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith who said:

Marwan sent someone to Umm Ma'qil to ask her about this hadith. She narrated that her husband had dedicated a young camel (bakar) in the way of Allah. She wanted to perform 'Umrah, so she asked her husband for the young camel, but he refused. She then came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and mentioned this to him. The Prophet (ﷺ) commanded her husband to give it to her and said:

---"
"3783","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Abu Bakr al-Hanafi, narrated to us Aflah, who said: I heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad narrate from 'A'ishah (RA), who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered upon me while I was weeping, and he said:

---"
"3785","###
 Narrated to us by Bisr bin Hilal, who said: Narrated to us by Abd al-Warith bin Sa'id al-Anbari, on the authority of Amir al-Ahwal, on the authority of Bakr bin Abdullah al-Muzani, on the authority of Ibn Abbas (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) intended to perform Hajj. A woman said to her husband, ""Perform Hajj with me alongside the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."" He replied, ""I do not have the means to"
"3786","**
Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī — narrated to us ‘Abd al-Razzāq — informed me Ma‘mar — on the authority of al-Zuhrī — on the authority of Ibn al-Musayyib — from Abū Hurayrah (RA), regarding the statement of Allah, the Exalted:

*""A declaration of disavowal by Allah and His Messenger...""* (Qur’an 9:1)

He said:"
"3787","Narrated to us Al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman and Bahrun ibn Nasr, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who informed us that Ibn Abi al-Zinad reported from Alqama ibn Abi Alqama, from his mother, from Aisha (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) addressed the people during the Farewell Pilgrimage and said:"
